Cumulative cumulative rapid number of coronavirus (COVID-19) in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, from 1 January to 15 March 2020
The cumulative number of COVID-19 shows similar trends in the countries of the European Union/Euradean Economic Area and the United Kingdom, confirming that although the stage varies based on the country, the COVID-19 pandemic is rapidly worsening in all countries.
Based on Italy’s experience, countries, hospitals and intensive care units should increase their readiness to increase the number of COVID-19 patients who will need health care, particularly intensive care.
On December 31, 2019, a series of cases of pneumonia of unknown pathogens were reported in Wuhan City, Hubey Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention reported that the causative factor, the novel coronavirus, is now referred to as Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, the disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 infection has been named Coronavirus (COVID-19).
The evidence so far is that almost 80 percent of individuals with COVID-19 have a mild illness, i.e
At approximately 14%, the incidence of COVID-19 has worsened into a more severe illness that requires hospitalization, while the remaining 6% has a serious illness that requires intensive care.
The mortality rate of patients hospitalized due to COVID-19 was about 4%.
In this study, we assess trends in the cumulative number of COVID-19 in each country in the European Union/European Economic Area (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom (UK) and compare them with those from Hubei Province, China.
We also compare the current number of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom with the number in Italy between 31 January and 15 March 2020.
Covid-19 cases in the European Union/Euronese and the United Kingdom
After what happened in China, COVID-19 has increasingly spread geographically and the dynamics of the COVID-19 pandemic in the rest of the world are currently tracking the dynamic of this country.
On 11 March 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
In the Eurosurvilence issue issued on March 5, 2020, Spitiri and others reported the first confirmed European COVID-19 cases according to the World Health Organization (WHO) definition of the case.
In the European Union/EEA, the first three confirmed cases were reported by France on 24 January 2020 for people returning from Wuhan, Hubey Province, China.
As of March 15, 2020, COVID-19 cases have been detected in all 30 EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom (UK), where, between 31 December 2019 to that date, 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths have been reported, with 17,750 and 1,441 deaths in Italy alone.
Obtaining the cumulative number and cumulative rate of COVID-19 cases
At the European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC), the number of reported COVID-19 cases in every country around the world, obtained only from official sources such as the Ministry of Health in countries, national and regional health authorities and the World Health Organization (WHO), is being updated daily at 8:00 a.m.
This data was used to assess COVID-19 trends in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom and compare them to those in Italy.
As a measure of the prevalence of COVID-19 active cases, we calculated the cumulative number of intermittent COVID-19 cases within 14 days, and then took into account the normal trajectory of COVID-19, in all EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom, between 1 January and 15 March 2020.
We also introduced the cumulative number of cases reported from each country as of March 15, 2020, at 8:00 a.m., compared to Italy between 31 January and 15 March 2020.
Covid-19 Trends in the European Union/European Economic Area (EEA) and the United Kingdom
Trends in the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases deducted over a 14-day period in the European Union/Eurasy Region and the United Kingdom have tracked those of Hubei Province (China) (Figure 1).
For the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom in general, the cumulative number of COVID-19 began to increase around February 21, then increased sharply around February 28, 2020 (complementary material).
This was often driven by a rapid increase in the number of cases reported from Italy, but all EU/EEA and UK countries showed trends similar to the cumulative number of COVID-19 (complementary substance).
Figure 2 shows the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases, in the European Union/EEA countries and the United Kingdom, compared in Italy between 31 January and 15 March 2020.
It highlights that as of 15 March at 8:00 a.m., 15 other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom have reported the total number of cases already similar to those in Italy only 3 weeks or less ago.
Our results indicate that the number of reported COVID-19 cases is rapidly increasing in the EU/EEA and the UK.
Significant trends in the cumulative number of COVID-19 indicate that the pandemic is escalating similarly rapidly in all countries.
This is despite the fact that countries are at different stages, despite the difference in national public health responses, and the possibility of differences defining the situation in different countries and protocols to choose patients who must be tested for COVID-19, including follow-up testing.
In early March 2020, doctors in the affected areas of Italy described the situation where about 10 percent of COVID-19 patients needed intensive care and media sources reported that hospitals and intensive care units in those areas had already reached their maximum capacity.
Data on hospital admissions and/or COVID-19 intensive care units are currently available at the EU/EEA level for 6% and 1% of cases, respectively (data not displayed).
However, they need to be systematically collected to complement existing monitoring data that focuses on the number of reported cases and the number of deaths.
A 2010–11 study showed great diversity in the availability of intensive care and intermediate care beds in Europe, ranging from 29.2 in Germany to 4.2 beds for every 100,000 people in Portugal.
This means that countries may have more or fewer resources than Italy (12.5 beds for intensive care and intermediate care per 100,000 people in 2010–11).
Satisfaction-related modeling scenarios for healthcare capacity, with estimates by each EU/EEA-UK country for the spread of COVID-19 cases that have been hospitalized with a 90 per centenary risk &gt; 90 per care capacity, are available in the Sixth COVID-19 Rapid Risk Assessment Update at the European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC).
As cases collected so far in certain regions of the European Union/European Economic Area (EEA) and the United Kingdom, and hospitals and intensive care units usually serve specific regional population groups, it is preferable to provide information about cases and intensive care beds at the designation level of regional statistical units 2 (NUTS-2).
Italy’s experience and current trends in other countries show that the COVID-19 pandemic is rapidly worsening in the EU/EEA and the UK.
States, hospitals and intensive care units should therefore prepare themselves for the SARS-CoV-2 sustainable community transition scenario and the increasing number of COVID-19 patients in need of health care, in particular intensive care, such as this occurring in the affected areas of Italy.
As outlined in the European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) rapid risk assessment, a rapid, proactive and comprehensive approach is key to delaying the spread of SARS-CoV-2 while shifting from containment to mitigation, as the expected rapid increase in the number of cases may not provide decision-makers and hospitals with sufficient time to understand, accept and adapt their response accordingly in the event that they are not implemented in advance.
Rapid risk assessment also lists public health measures to mitigate the impact of the pandemic.
There is a limited opportunity within which countries can increase their regulatory efforts to slow the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce pressure on health care.
If this fails, healthcare systems in other EU/EEA countries are likely to experience an increase in the number of patients requiring intensive care over the coming days or weeks.
The 2019 Coronavirus (COVID-19) outbreak, caused by SARS-CoV-2) causing severe acute respiratory Syndrome (SARS), killed more than 3,000 people and infected more than 80,000 people in China and elsewhere in the world, causing a humanitarian catastrophe.
The SARS-CoV-2 virus, similar to the similar virus, SARS-CoV, which infected thousands of people with SARS in 2003, can also be transmitted from bats and causes similar symptoms through a similar mechanism.
However, COVID-19 has a lower risk and mortality rate than SARS, but it is more moving and more influential among older people than young people and men than women.
In response to the rapidly increasing number of publications related to the emerging disease, this article seeks to provide a comprehensive and timely review of the rapidly evolving research topic.
We will address the basics related to the epidemiology of the disease, its causes, its virology, diagnosis, treatment, predictions of the course of the disease, and prevention.
While many questions still need to be answered, we hope that this review will help to understand and eliminate the disease that poses a threat.
On January 25, 2020, the Spring Festival has become an unforgettable memory for all Chinese who have been urged to stay indoors throughout the holiday and for many weeks afterwards due to an outbreak of an emerging viral disease.
The virus is very similar to the coronavirus (COV), which caused the severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) outbreak in 2003; hence, the World Health Organization (World Health Organization (World Health Organization) (World Health Organization (WHO) SARS-COV-CoV-2 on February 11, 2020, and its associated disease was called COVID-19.
The epidemic began in Wuhan, China, and quickly spread across the country, as well as nearly 50 other countries around the world.
As of 2 March 2020, the virus has led to 80,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19, with more than 40,000 patients discharged from hospitals and more than 3,000 deaths.
The World Health Organization (WHO) warns that COVID-19 is the “enemy number 1” and potentially stronger than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), in less than two months, more than 200 COVID-19 research has been published, including the disease’s virology, epidemiology, its causes, diagnosis and treatment, since the first report was released on January 7, 2020, which identified isolated virus sequencing through many patients.
This review aims to summarize the research progress in the field of the new and rapidly evolving topic.
Whenever possible, we will try to compare COVID-19 to SARS and another disease caused by Cove, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS, an outbreak that occurred in 2012).
We’ll also discuss what we’ve learned so far with regard to disease prevention and prediction, as well as some of the pressing questions that are still on the table.
Coronaviruses have traditionally been considered non-lethal pathogens in humans and have mainly caused about 15 percent of the four common colds.
However, in this century, we have confronted coronaviruses that cause severe human diseases twice, namely SARS-CoV and MERIS-CoV, which caused an outbreak of the disease originally in China in 2003 and Saudi Arabia in 2012, respectively, and the disease quickly spread to many other countries with a terrifying prevalence and deaths.
Therefore, the current COVID-19 is a third outbreak of coronavirus in recorded human history.
As illustrated in Figure 1.1, groups with unknown pneumonia were first reported by Wuhan on 31 December 2019 to the China National Health Commission.
Seven days later, the coronavirus sequence was released.
On 15 January 2020, Wuhan reported the death of the first case.
In the meantime, the epidemic has rapidly spread to neighboring cities, provinces and countries.
On 20 January, infections were reported among health caregivers, indicating that the disease could be transmitted from one person to another.
On 23 January, the city of Wuhan was closed and all public transport was suspended.
On January 24, the first clinical study on the disease reported that of the 41 confirmed cases, only 21 were in direct contact with the Wuhan seafood market, which was seen as the starting site of the infection from an unknown animal source.
On 30 January, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared an outbreak as a global health emergency.
At the time of the release of this report, the disease has already spread across China and about 50 other countries around the world (Figure (Figure 2).
As the situation is rapidly evolving, the final scope and seriousness of the outbreak have yet to be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a multi-centralized study of 8,866 patients, including 4,021 confirmed COVID-19 patients, provided a more up-to-date explanation (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX_rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 infected people of all age groups, but it mainly affected age groups between 30 and 65.
Approximately half (47.7 per cent) were over the age of 50, very few were under the age of 20, and only 14 were injured under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 infected a larger number of men (0.31/100,000) than women (0.27/100,000).
Covid-19 has mainly spread among and around groups in and around Hubei.
Covid-19 took an average of 5 (2-9) days from the onset of the infection to the diagnosis.
The incubation period averaged 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days.
The average time of injury from start to death was 9.5 (4.8-13) days.
The basic reproduction number (basic breeding number) was 3.77 (confidence range is 95%: 3.51-4.05), and the correlative reproduction numeral number was 2.23-4.82.
The number of infected people has increased exponentially before January 23, 2020, in line with the many travel operations ahead of China’s Spring Festival.
The mortality rate among confirmed patients was 1.44% (confidence range is 95%: 1.10-1.86%), and the rate of adjusted mortality for all patients was 3.06% (trust range is 95%: 2.02-4.59%).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 were gender (male), age (≥60), and acute pneumonia.
Coronaviruses are a family of large and coated viruses that contain a single-chasin DNA genome that is trendy.
It can be divided into four genera, alpha, beta, gamma and delta, and coronavirus viruses are known to infect humans.
The coated fork (S) of diabetic protein binds to its cellular receptors, the angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) and dipetidel peptidase 4 (DBB 4) for SARS-CoV and MERIS-CoV, respectively, then membrane merging occurs.
The genome of viral RNA is released into cytoplasm; after the viral genome is cloned, the genomic RNA is accompanied by diabetic casing proteins and vesicles containing pheronoids, which merge with the plasma membrane to release the virus afterwards.
Sars-CoV-2's first genomic sequencing was reported on January 10, 2020.
SARS-COV-2 was discovered to be a new type of beta-cove with a genetic identity of more than 99.98% among 10 sequencing samples collected from the original site of the outbreak, the Huanan Seafood Souq Bohan.
SARS-CoV-2 is more like SARS-CoV than MRS-CoV.
It was revealed through an electron microscopic examination of the transmission that SARS-CoV-2 is present in very thin sections of human airway epithelium.
It has been revealed that the angiotensin-conversing human enzyme 2 is the receiver of SARS-CoV-2 as well as SARS-CoV.
However, SARS-CoV-2’s S-Protent is bind to the angiotensin-converting human enzyme 2 weaker than SARS-CoV, which is consistent with the fact that SARS-CoV-2 causes a less severe infection in patients than SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form an emerging short protein encoded by orf3b and an excreted protein encoded by orf8.
Orf3b in SARS-CoV-2 may play a role in viral pathogenesis and limit the expression of IFNβ; however, orf8 does not have any domain or pattern that has a known function.
On February 18, 2020, Zhu and others communicated the structure of the full length of the angiotensin-conversing human enzyme 2 under the ultra-clow electron microscope with 2.9 Å accuracy within a composit with an amino acid vector B0AT1.
They discovered that the compound, which had open and closed structures, had accumulated to form a demer and that ACE2-B0AT1 could bind two S proteins, providing evidence to identify coronavirus and infections.
Sodium-based neutral amino acid vector may become a therapeutic target for examining drugs to suppress SARS-CoV-2 infections.
The original host and intermediate host
It has been known that both SARS-CoV and Merce-CoV originated from bats and were transported to humans through citriff and camels, respectively.
By comparing SARS-CoV-2's dynastical evolution with other coronaviruses, bats were considered SARS-CoV-2's original host, with the new 96 per cent identical to the two coronaviruses similar to SARS from bats called bats and bat-SL-CoVZX21.
However, the intermediate host that helped the virus cross the interfactional barrier to infect humans remains unknown and has not yet explained the trajectory of transmission.
Gi and others proposed snakes as carriers of the virus from bats to humans, which involved a symmetric recombination within the S protein.
According to a study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, have suggested that squamous ant eaters—long-toned mammals commonly used in traditional Chinese medicine—are the potential intermediate host for SARS-CoV-2 based on a 99% genetic similarity in coronavirus discovered in pangolinaries and SARS-CoV-2.
However, the 1% variation spread across two genomes is still a significant difference; therefore, we await conclusive results with concrete evidence (Figure 33).
SARS-COV-2’s physical properties are still largely unknown.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV are able to survive in the laboratory for 48 hours in a dry environment and up to 5 days at a temperature below 20 ° C and humidity ranging from 40% to 50%.
SARS-CoV-2 may have similar properties.
It has been reported that SARS-CoV-2 is sensitive to ultraviolet rays and temperature at 56 °C for 30 minutes; ethyrther, ethanol at a concentration of 75%, chlorine-containing disinfectants, peroxetic acid, chloroform, and other fatty solvents, but not chlorohxide, can effectively inhibit the virus.
The whole human race generally lacks immunity to SARS-CoV-2, so they are more likely to be infected with the emerging virus.
Currently, there has been no detailed study on SARS-CoV-2's immune response.
Therefore, we can only refer to previous studies related to other coronaviruses, in particular SARS-CoV and MERS-COV (Figure (Figure 4).
In general, after an invasion of a host virus, the natural immune system recognizes it through pattern recognition receptors (BRRS), including type C receptors similar to ketin, tulle-like receptors (TLR), receptors similar to nucletide-connecting vocabulary (NLR), and RIG-I receptor.
Through different trajectories, the virus induces the emergence of inflammatory factors, the maturation of dending cells, the synthesis of type I(IFN), which limits the spread of the virus and the acceleration of large phagocytes of viral antigens.
However, the N-protein in SARS-CoV can help the virus escape immune responses.
The adaptive immune response quickly joins the fight against the virus.
T lymphocytes, including CD4+ and CD8+, play an important role in defense.
CD4+ T cells stimulate B cells to produce antibodies intended for the virus, and CD8 + T cells directly kill virus-infected cells.
Auxiliary T cells produce inflammation-activating cytokines to help defending cells.
However, coronavirus can reduce the functions of T cells by indusciting the programmed death of T cells.
Mixed immunity, including complementary proteins such as C3a, C5a and antibodies, is also essential in the fight against viral infections.
For example, isolated antibodies have been neutralized from a recovering MyRS-CoV patient.
On the other hand, an overreaction of the immune system generates a large number of locally free radicals that can cause severe damage to the lungs and other organs and, in the worst-case scenario, a failure of multiple organ functions and death.
The SARS-CoV-2 infection, which initially appeared in groups, is more likely to affect the elderly with multiple illnesses and more pregnant women.
It’s common for people who are prone to a large number of viruses or whose immunosuppressive functions are more likely to get infected than others.
The estimated average incubation period of SARS-CoV-2 is 1-14 days, or often 3 to 7 days, based on a study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan.
However, a study of 1,099 cases shows that the incubation period reached an average of 3 days and ranged from 0 to 24 days.
A more recent study, as described above, showed that the incubation period amounted to 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days based on a demographic of 8,866 cases.
It’s very important that health authorities adjust effective quarantine time based on the strictest incubation period, thus preventing people who are infected but have no symptoms of transmitting the virus to others.
As a common practice, people who are exposed to or infected with the virus are usually asked to be quarantined for 14 days.
Does quarantine time have to be extended to 24 days?
Fever is often the main and primary symptom of COVID-19, and may not be accompanied or accompanied by other symptoms, such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscle pain, dizziness, headaches, sore throat, nasal stiffness, chest pain, nausea, and vomiting.
Some patients experienced shortness of breath and/or hypoxias one week after the onset of the disease.
In severe cases, the condition of the patients quickly progressed to suffer from acute respiratory syndrome, septic trauma, metabolic acidosis, and thrombopathy.
Patients with fever, respiratory symptoms and high fever should be screened, even without an imaging that detects pulmonary abnormalities, in search of the virus for early diagnosis.
A demographic study in late December 2019 showed that percentages of symptoms were 98 percent for fever, 76 percent for a dry cough, 55 percent for shortness of breath, and 3 percent for diarrhea; 8 percent of patients needed respiratory support.
Similar results have been reported in two recent studies of a group from a family and a group to which the infection was transmitted from an unsymptomatic person.
By comparison, a demographic study in 2012 showed that MERS-CoV patients also suffered from fever (98%), dry cough (47 percent), and shortness of breath (55 percent) as major symptoms.
However, 80 percent needed much more respiratory support from COVID-19 patients and were consistent with MERSE’s higher mortality rate than COVID-19.
Diarrhea is also observed (26 per cent) and sore throat (21%) in MERS patients.
In SARS’s patients, it was found that fever (99%-100%), dry cough (29%-75%), shortness of breath (40%-42%), diarrhea (20-25%), sore throat (13–25%) were the most important symptom, and respiratory support was needed to be provided to about 14%-20% of patients.
By 14 February, the mortality rate from COVID-19 had reached 2% while confirmed cases stood at 66,576 globally.
By November 2002, SARS had a mortality rate of 10% of the 8,096 confirmed cases.
According to MERS, the mortality rate was 37% of the 2,494 confirmed cases, according to a demographic study conducted in June 2012.
According to a previous study, SARS-CoV-2’s primary reproduction number was up to 6.47 with a 95 per cent confidence range from 5.71 to 7.23, while SARS-CoV’s primary reproduction figure ranged from only 2 to 4.
Comparison of SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV in terms of their own symptoms, mortality rate, and basic reproduction figure shown in table 1.1.
The figures above indicate that SARS-CoV-2 has a greater ability to spread than MERS-CoV and SARS-COV, but is less lethargic than the last two.
Therefore, the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic is far more difficult to control than the MIRS-CoV and SARS-CoV epidemic.
The onset of the disease occurs often in gatherings in the same family or from the assembly itself or the vehicle itself, such as a cruise ship.
Patients often have a history of travelling to or staying in Wuhan or other affected areas, or in contact with people or patients during the last two weeks before the onset of the disease.
However, it has been reported that people can carry the virus without showing symptoms for longer than two weeks, and recovering patients discharged from hospitals can carry the virus again, sending an alarm to increase quarantine time.
Patients have a normal or low number of peripheral white blood cells (especially lymphocytes) in the early stage.
For example, lack of lymphocytosis has been detected with a 4×109/L white blood cell count, including 1 × 109/L lymphocytes, and elevated levels of aspartate amino vector and blood predation in 1,099 patients with COVID-19.
Levels of liver, muscle and myoglobin enzymes increased in the blood of some patients, increased C-reactivating protein and the rate of erythrocytocytocytemia in the blood of most patients.
In patients with severe conditions, the level of demer D, which is caused by the cracking of fibrine in the blood, has gradually increased the number of lymphocytes.
Imbalances in radiography appear in most COVID-19 patients and are characterized by double patchwork shades or opaque glazed glazed in the lungs.
Patients sometimes have atypical pneumonia, severe lung injury, and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
When acute respiratory distress syndrome occurs, uncontrollable inflammation, fluid build-up, and elevated fibrosis strongly affect the exchange of gases.
The dysfunction of the pulmonary cells of type 1 and type 2 reduces the level of the superficial activator and increases surface tension, therefore, reduces the ability of the lungs to stretch and increases the risk of lung breakdown.
Therefore, the worst results of chest radiography and the maximum severity of the disease often compare.
On February 18, 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 showed pulmonary cell exfoliation, the formation of the hyalenic membrane, interstitial lymphatic infiltration, and the multi-influential syncytial cells in the lungs of a patient who died of the disease, in line with the pathology of viral infection and acute respiratory distress syndrome, similar to what happens to patients with SARS and MERS.
The discovery of SARS-CoV-2's RNA has been used through the polymerase chain reaction for reverse transcription (BT-PCR) as the main criterion for diagnosing COVID-19.
However, due to the elevated rate of false adverse outcomes, which may accelerate the epidemic, clinical manifestations of the diagnosis (which no longer individually rely on RT-PCR) began to be used in China on February 13, 2020.
A similar situation also occurred in SARS's diagnosis.
Therefore, a combination of the history of the disease, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests, and radiography discoveries is essential and essential for an effective diagnosis.
On February 14, 2020, Fengzhang Group described a protocol for the use of CRISPR-based Sherlock technology to detect SARS-CoV-2, discovering pieces of SARS-COV-2 synthesized RNA at a rate of 20 × 10-18 molly/liter to 200 × 10-18 molly/l (10-100 copies per �μL of input) using a dipping in less than an hour without the need for precision devices.
We hope that the new technology will be able to significantly improve allergies and convenience if they are verified in clinical samples.
Due to the lack of experience regarding the emerging coronavirus, doctors are able to provide supportive care mainly for COVID-19 patients, as they experience a variety of treatments that have already been used or proposed to treat other coronaviruses, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, and other viral diseases (Table 2.2).
These treatments include current and potential therapies with antiviral drugs, immunoconstrators, stimulants, plasma from recovering patients, Chinese medicine, and psychiatric support.
Even plasma from recovering patients has been suggested to be used for treatment.
Pharmaceutical manufacturers are racing to develop antibodies and anti-virus vaccines.
SARS-CoV-2 mainly attacks the lungs at first, and may also, to a lesser extent, attack other organs that emit an angiotensin-transformed enzyme, such as the gastrointestinal tract, intestine, and kidneys.
Respiratory dysfunction and failure, however, are the most serious threat to patients and the leading cause of death.
Therefore, respiratory support is necessary to relieve symptoms and save lives, and includes general oxygen therapy, high flow of oxygen, invasive respirators, and artificial respirators based on the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms should be supported by extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO), a cardiopulmonary metabolism technique used in the treatment of cardiac insufficiencia or life-threatening respiratory failure.
In addition, the maintenance of fluid balance, the prevention and treatment of secondary infections and septic shock, and the protection of vital organ functions are also essential for SARS-CoV-2 patients.
Cytokine storm is known to be caused by an overreaction of the immune system in SARS and MERIS patients.
Cytokine storm is a form of systemic inflammatory reactions characterized by a series of cytokins involving TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ, and MCP-1.
These cytokines induce immune cells to release a large number of free radicals and are the main cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome and multiple organ failure.
Immune suppression is essential in the treatment of cytokine storms, in particular for acute patients.
Activated hormones, tosilizumab, and an anti-interlukin-6 antibolic body have been used to treat cytokine storm.
Other immunosuppressants of the cytokine storm include the modification of T-cell-directed immunosuppressive reflux (IN-γ), IL-1 and TNF blockade; Janus kinase inhibition; plinatomumab; the cytokine-4 inhibitors; and heston dysitylase inhibitors.
Steroids and immunosuppressants have been extensively used to treat SARS to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
However, steroids in larger doses have not been helpful with severe lung infection in SARS and COVID-19 patients.
Instead, it can cause severe side effects, especially vascular necrosis, which greatly influences the prediction of the course of the disease.
However, it has been recommended to use short cycles of activated hormones in doses ranging from small to medium with caution for critical cases of COVID-19 patients.
At the time of writing, the efficacy of any antiretroviral treatment has not been confirmed.
However, it has been discovered that giving remedicivir, a neucletidic analogue, was effective with an U.S. COVID-19 patient.
Rimdecivir is an emerging antiviral initially developed by Gilead for the treatment of diseases caused by Ebola and Marburg viruses.
Subsequently, Remdecivir also showed a possible inhibition of other single-chain RNA viruses, including the MERS and SARS viruses.
Based on these viruses, Gilead introduced the compound to China to conduct some experiments on individuals infected with SARS-CoV-2, and the results are highly anticipated.
In addition, paristinibe, interferon-alpha, lupinavir/retonavir and ribavirin have been proposed as possible treatments for patients with severe respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, damage to the liver, and other adverse reactions can occur after polypinavir/ritonavire synthesis.
The interaction of these therapies with other drugs used with patients should be carefully monitored.
Plasma from recovered patients and antibodies
Collecting blood from patients who have recovered from an infectious disease to treat other patients with the same disease or to prevent healthy individuals from contracting the disease has a long history.
Indeed, recovering patients often have a relatively high level of antibodies against the pathogen in their blood.
Antibodies are immunoglobulin (IGs) produced by B lymphocytes to combat pathogens and other foreign objects, and they directly recognize and neutralize molecules unique to pathogens.
Based on this, plasma was collected from the blood of a group of patients who recovered from COVID-19 and injected into 10 patients with serious conditions.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours, accompanied by reduced inflammation, viral loads and improved oxygen saturation in the blood.
However, verification and clarification are necessary to introduce this method for large-scale use before specific treatments can be found.
In addition, due to therapeutic effects, certain plasma-associated defects should be carefully considered.
For example, antibodies can stimulate an over-stimulating immunosuppressive reaction and cause cytokine release syndrome, which is likely to be life-threatening toxicity.
Antibodies in the blood are usually low, and plasma demand is high for the treatment of patients with critical conditions.
It’s difficult to develop specific antibodies and produce them fast enough to fight a global epidemic.
Therefore, isolating B cells from recovering patients and identifying genetic codes that encode effective antibodies, or screening for effective antibodies against the virus’s underlying proteins, is more decisive and practical.
In this way, we can easily increase the production of antibodies.
Traditional Chinese medicine has been used to treat a variety of diseases in China for thousands of years.
However, its effects depend heavily on a variety of components in a composition that varies depending on the diagnosis of the disease based on the theories of traditional Chinese medicine.
Most active ingredients remain unknown or ambiguous as they are difficult to extract and check for these components or their optimal formulations.
Currently, due to the lack of an effective and specific treatment for COVID-19, traditional Chinese medicine has become an important alternative treatment for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or for those who have recovered from severe stages.
For example, the efficiencies of Xu Feng Ji-du capsules and Lian Hua Qing Win capsules for COVID-19 have been discovered.
The highest rates of recovery were observed in the treatment of COVID-19 patients in several provinces of Chine. Traditional Chinese medicine has been used in 87% of its patients, including Ganzhou (63.7%), Ningxia (50%), and Hunan (50%), while Hubei province, which used traditional Chinese medicine only with about 30% of COVID-19 patients, had the lowest cure rate (13%).
However, this is a very rough comparison, as many other impact factors such as the number and severity of their conditions should be included in the assessment.
On February 18, 2020, PolyZhang and his co-workers published a study comparing Western Medicine Therapy (WM) solemnly with the common therapy between Western Medicine and Traditional Chinese Medicine.
They discovered that the durations needed to recover from high body temperature, de-symptomatics, and hospital stays were much shorter in the Western Medicine + Traditional Chinese Medicine treatment group compared only to the Western Medicine Therapy group.
Most impressively, the rate of exacerbation of symptoms (which ranges from mild to severe) was much lower in the Western medicine treatment group + traditional Chinese medicine compared to the western medicine treatment group only (7.4% compared to 46.2%), and the mortality rate was lower in the western medicine treatment group + traditional Chinese medicine compared to the Western medicine treatment group only (8.8% compared to 39%).
However, the effectiveness and safety of traditional Chinese medicine is still waiting for more well-thought-out experiments on larger scales and in more centres.
It would also be interesting to characterize the mechanism of the procedures and clarify the active ingredients of traditional Chinese medicine treatments or their formulations if possible.
Patients who are suspected or confirmed to have COVID-19 are often very afraid of the highly contagious and even deadly disease, and quarantined people are bored, lonely, and angry.
In addition, symptoms of infections such as fever, hypoxia, and cough, as well as the negative effects of treatments, such as insomnia caused by stimulating hormones, can lead to more anxiety and mental disorder.
In the first phase of the SARS outbreak, a range of psychiatric illnesses were reported, including persistent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychotropic arousal, psychotic symptoms, delirium, and even suicidal tendencies.
Mandatory contact and quarantine, as part of public health responses to the COVID-19 outbreak, can make people more concerned and guilty about the effects of infection, quarantine, and stigmatizing their families and friends.
Therefore, mental health care must be provided for COVID-19 patients, people suspected of having it, people in their contacts, as well as the general public who need it.
Psychological support should include the establishment of multidisciplinary mental health teams, clear contacts with regular and accurate updates about the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak and its treatment plans, and the use of specialized electronic devices and applications to avoid direct contact with each other.
Effective vaccines to cut the chain of transmission of the virus from animal reservoirs and infected humans to vulnerable hosts are essential and often complementary to antiviral therapy in the fight against epidemics caused by emerging viruses.
Efforts have been made to develope S-based vaccines to generate neutral, effective and long-term antibodies and/or protective immunity against SARS-CoV.
Neighboring vaccinates have been evaluated in animal models for the treatment of SARS.
However, the efficaciency of these candidate vaccines has not been specified in the living body in the elderly and the models of the deadly challenge and their prevention and prevention of infection with an animal-sourced virus before the start of a clinical study.
This is probably because SARS faded 17 years ago and no new cases have since been reported.
In contrast, sporadic cases and groups of MERS continue to emerge in the Middle East and spread to other regions due to the continued presence of animal sources in endemic areas.
Immunization strategies were developed using an inactivated virus, oxygen-decrying RNA plasmids, viral vector carriers, nanoparticles, antiviral particles, and recombinant protein subunits, some of which were evaluated in animal models.
The development of a safe and effective vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 for non-immunity individuals is an urgent and critical task to control the ongoing epidemic.
However, overcoming difficulty is a challenge due to the length of time (averaging an average of 18 months) needed to develop a vaccine and the dynamic changes of coronaviruses.
Covid-19, as an emerging disease, has just begun to show its complete clinical trajectory over thousands of patients.
In most cases, patients can recover progressively without punishment.
However, COVID-19, similar to SARS and MERS, is also associated with high prevalence rates and deaths in severe patient cases.
Therefore, building a model for the progress of the disease is essential for health-care agencies to prioritize their services, particularly in resource-limited areas.
Depending on the clinical studies that have been reported to date, the following factors may influence or be associated with COVID-19 patients (Table 33):
Age: The age was the most important factor in determining the predictions of SARS, which also applies to COVID-19.
Age groups ranging from 30 to 65 were mainly infected with COVID-19, with 47.7% of these patients exceeding the age of 50 in a study of 8,866 cases as described above.
Patients in need of intensive care were more susceptible to latent conjunctive diseases and complications and were much older than those who did not need them (in the average age of 66 vs. 51), indicating age as a predictor of the outcomes of COVID-19 patients.
Gender: SARS-CoV-2 infected a larger number of men than women (0.31/100,000 versus 0.27/100,000), as described above.
Conjoining diseases and complications: COVID-19 patients who need intensive care are more likely to suffer from acute heart injuries and arrhythmias.
Cardiac accidents have also been the leading cause of death in SARS patients.
SARS-CoV-2 can also bind to positive bile cells with the angiotensin-converting enzyme 2, which can lead to dysfunctions in the liver in COVID-19 patients.
It should be noted that age and latent disease are strongly correlated and may interfere with each other.
Unnarmal laboratory results: The level of reactant Protein C (CRP) in the blood reflects the severity of inflammation or tissue injury, and it is suggested to be a predictor of the disease, response to treatment, and final recovery.
It was also suggested that the association between the level of reactive protein C and the severity and severity of COVID-19 disease and the projections of its functioning were also suggested.
In addition to this, an elevated enzyme that removes lactate hydrogen (LDH), aspartate amine vector (AST), alanine amine carrier (ALT), and creatine kinase (CK) may also help to predict the outcome.
These enzymes appear strongly in many organs, especially in the heart and liver, and are released during tissue damage.
Therefore, they are traditional signs of dysfunction in the heart or liver.
Main Clinical Symptoms: Chest radiography and the phased progression of clinical symptoms should be taken into account together, along with other problems, to predict the outcomes and complications of COVID-19.
The use of steroids: As described above, steroids are commonly used as an adjunctive treatment for infectious diseases to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
Since a high dose of large-scale stimulant hormones has been used in SARS patients, many survivors have suffered unvascular osteoporosis with lifelong disability and poor quality of life.
Therefore, steroids should be used in a low dose and for a short time in COVID-19 patients, if necessary.
As described above, during the COVID-19 outbreak, many patients experienced exceptional stress; they often endured long periods of quarantine, extreme uncertainty, and witnessed the deaths of close family members and fellow patients.
It is essential to provide psychological counseling and long-term support to help those patients recover from stress and return to normal life.
According to demographic studies to date, COVID-19 appears to have different epidemiological traits from SARS.
In addition to replication in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 can effectively replicate in the upper respiratory tract and cause mild or no symptoms in the early stage of infection, similar to other coronaviruses that cause common colds.
Therefore, patients infected in the early stage or incubation period are able to produce a large amount of the virus during daily activities, causing difficulty in controlling the epidemic.
However, SARS-CoV transmission was considered to occur when patients were in a severe medical condition, while most transmissions of the virus did not occur in the early stage.
Therefore, the current COVID-19 outbreak is much more severe and more difficult in terms of its control than the SARS outbreak.
Strenuous efforts are currently underway in China, including the closure of Wuhan and surrounding cities and the ongoing quarantine of almost the entire population, in the hope of hindering SARS-COV-2 transmission.
Although these measures have greatly affected the economy and other segments of the country, the number of new patients is declining, signaling a slowdown in the epidemic.
The most optimistic estimate is that the outbreak will end by March and the receding phase will last for 3 to 4 months.
However, some other experts do not have the same amount of optimism.
Paul Hunter and others have estimated that COVID-19, which looks far more contagious than SARS, will not end in 2020.
Ira Longini and others have created a model to predict the outcome of the epidemic and have suggested that SARS-CoV-2 may affect two-thirds of the world’s population.
SARS-CoV-2 has been detected in medium and throat swabs for patients who recovered and left the hospital two weeks ago, indicating that the newly discovered virus could become a periodic attack similar to the flu.
However, there have been promising signs in China based on the declining number of new cases, which suggests that current strategies may have worked.
Ebola was originally expected to cause up to one million cases with half a million deaths.
However, the disease was ultimately controlled by strict quarantine and isolation measures.
It’s possible, like SARS-CoV, that SARS-CoV-2 will become weaker in terms of causing the infection and eventually disappear or become a less pathogenic virus that coexists with humans.
A comparison of the COVID-19 COVID-19 Pandemic is shown below (Figure (Figure 55).
SARS-CoV-2 travels at a more elevated rate through cough or sneezing, and can also be transmitted through direct contact with substances contaminated with the virus.
The virus has also been found in stools, provoking a new possibility of moving from stool to mouth.
According to a recent study of 138 cases, 41% of cases may have been caused by hospital infections, including 17 patients with previous illnesses and 40 healthcare providers.
Significant precautions are therefore needed to protect human beings, especially healthcare providers, social workers, family members, colleagues, and even passers-by who have communicated with infected patients or people.
Wearing face masks is the first line of defense that can be used to reduce the risk of infection; the use of both surgical masks and N95 respiratory masks (1860s) helps to control the spread of viruses.
Surgical face masks prevent fluid diameters from a possible person from moving through the air or adhesing to the surfaces of the materials, as they can be passed to others.
However, only N95 masks (1860s) can protect against inhalation of small fireons by 10 to 80 nanometers, with only 5 percent of firionate being able to penetrate completely; SARS-CoV-2 resembles SARS-CoV in size of approximately 85 nanometers each.
Since the particles can penetrate up to five surgical masks together, health care providers in direct contact with patients must wear N95 masks (Series number 1860s) but not surgical masks.
In addition to masks, health care providers should wear appropriate isolation robes to further reduce contact with the virus.
Viruses can also infect a person across the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor contracted SARS-CoV-2 despite wearing an N95 mask; the virus may have entered his body through his inflamed eyes.
Therefore, health caregivers should also wear transparent facial protectors or glasses while working with patients.
For the general public of people in affected or potentially affected areas, it is highly recommended that everyone wash their hands with more disinfected soap than usual, and try to stay at home for self-quarantine and limit communication with potentially infected individuals.
Three feet is considered a convenient distance for people to stay away from the patient.
These measures are effective ways to reduce the risk of infection as well as prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 came as a new virus to the human world, its very similarity with SARS-CoV, as reported on January 7, 2020, would have caused a state of high alert in China based on its deep memory of the 2003 SARS outbreak.
However, until January 19, 2020, the director of the Centers for Disease Control in Wuhan did not reassure citizens by saying that the emerging virus has a low rate of infection and limited human-to-human reproduction and there is no problem in preventing and containing the disease.
This message sharply diminished the alertness of the general public, especially when the whole country was preparing for the Spring Festival, and a critical time was lost to contain the disease at its lowest sleeve in Wuhan.
China’s disease control agencies may benefit from this ruthless lesson and implement substantial improvements in the future.
For example, these agencies should be (1) more cautious when issuing public statements because every word is important for citizens and can change their behavior and decisions; (2) more sensitive and interactive with unusual information from clinics rather than waiting for official reports from doctors or officials; (3) more restrictive to contain a possible epidemic in its early stages rather than trying to reassure the public; and (4) make targeted and effective exercises to increase public awareness of epidemiological diseases and periodically test and improve the community response system.
The outbreak of COVID-19 caused by the novel SARS-CoV-2 virus began at the end of December 2019.
It spread in less than two months across China and about 50 other countries globally at the time of this writing.
Since the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and the symptoms are also similar between COVID-19 and SARS, the COVID-19 outbreak has sparked a recurrence of SARS infection.
However, there are some notable differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are necessary to contain the epidemic and treat patients.
Covid-19 affects older individuals more than young people and in men than women, and the rate of severity and mortality is also higher in older people than in young people.
SARS has a higher mortality rate than COVID-19 (10.91% versus 1.44%).
COVID-19 patients transmit the virus even while they don’t have symptoms, while SARS patients usually do this when they’re severely ill, causing more difficulty in contemplating the spread of COVID-19 from SARS.
This partly explains why SARS-CoV-2 has spread much faster and wider than SARS-CoV.
A regular SARS-CoV-2 RNA test can be negative in some COVID-19 patients.
On the other hand, recovering patients can catch the virus again.
These results significantly increase the risk of the spread of the virus.
With this rapid progress in COVID-19 research, there are still many critical issues that need to be resolved, as follows:
Where'd SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Despite the discovery of a 96 per cent genetic similarity between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-coronavirus in bats, we still cannot conclude that SARS-CoV-2 comes from bats.
Which animal was the intermediate species that transmitted the virus from the original host, such as bats, to humans?
Without knowing the answers for No. 1 and 2, we cannot stop the transition efficiently, and the outbreak can come back at any time.
Although molecular modeling and biochemical tests have shown that SARS-CoV-2 binds to the angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2, how exactly does the virus enterge into aerodynamic cells and cause subsequent pathological changes?
Is the virus also related to the secretory cells of the angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2 in other organs?
Without clear answers to these questions, we cannot make a quick and accurate diagnosis and provide effective treatment.
How long will this epidemic last?
How does the virus develop genetically as it is transmitted between humans?
Is it going to become a global pandemic, or fading like SARS, or it's coming back periodically, like the flu?
It’s necessitable but it may take some time to look for answers to the questions above and many other questions.
However, at any cost, we have no other choice but to stop the epidemic as quickly as possible and return our lives to normal.
Animal Origins of Human Coronaviruses
The mutation and adaptation have been driving the co-evolvement of coronaviruses (coronaviruses) and their hosts, including humans, for thousands of years.
Before 2003, it was known that two types of human coronaviruses (human coronaviruses) caused a mild disease, such as a cold.
Exclosions of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) have shown the other face to reveal the extent to which human coronavirus infections can be severe and life-threatening.
The appearance of SARS-CoV-2 in Central China at the end of 2019 re-shed light on coronaviruses, and surprised us with the speed of its spread but with the ability to cause the disease compared to his brother SARS-CoV.
Human coronavirus is one of the animal diseases of origin, so our understanding of the animal origins of human coronaviruses will serve us well.
Most human coronaviruses come from bats where they have not been pathogenic.
Intermediated repository hosts have also been known for some human coronaviruses.
Identification of hosting animals has a direct impact on the prevention of human diseases.
Investigating the interactive relationships between coronavirus and the hospiter in animals also offers an important insight into the emergence of coronavirus disease in humans.
In this review, we provide an overview of the available information about the seven human coronaviruses, focusing on the history of their discovery as well as their animal origins and interspecies transmission.
The most important thing is that we are comparing different human coronaviruses from the perspective of the evolution of the virus and the recombination of the genome.
In this context, the disease caused by the current coronavirus pandemic 2019 (COVID-19) is discussed.
In addition, it also highlights the requirements for successful hosting substitution and the impact of the complications of the development of the virus on the severity of the disease.
Coronaviruses (coronaviruses) belong to the coronavirus family, which includes a group of coated viruses with single-chained chain reboze DNA.
These viruses contain the largest genome among RNA viruses with 26 to 32 kilogram bases and are called "coronaviruses" by that name because their composition resembles a coron under an electron microscope.
In terms of synthetic properties, coronaviruses have undivided genomes that share similar regulation.
Approximately two-thirds of the genome contains two large overlapping open-read molds (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are converted into two types of multiproteins pp1a and pp1ab.
Polyproteins are additionally treated to generate 16 non-structural proteins, referred to as nsp1~16.
The remaining part of the genome contains open reading templates for structural proteins, including Spike (S), Casing (E), Membrane (EM) and Nuclear Protein (EN).
Different strains of coronaviruses are also encoded a number of additional proteins from special strains.
Based on the difference in protein sequences, coronaviruses are classified into four types (coronavirus alpha, coronavirus beta, coronavirus gamma, and delta coronavirus), including beta coronavirus types, which include most human coronaviruses and are divided into four strains (A, B, C and D).
Evidence of genetic evolution has shown that bats and rodents act as a genetic origin for most alpha coronaviruses and beta coronaviruses, while birds are the main repository of gamma coronaviruses and delta coronaviruses.
Over thousands of years, coronaviruses have consistently bypassed species barriers and some have emerged as causes of serious human diseases.
So far, seven human coronaviruses (human coronaviruses) are known.
Among them are the human coronavirus-229E and the human coronavirus-NL63, and they belong to alpha coronaviruses.
The other five beta coronaviruses include Human Coronavirus-OC43, Human Coronavirus-HKU1 Coronavirus, Coronavirus Associated with Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV), Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (MERS-CoV), and SARS-CoV-2.
Human coronavirus-229E, human coronavirus-OCC43, human coronavirus-HKU1, human coronavirus-NL63 usually causes mild symptoms, such as cold, diarrhea or both.
By contrast, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and the newly-recognized SARS-CoV-2 virus are all highly pathogenic, causing severe infection of the lower respiratory tract to a larger number of patients with a higher probability of acute respiratory distress syndrome (accidental respiratory distress syndrome) and non-pulmonary symptoms.
She was isolated from human coronavirus-229E, the first strain B814, from nasal excretions of cold-infected patients in the mid-1960s.
Lots of information has since been gathered by extensive studies on HIV-229E and H Coronavirus-OC43, whose symptoms spontaneously heals.
In fact, it was generally recognized that infection with human coronaviruses was harmless until the SARS outbreak.
The 2003 SARS outbreak is among the deadliest epidemics in our present history, exceeding 8,000 with approximate deaths of about 10%.
Ten years later, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) has spread, causing a steady epidemic in the Arabian Peninsula, with sporadic spread to the rest of the world.
The novel coronavirus 2019 (HCV-2019), which was renamed SARS-CoV-2, is the causative agent of the current COVID-19 coronavirus disease (COVID-19), which killed more than 3,120 people and infected more than 91,000 people on March 3, 2020.
The alarm bell rings and the world must prepare for the upcoming SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
All seven human coronaviruses have animal origins from bats, mice, or pets.
Multiple evidence supports that the evolutionary origins of all human coronaviruses are bats, where viruses are well adapted and not pathogenic but show great genetic diversity.
The COVID-19 pandemic has sparked enormous medical, scientific, social, and ethical challenges for China and the world.
Tracking the animal origins of human coronaviruses has shown a framework for understanding natural history, the driving force and the restrictive factors of species mobility.
This may also guide or facilitate the search for SARS-CoV-2’s warehouse, intermediate host, and amplified hosting (inflated host animals), with important impacts to prevent future repercussions.
In this review we provide an overview of animal origins, interspecies transmission, and human coronaviruses cause diseases.
In particular, we highlight and discuss the popular belief that the original viruses of human coronaviruses are usually not pathogenic in their natural reservoid hostesses but become pathogenic after they are transmitted between species to a new host.
We also review the assessment of the course of the human coronavirus where the increase in transmissibility is often accompanied by a decrease in the severity of the disease.
In this context, we also discuss the consequences of the current SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.
Animal coronaviruses have been known since the late thirties of the 1930s.
Before the B814 strain of the human coronavirus-229E was isolated for the first time from nasal excretions of cold-infected patients, various types of coronaviruses were isolated from various infected animals, including turkeys, mice, beef, pigs, cat, and dog.
In recent decades, seven types of human coronaviruses have been identified.
A brief summary presentation of the history of human coronavirus discovery in chronological order (Table 1) would be educational and indicative.
The first strain of human coronavirus-229E from the respiratory tract was isolated for patients with upper respiratory tract infection in 1966, after which it was adapted to live in the ranks of WI-38 lung cells.
Patients infected with HIV-229E have common cold symptoms, including headaches, sneezing, malaise, and sore throats, with a fever, and a cough in almost 10~20% of cases.
Later in 1967, the human coronavirus-OC43 was isolated from the organ farm and was the subsequent transmission in the brains of infant mice.
The clinical traits of human coronavirus-OC43 infection appear to be similar to those caused by HIV-229E, which are symptomatic and indistinguishable from infection with other respiratory viruses such as influenza A viruses and nasal viruses.
Human coronavirus-229E and human coronavirus-OC43 have spread globally, often inclined to transition during the winter season in the temperate climate.
In general, the incubation time of these two viruses is less than one week, followed by a sense of disease for about two weeks.
According to a human volunteering study, the infection of healthy individuals with human coronavirus-229E has caused a mild cold.
Only a few immunodeficient patients have shown severe injury to the lower respiratory tract.
SARS, also known as ‘atypical pneumonia’, is the first human coronavirus-induced pandemic to be well documented in human history and the causative agent is SARS-CoV, the third human coronavirus to be detected.
The first SARS case dates back to late 2002 in China’s Guangdong province.
The SARS epidemise has resulted in 8,096 confirmed cases and 774 deaths, and has spread across many countries and continents.
Regardless of the supervasive vector, it has been estimated that a single case can lead to about two secondary conditions, with an incubation period of 4 to 7 days and the peak viral load appears on the 10th day of the disease.
Symptoms of SARS-CoV begin with muscle pain, headaches, fever, malaise, and chills, followed as late-symptom by difficulty breathing, cough and respiratory distress.
Lack of lymphocytosis, liver dysfunction, and high creatine kinase are common laboratory imbalances of SARS.
It is also noted in SARS patients a diffused sulchar injury, apparent cell proliferation, and an increase in the number of phagocytic cells.
Approximately 20–30% of patients then need intensive care and artificial respiration.
In addition to the lower respiratory tract, in severe cases, many organs, including the gastrointestinal tract, liver and kidneys, can also be affected, usually accompanied by a cytokine storm that can be fatal, especially in immunodeficient patients.
The virus was isolated for the first time from an open lung biopsy of a relative of the first infection who traveled from Guangzhou to Hong Kong.
Since then, huge efforts have been devoted to conducting research on human coronavirus.
Human coronavirus-NL63 from a 7-month-old child was isolated from the Netherlands in late 2004.
Initially found to be common in young children, the elderly, and immunodeficiency patients with respiratory diseases.
Colds, conjunctivitis, fever, and bronchitis are a common symptom of disease caused by human coronavirus (NL63).
Another independent study described isolating the virus itself from a sample from the nose of an 8-month-old child with pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Despite its discovery in the Netherlands, it is widespread globally.
It is estimated that the human coronavirus-NL63 is responsible for nearly 4.7% of common respiratory diseases and peaked during early summer, spring and winter.
Human coronavirus-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, also known as the strangulation.
In the same year, the human coronavirus-HKU1 has been quarantined from a 71-year-old man who has been hospitalized for pneumonia and bronchitis in Hong Kong.
In addition to community pneumonia and bronchitis, HKU1 has reportedly been associated with worsening acute asthma attacks.
It has been found that the human coronavirus-HKU1 is widespread globally, and causes mild respiratory diseases, similar to human coronavirus-NL63, human coronavirus-229E and human coronavirus-OC43.
In general, the probability of it mutating to cause diseases characterized by high prevalence is low, but the coincidence occurred for unknown reasons, as in the rare case of the most aggressive subtype of the human coronavirus-NL63, which has recently been reported to cause a severe infection of the lower respiratory tract in China.
In general, when coronaviruses acquire this ability to transmit efficiently and continuously maintain themselves within humans, they also become less virulent or pathogenic.
MERS-CoV was first isolated in 2012 from the lungs of a 60-year-old patient with pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia.
While most laboratory-confirmed cases come from the Middle East, in many European countries and Tunisia it has been reported cases that have been brought from abroad and a rare secondary transfer of contacts in close personal contact.
There was another secondary outbreak in South Korea in 2015, resulting in 186 confirmed cases.
The clinical symptoms caused by MERS are similar to those caused by SARS, and are characterized by worsening acute pneumonia.
Contrary to SARS, many people living with MERS have also experienced acute kidney failure, which distinguishes MERS from other diseases caused by human coronavirus.
More than 30% of patients experience gastrointestinal symptoms, such as diarrhea and vomiting.
On February 14, 2020, more than 2,500 laboratory-confirmed cases were reported with a high mortality rate of 34.4%, making MERS-CoV one of the deadliest destructive viruses known to humans.
From mid-December to late 2019, groups of pneumonia patients were later identified as SARS-CoV-2 in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has announced that the current outbreak of the lower respiratory tract infection due to SARS-CoV-2 is a public health emergency of international importance as called COVID-19.
On March 3, 2020, 90,053 cases were confirmed worldwide, with an initial mortality rate of 3.4%.
It is noteworthy that the mortality rate in the Hubei region of China is 4.2%, while it is 1.2%.
SARS-CoV-2 causes severe respiratory infections such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which appear in the form of fever, cough and shortness of breath.
Diarrhea also appears in some infected people.
Pneumonia is one of the most serious symptoms and can progress quickly to acute respiratory distress syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very similar due to the large homogeneity in the nucleotide sequence of 82%, they belong to different branches in the tree that develop breeds.
SARS-COV-2 appears to be less severe but more contagious than SARS-CoV and MERS-COV.
It has been reported that there are asymptomatic people despite being infected with SARS-CoV-2, which may contribute to its rapid spread around the world.
The comparison and disparity between SARS-CoV-2 and the other six human coronaviruses has revealed similarities and differences of great importance.
Initially, human coronaviruses are similar in incubation period and the time period of the disease.
In this regard, SARS-COV-2 follows the general trend of the other six human coronaviruses.
Secondly, the severity of the symptoms of COVID-19 infection falls between SARS-CoV and the four human coronaviruses acquired by society (i.e
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 appears with features that are more common in cases of community-acquired human coronaviruses, such as non-specific or minor symptoms or even the absence of symptoms.
On the other hand, a small subset of severe cases of COVID-19 can also be seen as in SARS-CoV, although the percentage is slightly lower.
Thirdly, SARS-CoV-2 transmission shows interesting patterns related to both community-acquired human coronaviruses and SARS-CoV.
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2’s transmissibility is at least at least the transmissibility of community-acquired human coronaviruses.
On the other hand, it remains to be verified whether the transmissibility of SARS-CoV-2 decreases after transmission to humans, as is the case with SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Finally, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in stool specimens as in other human coronaviruses.
Further studies are still needed in the future to clarify whether SARS-CoV-2 transmission from the oral anal route plays an important role, at least in the case of SARS-CoV.
It’s worth caring whether SARS-CoV-2 may show a seasonal spread as is the case of human coronaviruses acquired by the community.
However, SARS-CoV-2 attributes, including the possibility of transmission, the mechanism of disease and sustainable spread after human transmission will have an impact on the ultimate fate of the current COVID-19 outbreak.
All of the four human coronaviruses acquired from the community and causing minor symptoms have adapted well to humans.
Another perspective, which may also be true, is that human beings have adapted well to these four human coronaviruses.
In other words, they’re probably both survivors of ancient human coronavirus epidemics.
Human coronaviruses that infect humans with severe diseases, as well as people who have contracted acute human coronavirus diseases, have been eradicated.
For this to happen, human coronaviruses in humans mutate enough to accumulate adaptive mutations facing the host’s restricting factors.
Proceeding from this logic, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak persists and the greater the number of those infected, the greater the chance that it will fully adapt to humans.
If it adapts well, it’s hard to stop its transmission between humans by quarantine or other infection control measures.
Coronaviruses, which are acquired from the community, have spread among human populations for many years, causing a cold in people with good immunity.
These viruses don't need animal repositories.
In contrast, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which cause severe morbidity, did not fully adapt to humans, nor were they able to keep moving between humans.
They need to stay and breed in their animal reservoirs and then look for the right opportunity to move to the most vulnerable human goals, perhaps through one or more inflated intermediate hostesses.
SARS-CoV-2 is characterized by similar traits of both SARS-CoV/MiRS-CoV and the four human coronaviruses acquired by society.
It's very contagious, like human coronaviruses acquired from the community, at least for now.
However, it causes the disease more than community-acquired human coronaviruses and less than SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV.
It’s not yet clear if it’s going to fully adapt to humans and spread among them without the need for an intermediary animal reservoir or host.
Before discussing the animal origins of human coronaviruses, it will be useful for us to review the definitions and characteristics of evolutionary, natural, repositorious, intermediated and inflated holy coronaviruses.
The animal acts as an evolutionary host of the human coronavirus if it harbors the virus of its closely related origin and shares a significant similarity in the level of nucleotide sequences.
The original virus is usually well-adapted and not causing morbidity to that host.
Similarly, the repository host harbors the human coronavirus continuously and over a long period.
In both cases, the hosts become naturally infected and the natural hosts of the human coronavirus or their father virus become normal.
In contrast, if the human coronavirus enters a newly intermediate host just before it attacks humans or nearby, it doesn’t adjust well to the new host and often causes morbidity.
This intermediate host can act as an animal source of human infection and serve as an amplified host by allowing the virus to be temporarily copied and thus transmitted to humans to expand the extent of infection among humans.
Human coronavirus infection may reach a dead end if the virus fails to be transmitted from within the intermediate host.
Conversely, human coronaviruses can also adapt to their intermediate host and may even settle for a long time.
In this case, the intermediate host becomes the natural repository host.
Epidemiological data later revealed that SARS’s first case had a history of contact with the prey.
Serological diffusion surveys then indicated that animal traffickers have a higher prevalence of SARS-CoV IgG compared to ordinary people.
For the first time, it was determined that Paguma larvata and raccoon dogs in live animal markets carry SARS-CoV-like viruses that roughly match SARS-CoV.
This was indirectly supported by the fact that no other SARS infection was reported after all civet cats were killed in the markets.
However, it has been reported that the result of the SARS-COV examination of the masked palm yogurt that lives in the wild or farmed without being exposed to living animal markets has been largely negative, indicating that masked palm yogurt may be only an intermediary inflated host, but it is not the natural repository of SARS-CoV.
It is noteworthy that since 80% of the different animals in the Guangzhou markets have antibodies to SARS-CoV, the likelihood that multiple types of small mammals also play the role of an inflated mediator for SARS-CoV is not excluded.
Looks like all of these are hosts who don’t move SARS-CoV.
The subsequent search for the animal natural host of SARS-CoV led to the detection of the closely related bat coronavirus, which is dubbed by the Chinese horseshoe bat virus associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome HKU3 (Chinese horseshoe bat virus associated with HKU3), which is found in Chinese horseshoe bats.
SARS-CoV antibodies in these bats and the genome sequencing of the Chinese horseshoe battach sensor associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (HKU3) tested positive for SARS-CoV antibodies.
This virus and other coronaviruses of bats share 88-92% similarity of nucleotide sequences to SARS-CoV.
These studies have laid the foundation for the new concept that bats are hostest of emerging human pathogens.
Many severe acute respiratory syndrome-like coronaviruses have been identified from bats, but none of them have been isolated as a living virus, with the exception of WIV1.
Angiotensin-conversive enzyme 2 (Angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2) is known as SARS-CoV receptor.
WIV1, extracted from a bat stool specimen, has been shown to use the angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 in the bat, yogurt cat, and humans as a receiver to enter the cell.
Interestingly, serum recovered from severe acute respiratory Syndrome had the ability to equalize WIV1.
So far, WIV1 is the most closely linked to SARS-CoV in bats, as they share 95 percent symmetry of nucleotide sequences.
Despite the high similarity between these two viruses, it is generally believed that WIV1 is not the SARS-CoV direct father virus and that bats are not a direct repository for SARS-CoV.
Analyzing the evolvence of MRS-CoV strains in the HKU4 coronavirus group in bats and coronavirus-HKU5 in bats itself.
Coronavirus-HKU4 is used in bats and MERS-CoV receivables of the same host, peptidyl peptidase4 (peptidyl peptidase4), to enter the virus.
The polymeriase sequencies of the RNA based on MIRS-CoV are closer in terms of the evolution of strains to their counterparts from beta coronaviruses in bats identified in Europe and Africa.
It has not yet been discovered that Meers-Cove lives in wild bats.
Myris-Cove and Coronavirus-HKU25 in their closest bats share only 87% symmetry of nucleotide sequences.
Thus, host bats may not be the direct repository of MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, studies in the Middle East have shown that the only camel hump-positive serum for the equivalent antibodies of MERS-CoV, as well as camels of Middle Eastern origin in many African countries.
MERS-COV, the living confined virus found in humans, has been isolated from nasal swabs of the only hump, noting that camels serve as the original repository of MERS-CoV.
It is also noteworthy that minor symptoms generally appeared in camels that were experimentally infected with MIRS-CoV, but extensive viral scattering has been observed.
Infected camels were scattering viruses remarkably not only through breathing but also through the oral anal route, which is the primary way to scatter the virus from bats.
However, there are still questions because many of the confirmed cases of MERIS have no contact history with camels prior to the onset of symptoms, and these cases have been attributed in logical terms to the transmission from human to human or unknown modes of transmission involving unidentified animal species.
SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus RaTG13 participates in bats isolated from horseshoe bats in nucleotide symmetry by 96.2%.
Sequencing between SARS-CoV-2 and RATG13 is too long to determine the relationship with the father, as in the cases of SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
This means that bats may not be the host (hosters) direct repository of SARS-CoV-2 unless coronaviruses are found almost identical in the future.
SARS-COV-2’s intermediate hostesses are presumed to be among the wildlife species sold and killed at the Wuhan Seafood Market, to which many of the initial COVID-19 cases have been linked, suggesting a possible animal-to-human transmission.
Numerous recent studies based on metagenomic sequencing have suggested that a group of endangered small mammals known as manis janavica can also harbor original beta-coronaviruses associated with SARS-CoV-2.
The genomes of the novel coronavirus for these ant eaters and SARS-CoV-2 share the symmetry of nucleotide sequences by 85-92%.
However, they are closely associated with RaTG13 with about 90 percent similarity at the level of nucleotide sequences.
Two subspecies of SARS-CoV-2-like viruses are clustered into a strain evolutionary tree, one of which shares a similar receptor binding (receptor correlation area) with SARS-CoV-2, with 97.4 per ceventures of amino acides matched.
In stark contravention, SARS-CoV-2 receptor correlation areas differ significantly from RaTG13, although the genome-full sequencing symmetry is higher.
A previous study on a diseased anthropaxi also revealed the presence of viral organisms from lung samples, which turned out to be similarly linked to SARS-CoV-2.
This study adopted different assembly methods and manual processing to generate partial genome sequencing consisting of a full-length viral genome of approximately 86.3%.
We cannot rule out the possibility that the pangolin is one of SARS-CoV-2's intermediate host animals.
However, there is currently no evidence to support that the pangolin is the direct origin of SARS-CoV-2 due to the sequence variation between SARS-CoV-2 and beta-coronaviruses associated with SARS-CoV-2 in the pangolic.
In addition, spacing between SARS-CoV-2 and RATG13 is less than the spacing between SARS-CoV-2 and beta coronaviruses associated with SARS-CoV-2 in the pangolin.
The evolutionary trajectory of SARS-CoV-2 in bats, squamous ant eaters, and other mammals is still under appointment.
While the highest sequential symmetry has been found in receptor binding areas between SARS-CoV-2 and squamous ant eaters, beta coronaviruses linked to SARS-CoV-2, SARS-CoV-2 and RATG13 share the higher symmetry of full genome sequence.
It is a major speculation that the high degree of similarity between SARS-CoV-2 receptor receptor coronavirus (PETA) and SARS-CoV-2 is subdued by selective convergent evolution.
A counterproposal is likely to reconstruct between SARS-CoV-2 in beta coronavirus and RaTG13 in the terrestrial animal species.
Reconstructivation is widespread among beta coronaviruses as a driving force for development.
The direct animal origin of SARS-CoV-2 has not yet been confirmed.
In addition to highly pathogenic human coronaviruses, the animal origin of human coronavirus-229E, human coronaviruse-OC43, human coronavirus-NL63, and human coronavirus-HKU1 has also been examined.
Evidence for the evolution of strains has indicated that both the human coronavirus-NL63 and HIV-229E may have originated from bat coronaviruses, while the father viruses of the human coronavirus-OC43 and the human coronavirus-HKU1 have been found in rodents.
Coronavirus in bats called ARCoV.2 (coronavirus altitudes) detected in tricolor bats in North America has a close relationship with the human coronavirus-NL63.
On the other hand, it has been found that human coronavirus-229E is genetically associated with another coronavirus in bats, called Hipposideros / GhanaKwam/19/2008, which was discovered in Ghana, and it has been suspected that calias are also its intermediate host.
To clarify, current information has been summarized about the animal origins of human coronavirus known in Figure 1 and Table 2.
Analyzing the evolution of strains has provided evidence for the transmission of human coronaviruses between species throughout history.
When the human coronavirus-OC43 crossed the species to infect humans from household cattle around 1890, a respiratory infection epidemic was recorded.
The history of human coronavirus-229E transmission between species is still less clear.
It has been discovered that alpha coronaviruses in bats are closely related to human coronavirus-229E.
Between them there is coronavirus alpha in Albaca.
Many clues support the transmission of the virus from bats directly to humans.
Firstly, humans, not albakas, may have contact with bats in a shared environmental habitat.
While human beings have close contact with Albaka.
Secondly, the human coronavirus-229E belonging to coronavirus alpha in bats is multiple and not pathogenic in bats, while coronavirus alpha in albaca causes outbreaks of respiratory diseases in infected animals.
Finally, the alpha coronavirus associated with albaca has not been detected in wild animals.
Thus, the possibility that albaca has been infected with HIV-229E is not excluded from humans.
In fact, bats are the direct source of viruses that cause diseases to humans including rabies virus, Ebola virus, Nipah virus, and Hendra virus.
So it’s not surprising that bats may transmit human coronavirus-229E directly to humans.
While alpha coronaviruses in bats play the role of assembling human coronavirus-229E genes, albaca and single-humpine camels may act as intermediate hosts that transmit viruses to humans, just like in Meers-Cove.
Merse-Cove gives an excellent example of moving between species as it travels from bats to hump-only camels and from hump-only camel to humans.
The evolutionary origin of Merce-Cove has been known from bats from the very beginning of their identification and has also been strengthened by subsequent results.
It is clear that bats provide a rich pool of virus species to exchange gene parts within the same species and transmit them between species.
Bats are characterized by longevity, living in overcrowded colonies, close social interaction, and the strong ability to fly, all of which are favorable conditions to make an ideal "virus distributor".
On the other hand, Meers-Cove has moved on to the only camel for decades.
You’ve copied with these camels well until you switched from an intermediary host to a natural and stable repository hoster.
MERS-CoV causes a very mild illness and maintains a relatively low mutation rate in these animals.
Its intermittent transmission to human beings remains coincidental and man is an unvecasing host for Merce-Cove where its transmission cannot continue.
Contrary to the role of camels in the transmission of MERS-CoV, the role of the pangolin, if any, in the SARS-CoV-2 transmission is different.
In particular, beta coronaviruses associated with squamous ant eaters make him very sick.
They may be non-vector hosts for SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus beta, similar to the civet cat in SARS-CoV case.
Future studies will resolve several possibilities for SARS-CoV-2’s transmission between species from animals to humans either by proving or excluding them.
Initially, bats may be the hostess a repository of a virus linked to SARS-CoV-2, mostly identical to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans and bats may share an ecological habitat through slaughter or coal mining.
Secondly, squanistic ant eaters can be one of their recently discovered SARS-CoV-2 intermediate inflated hosts.
Human beings are infected with the virus by slucking and consuming prey meat.
Many mammals, including pets, are likely to be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2.
An antibody survey is warranted in pets and wild animals.
Thirdly, as mentioned above, there may have been a recombination and adaptation of SARS-CoV-2 in a third species that has contact with bats and squamous ants.
The search for Sars-CoV-2’s animal origin is still ongoing.
Regardless of the different types of animal hostesses, from the viral side there are three main and also important factors in facilitating coronavirus infection to bypass species barriers.
First of all, her mutation rates are relatively high in multiplying RNA.
The estimated rates of coronavirus mutations, compared to other single-chain RNA viruses, can be considered "medium" or "high" with an average substitution rate of ~10-4 switches each year for Site 2, depending on the coronavirus adaptation phase to the new flight attendants.
Coronaviruses involve a corrective external ribonicase enzyme, which eliminates turbulence and very high attenuation or inability to evolve.
Interestingly, remedisiver isotope nucleotides is known for its ability to suppress coronavirus coronavirus by inhibiting the external ribonuclease enzyme and RNA-based RNA-based polymerase.
Rimdecivir is one of the anthropogenic agents of SARS-CoV-2, which is scheduled to be tested in clinical trials.
However, coronavirus mutation rates increase by about a million times more than those of hostesses.
In addition, mutation rate is often high when coronaviruses don’t adapt appropriately to the host.
The mutation rate of SARS-CoV-2 is remarkably low when compared to SARS-CoV with a high mutation rate, indicating a higher level of human adaptation.
It seems that he has already adapted with another host close to the human.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2, this is also true of MERS-CoV, which has adapted well to the only camel hump.
In theory, hereditary deviation is unlikely helping vaccines and antiviral drugs to resist SARS-CoV-2 by rapidly weakening its effectiveness.
Secondly, the genome of massive RNA in coronaviruses actively provides an additional influenza in genome modification of mutations and recombination, thus increasing the likelihood of interspecies co-evolution, which is useful in the emergence of new coronaviruses when conditions become appropriate.
This is supported by unique abundant open reading templates and encoded protein functions near the end of the genome.
Third, coronaviruses randomly and repeatedly switch templates during RNA coping through the unique “copy selection” mechanism.
In a host that is like a blending vessel, sequencing switching occurs frequently during coronavirus RNA copies.
Full-length high-length RNA and secondary genomic DNA may recombinate new coronaviruses.
A special guide has been discovered on the evolution of strains for the natural recombination of the human coronavirus-HKU1 and the human coronavirus-OC43, as well as animal coronaviruses, as well as in coronavirus similar to severe acute respiratory syndrome in bats and HKU9 in bats.
Virus interaction with the host associated with transmission
In addition to the three viral factors mentioned above, the viral interaction with the host receptor is another key factor in the transmission between species.
In this regard, we address the reconstruction of SARS-CoV as a typical example, which has also shown evidence of positive selection during interspecies transmission incidents.
Based on the comparative analysis between isolated samples from SARS-CoV in humans and civet cats, SARS-CoV is believed to be undergoing rapid adaptation in different hostesses, particularly through mutations that occur in receptor binding to S protein.
In general, the receptor binding range of the coronavirus S-protein interacts with the cellular receptor and is densely selected through the antibody response of the host.
In SARS-CoV, the receptor binding field in amino acids occurs from 318 to 510 in S-1, which is associated with the angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 as well as auxiliary receptors to enter the virus.
SARS-CoV Receptors are able to identify auxiliary angiotensin-converting enzyme receptors in different animals, including bats, civet cat, mouse, and raccoon dogs, allowing the virus to be transmitted between species.
In fact, only the residues of 6 amino acids were observed that were different from viral isolated samples from humans and civet cats in the field of receptor binding, four of which are found in the receptor correlation sequence pattern to interact with the angiotensin-converting enzyme receptor.
Mutations K479N and S487T are found in SARS-CoV receptor binding in a civet cat, which can increase the likelihood that the spike protein will react in the receptor of the angiotensin-moving enzyme 2.
In other words, these two types of amino acid substitutes may be critical to viral adaptation in humans.
It should be noted that SARS-COV-2 shares the same cellular receptor found at SARS-CoV.
There is a 30% difference between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV in the S-1 unit of S-Protein, which implicitly suggests that the probability of binding in the angiotensin-conversing enzyme of human angiotensin-2 has changed.
Already, an ultra-cold electron microscope study has indicated that there is a 10 to 20 - twice higher probability of this association compared to that occurring between the angiotensin-convergent enzyme of human angiotensin-2 and the SV SARS-COV protein.
It’s also interesting to determine if any other type of auxiliary receptors may be necessary for SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
It is also interesting that the human coronavirus-NL63 is also associated with the angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2 but in a different part of the S-protein.
There are many other human coronavirus receptors, such as an HCV-229E protein analyzer, and an induced 9-O cyalic acid for the human coronavirus-OC43.
They may also be responsible for the successful adaptation of these coronaviruses to humans after the transition between species from their animal hosts.
In addition to cellular receptors, as a result of interspecies transmission of human coronaviruses, other factors of dependency and constraints in the host are also controlled.
The ramification of these hospital proteins between humans and natural repositories hosts for coronaviruses, such as bats, humps, and rodents, may be an impediment to the transition between species.
Human coronaviruses need to control and impaire their host dependencies to carry out a successful intertype transmission.
In this regard, the molecular determinants in this important space for the interaction of the virus with the hoster remain detectable and determine characteristics.
Extensive unbiased examination on the genome can be used to look at the dependency factors and host constraints of SARS-CoV-2 using cutting-edge CRISPR technology.
Emergence of emerging human coronaviruses: returning to the starting point
The diversity of coronaviruses in bats presents abundant opportunities for the emergence of emerging human coronaviruses.
Based on this logic, coronaviruses in bats form a genetic compound for human coronaviruses.
In addition, the rapid mutation and genetic recombination are also driving the development of human coronavirus and represent two important points in this process.
For example, the acquisition or loss of emerging protein encoding genes has the potential to significantly modify viral phenotypes.
Among SARS-CoV’s regulatory proteins, ORF8 was thought to be important in adapting to humans, as SARS-CoV associated with viruses in bats was isolated but found to encrypt ORF8’s disparate proteins.
It was found that the 29 properties to delete SARS-COV nucleotide were found to be found in isolated strains at the beginning of the human epidemic.
This deletion has divided ORF8 to ORF8a and ORF8b, and is thought to be an adaptive mutation that enhances the switching of hosters.
In addition, SARS-CoV has a history of possible recombination through alpha and gamma coronavirus strains, where a large number of small recombination areas have been identified in RNA-based RNA polymerase.
Recombination sites have also been identified in nsp9, most nsp10, and parts of nsp14.
In a similar way, research has shown that the MERS-CoV pandemic has seen reconstructions between different dynasties, which have taken place in Saudi Arabia’s only hummes.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, recombinations have also been observed in other human coronaviruses, where human coronaviruses have been combined with other animal coronaviruses in their non-constructive genes.
It should be noted that artificial selection can also help unintended changes in the viral genome, most of which are most likely caused by the removal of viruses from selective stresses, such as what happens with the host’s immune system.
Loss of full-length ORF4 protein in the human coronavirus 229E phenotype strain due to the deletion of two nucleotides is an example of these effects.
Although ORF4 can be properly observed in camel and bat viruses associated with HIV-229E, coronavirus alpha infesting albeca shows one nucleotide stitches, which leads to the mutation of the underlining frame.
Last but not least, the development of new coronaviruses is also driven by selective pressure in their repository hostesses.
Unsymptomatic or mildly symptomatic cases have been detected only when bats are infected with coronaviruses, indicating mutual adjustment between coronaviruses and bats.
It showed that bats appropriately adapted to anatomical and physiological coronaviruses.
For example, impaired activation of the pro-inflammatory response in bats has effectively diminished pathogens provoked by coronaviruses.
In addition, natural lethal cellular activity was suppressed in bats due to an increase in NKG2/CD94 and a decrease in the expression level of the molecules of the main Histological Compassion Compatibility Composites Class I.
In addition, the elevated level of reactive oxygen compounds (reactive oxygen compounds) arising from the high metabolic activity of bats can lead to both the suppression of coronavirus coronavirus replication and the effect of correction by the external ribonucliase enzyme, thus providing selective pressure for the emergence of highly pathogenic strains of the virus when a new host is infected.
Additional disease-causing strains of coronavirus may originate by recombination, resulting in the acquisition of new proteins or protein properties to adapt to the host.
Based on this, it’s no coincidence that three human coronaviruses have emerged over the past two decades.
Coronaviruses are not pathogenic or cause mild symptoms in repository hostesses such as bats and camels.
They replicate aggressively without triggering a strong immune response from the host.
Here are the secrets that explain the cause of the vision of carriers of the virus who do not show symptoms and the causes of acute cases of human infection.
Acute symptoms are mainly caused by an overactive immune response and cytokine, where the stronger the immune response, the sharper the pneumonia.
In contrast, in the case of unsymptomatic carriers, the immune response is disconnected from coronavirus coronavirus.
A strategy similar to the detachment of the immune response may have beneficial effects in anti-SARS-CoV-2 therapy.
Interferon's response is particularly strong in bats.
Thus, the ingestion of type I can be used at least in the first stage of SARS-CoV-2 infection in humans.
In addition, the activation of the inflammatory particle of Creobaerin NLRP3 in bats is impaired.
In this rational logic, inhibition of the inflammatory particle of Cryopyrene NLRP3 using the inhibitor MCC950 is useful in the treatment of COVID-19.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 follows the general idea by which SARS-CoV and Meres-Cove originated.
At a time when it was discovered that the coronavirus beta in bats involved SARS-CoV in the homogeneity of nucleotide by 95%, there was also the coronavirus in bats sharing 96% of the homogeneity of nucleotide with SARS-CoV-2.
While it was discovered that civet cats and other animals in the market were harboring viruses similar to SARS-CoV did not detect SARS-CoV-2's intermediary direct hosts.
Beta coronaviruses have been detected in the pangolitón, which are highly homogeneous with SARS-CoV-2, suggesting that pangolins may be an intermediate hospital, or that beta coronaviruses in the pangolin have contributed to genetic fragments, leading to the final version of SARS-CoV-2.
Although questions are still on the table, there is no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 is man-made, neither intentionally nor by chance.
Coronaviruses have returned to the focus of attention due to the start of the recent outbreak of SARS-CoV-2.
The coronavirus study in bats and other animals has dramatically changed our idea about the importance of the origins of zoonotic diseases and animal repositories for human coronaviruses in human transmission.
Conclusive evidence has showned that SARS-CoV, MERIS-CoV and SARS-COV-2 all trace their origin to the bat and passed on to humans through intermediate hosts.
Based on the fact that SARS-CoV infection originated from the communication between humans and civet cats in the markets, the closure of fresh product markets and the killing of civet cats were supposed to effectively end the SARS epidemic.
In the same mental sense, pyramid-eaters should be disposed of from fresh product markets to prevent the transmission of animal-oring disease, in the light of the discovery of multiple genealogy of beta-coronaviruses in the pangolin, which are very close to SARS-CoV-2.
However, whether or not SARS-COV-2 is transmitted to humans, as well as, no matter how it travels, through pangolins and other mammals, its transmission remains a subject that needs to be explained in future research.
On the other hand, Meres-Cove was found in the only camel hump for a long time.
These camels are an important means of transportation in addition to being a major source of meat, milk, leather, and wool products for local citizens.
It’s widespread throughout the Middle East and Africa region.
That’s why it’s impossible to sacrifice all the camels to control MERS, similar to what officials have done in China’s wildlife markets to prevent the spread of SARS-CoV and SARS-COV-2.
To stop repeated outbreaks of MERS, a comprehensive approach should be taken to develop effective MERS-CoV vaccines, as well as other infection control measures.
Unable to completely eliminate these viruses, new genetic patterns may arise and cause outbreaks.
A variety of coronaviruses associated with zoonotic diseases are found in wildlife.
In particular, coronaviruses are very diverse in bats that have the potential to cause zoonotic diseases.
There are many opportunities for the emergence and reconstruction of coronaviruses associated with zoonotic diseases, which leads to the emergence of new coronaviruses more transmissible and/or deadly in humans in the future.
In some places in China, the culture of eating wild animals should be abandoned to reduce unnecessary communication between humans and animals.
In light of the adversities experienced by humanity due to SARS, MERS and COVID-19, an appropriate preparedness and response plan should be prepared.
The fact suggests that many viruses have taken their place on the planet for a very long time.
They live in their own natural depots until they have an opportunity to spread and multiply.
Although bats carry many characteristics that suggest the spread of viruses, the chance of human contact with bats and other wild animal species can be reduced if humans adopt a culture that obliges them to stay away from them.
Continuous monitoring of mammals is a necessary measure to reach a better understanding of the coronavirus environment and their natural hosts, which proves its usefulness in the prevention of their transmission from animals to humans and the prevention of future outbreaks.
Finally, the best way to prevent viral diseases of animal origin is to keep humans away from the ecological positions of natural reservoirs of animal-causing viruses.
The mystery of the origin of the animal disease of SARS-CoV-2 is still surrounded by many questions that need to be answered.
First, if bats transmit the SARS-CoV-2-origin virus to the pangolin, it is important to know the conditions created for the participation of bats and palantropians of the same ecological habitat.
Secondly, if bats play a more direct role in the transmission of the disease to humans, then the way of human contact should be determined.
Thirdly, if a third type of mammal performs the role of a real intermediate host, then it should be clarified how it interacts with different species, including humans, bats, and squamous ant eaters.
Finally, since many mammals, including pets, may be suspected of carrying SARS-CoV-2, both monitoring and experimental infections should be performed.
Whether a bat host, squamous anthropax or other mammals, SARS-CoV-2 or the faction viruses that originate from them are almost identical in their future natural hosts are expected to be identified.
Continuing research in this area will reveal the evolutionary trajectory of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, with important measures to prevent and control COVID-19 in humans.
‘Suspected Case’ and ‘Confirmed Case’ Criterion to Update When Diagnosing COVID-19
On February 6, 2020, our team published a rapid guidance on the diagnosis and treatment of the novel coronavirus (2019) infection, and this guidance provided our experience and included appropriate reference information to combat the pandemic globally.
Although COVID-19 is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge are gradually increasing based on the results of ongoing research and clinical practice experiments; diagnosis and treatment strategies are constantly updated.
In this speech, we responded to an incoming comment on our guidance and presented the latest diagnostic criteria of “suspected case” and “confirmed case,” in accordance with the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Guidelines (Issuance VII) issued by the National Health Commission of the People’s Republic of China.
In December 2019, the novel coronavirus 2019 (the novel coronavirus-2019), which is now officially known as Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and the virus has been dubbed the coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) described COVID-19 as a pandemic.
To combat SARS-CoV-2 infection, our team quickly drafted and published it online in Military Medical Research on February 6, 2020.
He's been very interested since it was published.
Please note that although COVID-19 is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge are gradually increasing based on ongoing research findings and clinical practice experiences; diagnosis and treatment strategies are also constantly updated.
For example, the COVID-19 Diagnostics and Treatment Guidances issued by the National Health Commission of the People’s Republic of China (/http://www.nhc.gov.cn), between January 16, 2020 and March 3, 2020, issued seven editions in total with some contexts fundamentally changing.
Our indicative directive has now received a comment from Zhou and others, who have made a simple suggestion for grading monitoring based on the clinical trial.
Their efforts have added a new guide to our guidance as well as becoming a valuable reference for this pandemic around the world.
We support their great effort and express our appreciation.
However, their work also needs to be updated in accordance with the latest COVID-19 Diagnostics and Treatment Guidelines (Philanthropic 7th Edition) and recent studies.
According to the seventh edition (March 3, 2020), the confirmation of the suspected case needs to combine any of the characteristics of the epidemiological history and two elements of the clinical manifestations to reach a comprehensive analysis, or needs to verify three elements of clinical manifestations in the absence of a clear epidemiological history:
Epidemiological history: (1) a date that includes travel to Wuhan City and surrounding areas, or other communities where COVID-19 cases have been reported to occur, or stay, during the last 14 days before the onset of symptoms; (2) a date that includes contact with cases of SARS-CoV-2 infection (with the positive results of a DNA test); (3) a history that includes communication with patients with fever or respiratory symptoms from Wuhan city, surroundings or other communities where COVID-19 cases have been reported during the last 14 days before the onset of symptoms; and (4) a date that includes communication with a cluster of confirmed cases (2 of one or more cases of fever and/or respiratory symptom that occurred during 2 weeks in small areas, such as home, office, classroom, and so on).
Clinical manifestations: (1) fever and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) with the properties that are confirmed by the imaging examination of the COVID-19 infection; (3) the total number of white blood cells shows a normal, little or low level of early stage lymphocytosis.
Diagnosis of the confirmed case on a suspected case involving any of the evidence for pathogens or serumal reactions should be based on the following: (1) a positive PCR test result for SARS-CoV-2; (2) high homogeneity of viral genome sequencing entirely with the characteristics of known emerging coronaviruses; (3) positive IgM antibody test results and SARS-CoV-2 specific antibody IgG in serum; IgG specific antibody of SARS-CoV-2 has changed from negative to positive, or the high calibration in the recovery stage is equal to or more than four times that level in the acute stage.
We believe that a real-time DNA reaction test in the respiratory tract or blood samples was added to the second version (January 18, 2020) and the third (January 22, 2020).
The discovery of pathogens in the blood sample was added to the fourth (January 27, 2020) and fifth (February 8, 2020) version; then the evidence for servoidal reactions was added to the seventh version.
These modifications were based on the researchers’ continuous work to reach the design of an exemplary DNA detection toolkit for rapid diagnosis, as well as samples from the respiratory trajectory, including blood samples, which led to an increase in the availability of various samples, and to support the transfer of the positive result of the qualitative antibody to the standards of confirmed cases.
In addition, there is growing evidence to be cautious when dealing with patients with unusual symptoms and unsymptomatic conditions.
Therefore, you should update the data flow chart content of Zhou and others, where they classified a person free of clinical symptoms in a “low risk” score.
A grading monitoring system also needs to be validated in practice and additional clinical studies.
In conclusion, we hope to find more direct evidence and we invite readers to provide us with their comments.
To diagnose the ‘suspected case’ and ‘confirmed case’, we call for the latest guidelines issued by home countries to be tracked and adhered to.
Our team will also update our guidance in a timely manner to provide assistance.
Bangladesh records five new deaths from COVID-19, the highest daily rate
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new deaths from COVID-19 that day.
That's the highest number of deaths in a single day because of the virus.
The Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research (IEDCR) in Bangladesh reported that, as of yesterday, the number of registered cases had reached 114 active cases and 33 recoveries residing at home.
There have been 17 deaths.
In an online press conference, the director of the Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research, Dr. Mirgadi Sabrina Flora, said that the deaths included four males and one female.
According to Dr. Mirgadi, there were two cases over the age of 60, two cases between 51 and 60, and one case between 41 and 50 years old.
She also said that two of the deaths were from Dhaka.
The World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic on March 11.
An official at an Anadolu hospital, a local news agency, said one of the deceased was Jalal Saif al-Rahman, director of the Bangladesh Anti-Corruption Commission, who was receiving care at Kuwait Maitri Hospital.
Bangladesh’s Minister of Land and Bridge Transport, Obaid al-Qadir, said in an online video statement on Saturday that public transport would be halted longer than initially planned until Saturday.
Disruption of public transport initially began on March 26 and was scheduled to end on Saturday, April 4.
Substantities are still allowed to be transported: medicinal products, fuel and food.
The first cases of COVID-19 were recorded in Bangladesh on March 8, for two people who returned from Italy, as well as the wife of one of them.
All three have recovered by March 19.
SARS-CoV-2 infections have exceeded one million worldwide
Data released by Johns Hopkins University on Thursday indicated that the total number of cases of severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus (SARS-CoV-2) has exceeded one million worldwide.
Coronavirus COVID-19 has caused at least 52,000 deaths.
This dangerous development came on the same day that Malawi confirmed its first coronavirus infections, and the first associated death was recorded in Zambia.
North Korea has claimed that, from Thursday, it is one of the few countries that is still free of coronavirus infections.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has reported 105,1635 confirmed cases, including 79,332 cases in the 24 hours before 10 a.m
More than 244 thousand cases of coronavirus have been recorded in the United States of America, involving at least 5900 deaths.
CBS News, citing Johns Hopkins University data, reported more than 1000 deaths in the United States on Wednesday caused by coronavirus infection.
Countries around the world have announced stricter measures to prevent the spread of the disease.
Mr. Sergey Sobyanin, the mayor of Moscow, announced on Thursday the extension of the closure of the city until 1 May.
At the national level, President Vladimir Putin announced that salaries would continue to be paid to Russians without going to work until April 30.
Portugal’s parliament voted to extend the national state of emergency for 15 days, with 215 votes approved, 10 abstentions, and one vote opposition.
Saudi Arabia extended the curfew in the cities of Mecca and Medina to all day, previously from 3 p.m. to 6 a.m.
Thailand plans to impose a curfew from 10 p.m. to 4 a.m.
The Governor of Ohio, Mr. Mike De Wayne, has announced that the state has extended the stay-at-home order until May 1.
Australia stores lower the limits on buying toilet paper for each transaction
On Saturday evening and on Sunday, Australian supermarket chains Wall Worths and Coles tightened restrictions on buying toilet paper to be reduced to two rolls from Wal-Worth Stores and one roll of Coles stores per transaction in all branches across the country.
Aldé stores also identified one scroll on Monday.
These purchase restrictions have been posted as messages at payment points in store chains and on their Facebook pages.
Buyers have reportedly stockpiled it due to COVID-19-related concerns in case people need self-isolation.
On Wednesday, Wall Worths stores approved restrictions on the delivery of toilet paper to homes, becoming one roll for each purchase order.
These changes came after the previous restrictions approved by Wall Worths stores on March 4 and Coles stores on March 5 by allowing the purchase of only four rolls per transaction.
Cole’s stores said in a media statement issued on March 8 that despite the application of the four-scroll restrictions, “many stores still sell everything they have within one hour of its receipt,” and described the order as “unprecedented,” and Aldy’s stores in a Facebook post on Tuesday called it “unpredictable.”
Sales increased “significantly” in the past week, according to a spokesman for Wall Worths stores.
Last week’s Costco store in Canberra also determined that the amount allowed to be purchased was two rolls.
To ease the shortfalls, Colz stores ordered larger rolls from suppliers and increased the frequency of delivery, and Wallworths stores ordered additional inventory, while Aldi stores made their stock available for the special offers scheduled for Wednesday early.
Russell Zimmermann, executive director of the Australian Retailers Association, said retailers were trying to increase inventory, but local council restrictions on the timing of truck delivery made it difficult.
Production costs are expected to rise, as suppliers try to meet demand, with few special offers.
On Tuesday, Aldi stores announced that as a result of the early availability of inventory, some stores will not be able to provide Wednesday’s special offer.
In a report to News.com.au, a retail expert at the Queensland University of Technology, Dr. Gary Mortemmer reported that stores fill stores every night.
He pointed out that toilet paper is a large commodity, which leads to a decrease in the amount of inventory in terms of numbers, and when sold in full, leaves large empty spaces on the shelves, which enhances the feeling of inferiority.
Russell Zimmerman stated, according to ABC News, that: “Colz and Wal-Worths stores have the view that if a lot of goods are available on shelves, and if products such as toilet rolls and sterilizers can be purchased in large quantities, this may reduce panic.”
Who Gives a Crap, which produces recycled toilet paper, said on Wednesday that its stock had run out.
According to the News.com.au report, Cumberley-Clark, which produces Kleinx toilet napkins, and Solaris Pepper, which produces Sorpent wipes, confirmed that they are working 24/7 to continue to provide their products.
Domain.com, a real estate website, noted that some real estate vendors offer free toilet paper to the first bidder at auctions in Melbourne, while fewer auctions were held as buyers stopped working on the long holiday of Labor Day.
Thursday’s edition of NT News, published daily in the city of Darwin, featured eight-page advertisements made so that they could be cut and used as toilet paper.
Stores were initially hesitant about imposing restrictions, according to a report released by ABC Australia on March 3, with stores saying they had no plans to impose restrictions on purchases.
Russell Zimmermann added that there is also a high demand for other products, including masks, sterilizers, dried goods, hand wash and flour.
In the same way, outside Australia, it was observed on Sunday evening that a British online store named Okadó identifies the purchases of Toilet paper Andrès with two blocks from 12 rolls.
The World Health Organization (WHO) declares COVID-19 a pandemic
The World Health Organization (WHO) announced on Wednesday that the ongoing outbreak of COVID-19, a disease caused by the severe acute respiratory syndrome-related coronavirus (CPS-linked coronavirus), commonly known as SARS-CoV-2, has reached the level of the pandemic.
Although the word ‘pandemic’ refers only to the extent of the spread of the disease, and not to the seriousness of specific cases, the World Health Organization (WHO) has noted the need for governments to take the necessary measures:
“All countries can still change the trajectory of this epidemic.
The Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO), Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, said:  �If countries detect, test, treat and isolate people, track their cases and mobilize them for confrontation.’
“We’re all very concerned about the alarming prevalence and risk levels as well as the alarming levels of inaction.”
According to Dr. Tom Frídín, former director of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, this pandemic is “unprecedented.”
In comments published by CNN in February, he said, “The trajectory of any other respiratory virus has not been tracked from the onset to the ongoing global spread, unlike influenza.”
Ghebreyesus expressed a similar view, saying: “We have never been exposed to a pandemic caused by coronaviruses.”
“We’ve never seen a pandemic that can be controlled at the same time.”
The new situation is described as a pandemic following the WHO’s decision in January to declare the outbreak a public health emergency of international concern.
Dr. Anthony Fauci, director of the U.S. National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, said of the outbreak: “The bottom line is that things are going to get worse.”
There are at least 126,000 COVID-19 cases worldwide from Thursday, and more than 4,600 deaths.
Coronavirus Pandemic 2019-20 is an ongoing COVID-19 pandemic, caused by Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
An outbreak was identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019, and was declared a public health emergency of international concern on January 30, 2020, and was recognized as a pandemic on March 11, 2020.
As of April 10, 2020, about 1.61 million COVID-19 cases have been reported in 210 countries and territories, resulting in about 97,000 deaths.
About 364,000 people recovered.
China’s mortality rate was estimated at 4%, compared to 13.04% in Algeria and 0.80% in New Zealand.
Common symptoms include fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Complications may include pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The period between exposure and the appearance of symptoms is usually about five days, but may range from two to fourteen days.
There is no known vaccine or specific antiretroviral treatment.
The primary treatment is a supportive and symptomatic treatment. Recommended preventive measures include hand washing, covering the mouth when coughing, maintaining interpersonal spacing, monitoring, and self-isolation of those suspected of being infected.
Authorities around the world are responding to this risk of travel restrictions, quarantines, curfews, workplace risk controls and the closure of facilities.
This pandemic led to severe global social and economic turmoil, the postponement or cancellation of sporting, religious, political and cultural events, and widespread supply shortages exacerbated by buying panic.
Schools and universities were closed either nationally or domestically in 193 countries, affecting about 99.4 percent of the world’s student population.
Misinformation about the virus has spread online, and there have been incidents of xenophobia and discrimination against Chinese, and others of Eastern and South-Asian origin and appearance, and other areas where there have been major cases of the virus.
As a result of the reduction in travel and closure rates for heavy industries, there has been a reduction in air pollution and carbon emissions.
Health authorities in the city of Wuhan (the capital of Hubei Province), China, reported a series of cases of pneumonia for an unknown cause on 31 December 2019, and the investigation began in early January 2020.
Cases are mostly related to the Wuhan seafood market for wholesale sale, so it is believed that the virus has animal origin.
The virus that caused the outbreak is known as SARS-CoV-2, a newly discovered virus closely linked to coronaviruses in bats, coronaviruses in pangolic anthropies, and SARS-CoV. It was later discovered that the first symptomatic person had contracted the disease on 1 December 2019, and this person did not have a clear contact with the wet market group that was later discovered.
Among the range of early cases reported in December 2019, two-thirds were found to be related to the market.
On March 13, 2020, an unverified report published by the South China Morning Newspaper noted that a 55-year-old case from Hubei province, probably the first. On February 26, 2020, the World Health Organization reported that despite the decline in new reported cases in China, it suddenly rose in Italy, Iran, and South Korea, where the number of new cases outside China exceeded the number of new cases inside it for the first time.
There may be a significant lack of reporting of cases, especially among those with mild symptoms.
By 26 February, relatively few cases had been reported among young people, with 19-year-old and lower-year-olds accounting for 2.4 per cent of cases around the world. The UK’s chief scientific adviser, Patrick Valance, estimated that 60% of Britons needed to be infected before effective herd immunity.
Cases indicate the number of people who have undergone a COVID-19 test, and have been confirmed in accordance with official protocols.
As of 23 March, no country has tested more than 3% of its population, and many countries have had official policies regarding the lack of testing for only those with mild symptoms, such as Italy, the Netherlands, Spain and Switzerland.
A study published on March 16 found that an estimated 86 percent of COVID-19 cases had not been detected in China as of January 23, and that these unregistered infections were the source of infection for 79 percent of recorded cases.
A statistical analysis published on March 30 estimated that the number of infections in Italy was much higher than the recorded cases.
Preliminary estimates of the basic reproduction number (R0) for COVID-19 ranged from 1.4 to 2.4.
A study published by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention concluded it could be 5.7.
Most people with COVID-19 are recovering.
For those who don’t recover, the period from the deterioration of symptoms to death takes between 6 and 41 days, the most common of which is 14 days.
As of 10 April 2020, COVID-19 has caused about 97,000 deaths.
Approximately 80% of deaths were concentrated in China from 5 February in those over the age of 60, and 75% had pre-existing medical conditions, including cardiovascular disease and diabetes.
The real number of deaths from COVID-19 may be much higher, because it may not include people who die without being tested – for example; at home, in nursing homes, and so on.
Partial data in Italy revealed that the number of excess deaths during the pandemic exceeded the official number of Covid deaths recorded with a 4-5 x laboratory.
A spokeswoman for the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) acknowledged that “we know that [the number of deaths announced] reflects an understatement,” a statement supported by narrative reports indicating inaccuracy in the U.S
The first death was outside mainland China on February 1 in the Philippines, and the first death was non-Asia in France on 14 February.
By 28 February, more than a dozen deaths had been recorded outside mainland China in Iran, South Korea and Italy.
By 13 March, more than forty countries and territories had reported deaths, on all continents except Antarctica.
These numbers vary by region and over time, and are affected by the size of the tests, the quality of the health care system, treatment options and the time that has fallen since the outbreak began, and the characteristics of the population such as age, gender, and public health. The mortality-to-injury ratio reflects the number of deaths divided by the number of diagnosed cases over a certain period of time.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, the global casualty mortality rate is 6.0% (97,039/1,617,204) as of April 10, 2020.
The number varies depending on the region.
In China, estimates of mortality from infections fell from 17.3% (for those who developed symptoms from 1 to 10 January 2020) to 0.7% (for those who showed symptoms after 1 February 2020). Other measures point to the case mortality rate (CFR), which reflects the percentage of infected people who have died from the disease, and the infection mortality rate (IFR), which reflects the percentage of those who have tested positive and those who have not been infected.
These statistics do not have a time frame and track a specific population from injury to the fate of the condition.
A number of academics tried to calculate these numbers for certain populations.
The University of Oxford’s Evidence-Based Medical Center estimates that the pandemic’s infection rate as a whole is between 0.1% and 0.39%.
The highest estimate of this range corresponds to the results of the first randomized COVID-19 test in Germany, and with a statistical study that analyzes the impact of the test on the estimates of case mortality.
The World Health Organization (WHO) confirms the possibility of controlling the pandemic.
The peak of the outbreak and its maximum duration are unconfirmed and may vary depending on the location.
Massage Bonnie of Pennsylvania State University stated: “Infectious disease outbreaks that are left uncontrollable usually reach peak and begin to decline afterwards when the disease does not find available hosts.
“But it’s almost impossible to reach any logical expectation right now about the timing of it.”
China's chief medical adviser Zhong Nanshan said it "could end by June" if all countries could be stimulated to follow World Health Organization (WHO) advice on measures to stop the spread of the virus.
On 17 March, Adam Kutcharsky of the London School of Hygienic and Tropical Medicine stated that SARS-CoV-2 "would be prevalent, possibly for a year or two."
According to a study conducted by the Imperial College of London undersered by Neil Ferguson, we will need to rely on physical distancing and other measures "until a vaccine becomes available (perhaps after 18 months or more)."
William Schaffner, from Vanderbilt University, said, "I don't think this coronavirus is likely to disappear completely — because it's easily transmissible," and it might "turn into a seasonal disease — so that it comes back every year."
The ferocity of return will depend on collective immunity and mutation.
Symptoms of COVID-19 may be relatively indeterminative and may not show symptoms in infected people.
The two most common symptoms are fever (88%) and dry cough (68%).
Less common symptoms include fatigue, respiratory phlegm excretion, loss of sense of smell, shortness of breathing, muscle and joint pain, sore throat, headache, chills, vomiting, pulp, diarrhea or cyanosis. The World Health Organization (WHO) confirms that approximately one in six people suffers severely from the disease and has difficulty breathing.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) in the United States counted emergency symptoms of difficulty breathing, persistent chest pain or pressure, sudden confusion, difficulty waking up, and cyanosis of the face or lips. Immediate medical attention is recommended if these symptoms appear. The exacerbation of the development of the disease may lead to severe pneumonia, acute shortness of breath syndrome, sepsis, septic shock, and death.
There may be no symptoms in some of the infected people, who do not have clinical symptoms, but the results of the tests confirm their infection, so the specialists provided advice that people in contact with the confirmed cases should be monitored and examined closely to exclude the infection.
Chinese estimates of asymptomatic cases range from a few to 44%.
The usual incubation period (the period between infection and the onset of symptoms) ranges between one and 14 days; it is often five days. As an example of confusion, those with COVID-19 who initially lost their sense of smell were estimated at 30% and recently reduced to 15%.
Some details about how the disease has spread are still under consideration.
The disease is mainly believed to be prevalent during mixing or through splashing when coughing, sneezing, or talking, when mixing at a distance of 1 to 2 meters (3 to 6 feet).
Studies have shown that a cough without covering the mouth may result in a splatter for a distance of 4.5 meters (15 feet) to 8.2 meters (27 feet).
Some argue that the virus may also be transmitted by spray stuck in the air for a long time, which may be produced while talking. It may also produce a respiratory spray during exhalation, including when talking, even though the virus is not generally transmitted by air.
Spray may fall on the mouths or noses of nearby people or inhale into the lungs.
Some medical procedures such as intubation and CPR may cause respiratory secretions to be released in the form of a spray and then cause the spread through the air.
It may also spread when one touches a contaminated surface, including the skin, and then touches one’s eyes, nose, or mouth.
Although there are concerns about the possibility of spreading from feces to the mouth, it is believed that risk is limited.
The Chinese government has denied the possibility that SARS-CoV-2 will be transmitted from feces to the mouth. The virus is more contagious during the first three days of the onset of symptoms, although it may spread before the onset of any symptoms and during later stages of the disease.
People have tested positive for the disease up to three days before the onset of symptoms, indicating the possibility of transmission before the appearance of severe symptoms.
There are only a few reports of asymptomatic, laboratory-confirmed cases, but asymptomatic transmission has been identified by some countries during contact tracing surveys.
The European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDCC) asserts that despite some ambiguity about how easily the disease spreads, one person can encroach from two to three other people in general.
Specifically, it has been found that the virus can be detected for up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and 304 stainless steel, one day on cardboard, and up to four hours on copper.
However, this varies depending on the humidity and temperature. Pets and other animals have been confirmed with COVID-19.
There’s no evidence that animals can transmit the virus to humans, yet British authorities are advised to wash their hands after contact with animals, such as in contact with other surfaces that may have been touched by an infested person.
Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is an emerging virus, first isolated from three people who were in contact with a group of cases of acute respiratory disease in Wuhan.
SARS-CoV-Virus-2 shares all of its properties with coronaviruses found in nature. Outside the human body, domestic soap kills the virus, melting its protective membrane. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-CoV virus.
It's believed to have animal origin.
Genetic analysis has revealed that coronavirus genetically meets the genuse of beta coronavirus, and falls under the subspecies of sarpy coronavirus (Strain B) as well as two strains originating from bats.
It corresponds to 96% at the level of the entire genome with other samples of coronaviruses in bats (Bat-Cov-Rat G-13)
In February 2020, Chinese researchers discovered that there was only one difference in amino acids in certain parts of the genomic sequences between viruses originated by pangolins and viruses originating from humans.
Comparison of the entire genome so far has resulted in the discovery of a maximum of 92 per ceremonies of coronavirus genetic material for squamous ants and SARS-COV-2, an insufficient percentage to prove that pangolin is the mediating breadwinner of this virus.
Infection with the virus can be temporarily diagnosed based on symptoms, although confirmation is ultimately done by reversible polymerase chain reaction (RRT-PCR) of infected secretions or computed tomography.
According to a study in Wuhan that compares the interaction of polymerase and computed tomography indicates that computed tomography is more sensitive than polymerase chain reaction, although it is less accurate, with many of its imaging properties involved with pneumonia and other pathological stages.
As of March 2020, the U.S. College of Radiography recommends “not to use computed tomography to detect COVID-19 or consider it a preliminary examination for the diagnosis of the disease.”
The World Health Organization (WHO) has published several RNA testing protocols for SARS-CoV-2 for the first time on 17 January.
The test uses real-time retraction polymerase chain reaction (RRT-PCR).
The test can be carried out on samples from the respiratory or blood tract.
Results are generally available within a few hours to days.
This test is generally carried out by a nasopharyngeal swab, and a swab from the throat can also be used. A number of laboratories and companies are developing serological tests that detect antibodies.
As of April 6, 2020, none of them have been proven accurate enough to be widely used.
A serological test developed by Selex has been certified for emergency use in the United States only in accredited laboratories.
Imaging characteristics of radiological images and computed tomography (CT) for people with symptoms of the diseast include asymmetric peripheral octars such as fog of scattered glass and confusing crystal infiltration.
The Italian Radiation Society collects an international online database of imaging results for confirmed cases.
Polymerase chain reaction radiational imaging is a limited-precision procedure in relation to the detection of COVID-19, due to its interference with other diseases such as adenovirus infection.
A large study in China compared the results of chest computed tomography and polymerase chain reaction and proved that although the imaging is less accurate in terms of proving the injury, it is faster and more sensitive, suggesting that it is considered an infested screening tool.
Synthetic synthesized neural networks based on artificial intelligence have been developed to detect virus imaging properties in both radiographic images and computed tomography.
Strategies to prevent the transmission of the disease include maintaining good personal hygiene in general, washing hands, avoiding contact with the eyes, nose or mouth with unwashed hands, and using a handkerchief when coughing or sneezing and then throwing it directly into the trash.
Those who carry the infection have already been advised to wear a medical mask in public.
Physical distancing measures are also recommended to prevent transmission. Several governments have banned non-essential travel from countries and regions affected by the outbreak to and from or advised not to travel.
However, the virus has reached the stage of spreading throughout society in large parts of the world.
This means that the virus spreads within communities, and some individuals don’t know where or how they’ve been infected. Health caregivers, who take care of a person likely to be infected, are advised to use considerable measures, mixing precautions and eye protection.
The use of mobile location data by governments has raised concerns about privacy, with Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations issuing a statement calling for the restriction of this type of surveillance.
As of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen specialized groups have worked to find privacy-friendly solutions, such as using Bluetooth to record the user’s proximity to other mobile phones.
Users then receive a message if they approach someone who has tested positive for COVID-19. Misconceptions about how to prevent an infection are promoted; for example, washing the nose and gargling with mouthwash is not a viable solution.
There is still no COVID-19 vaccine, although many organizations are working to produce a COVID-19 vaccine.
It is advisable to wash your hands to prevent the spread of the disease.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that people wash their hands frequently with soap and water for at least twenty seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when dirt appears on their hands, before eating, and after emptying the nose, coughing or sneezing.
That’s because household soap kills the virus outside the body, destroying its protective membrane.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention has also recommended the use of a hand sanitizer that contains at least 60% alcohol when water and soap are not available.
The World Health Organization (WHO) advises people to avoid touching the eyes, nose or mouth with unwashed hands.
Surfaces can be disinfected using several disinfectants (within one minute of applying disinfectation to the surface of stainless steel), including ethanol 62–71%, isopropanol 50-100% and sodium hypochlorite 0.1%, hydrogen peroxide 0.5%, and iodopopidone 0.2–7.5%.
Other disinfectants, such as benzalconium chloride and chlorhexicidine gluconate, are found less effectively.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that when a person is suspected of having COVID or is confirmed to be infected in a facility such as an office or care center, all places such as offices, bathrooms, shared spaces and shared electronic devices such as tablets, touchscreens, keyboards, remote controls and ATMs used by the patient should be sterilized.
Health organizations recommended that people cover their mouths and noses when coughing or sneezing, either bending the elbow and placing it in front of the mouth and nose, or by using a handkerchief and disposing of any napkins immediately.
Possibly infected with the use of medical masks is recommended, as the wearing of the mask reduces the size of the respiratory spray and the distance it travels when scattered due to talking, sneezing and coughing.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has instructed on the timing and use of masks.
According to Stephen Griffin, a virologist at the University of Leeds, “Wearing a mask can reduce [persons]’s desire to touch their faces, which is a major source of infection in the event that their hands are not properly cleaned.”
The World Health Organization (WHO) has recommended that healthy people should not wear masks unless they are at high risk of infection, such as those with COVID-19 care, although it also acknowledges that wearing masks may help people avoid touching their faces.
Several countries have begun to encourage ordinary people to wear face masks.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends wearing a non-medical face mask made of cloth. China specifically recommended healthy masks that can be disposed of, especially in the case of contact with people (1 meter (3 feet) or less) directly.
Hong Kong recommends wearing a medical mask when using public transport or staying in crowded places.
Thai health officials encourage individuals to make cloth masks at home and wash them daily.
The Czech Republic and Slovakia have banned going out into public places without wearing a mask or covering the nose and mouth.
On March 16, Vietnam asked everyone to wear masks when going to public spaces to protect themselves and others.
The Austrian government obliged anyone entering a grocery store to wear a mask.
Israel has asked all residents to wear masks in public.
Taiwan, which produces 10 million masks a day since mid-March, has demanded that passengers on trains and intercity buses wear masks on April 1.
Panama has made wearing a face mask when going out necessary, while also recommending the manufacture of face masks at home for those who are unable to buy face masks.
Facial masks are also widely used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia and Singapore.
Social distancing (also known as physical distancing) includes infection control measures aimed at slowing the spread of the disease by minimizing mixing between individuals.
Routes include quarantine; travel restrictions; and closures of schools, workplaces, playgrounds, theatres or shopping malls.
Individuals can apply social distancing methods by staying at home, limiting travel, avoiding crowded areas, uncontemplated greetings, and physically distancing from others.
Many governments currently impose or recommend social distancing in areas affected by the outbreak.
The maximum volume of gatherings recommended by U.S. government agencies and health organizations was quickly reduced from 250 people (if a COVID-19 outbreak is not detected in the region) to 50 people, and 10 people later.
On March 22, 2020, Germany banned public gatherings of more than two people. Elderly and those suffering from chronic diseases such as diabetes, heart disease, respiratory disease, high blood pressure, and weakened immune systems face an increased risk of serious diseases and complications. The CDC has advised them to stay at home as much as possible in areas of community outbreaks.
The use of the term "social distancing" led to the suggestion that people should engage in complete social isolation, rather than encouraging them to communicate with others through alternative means. Some authorities issued sexual health directives for use during the pandemic.
This includes recommendations not to have sex except with someone you live with who is not infected with the virus or experiencing its symptoms.
Self-isolation is recommended at home for people with COVID-19 and those who are suspected.
Health agencies have issued detailed directives for proper self-isolation. Several governments have imposed or recommended self-quarantine for all residents living in affected areas.
The strongest self-quarantine instructions have been issued for those of the most vulnerable.
Those dealing with someone with COVID-19 and those who have recently traveled to a country or region where the infection is widely transmitted have been advised to quarantine themselves for 14 days from the time of another possible exposure.
Epidemic control strategies are containment or braking and limiting the spread.
Containment is applied in the early stages of the outbreak and aims to track and isolate the infected, as well as the introduction of other measures to control the infection and vaccines to prevent the disease from spreading to the rest of the population.
When the spread of the disease becomes impossibly contained, efforts are then directed to the mitigation phase: measures are taken to slow the spread and mitigate its effects on the health care system and the community.
A mixture of containment and mitigation measures can be carried out at the same time.
Braking requires tougher measures to reverse the pandemic by reducing the core reproduction number to less than 1. Part of managing infectious disease outbreaks attempts to reduce the peak of the epidemic, known as flattening the curve of the epidemic.
This reduces the risk of straining health services and provides more time for the development of vaccines and treatments.
Non-pharmaceutical interventions that may address outbreaks include personal protective measures, such as hand hygiene, wearing masks and self-quarantine; community actions aimed at physical distancing such as closing schools and abolishing major gathering events; community solidarity to encourage acceptance and participation in such interventions; as well as environmental measures such as cleaning surfaces.
Other countries have also taken a variety of measures aimed at reducing the spread of the virus.
South Korea has introduced mass checks and local quarantines and issued alerts on the movements of the injured.
Singapore has provided financial support to those injured who isolated themselves and imposed heavy fines on those who did not.
Taiwan has increased the production of masks and criminalized hoarding medical supplies. Simulations of Great Britain and the United States show that easing (slowing rather than stopping the spread of the epidemic) and braking (reversing the growth of the epidemic) face major challenges.
Optimal alleviation policies may reduce the peak demand for health care by 2/3 and deaths by half, but they still lead to hundreds of thousands of deaths and strain health systems.
Braking may be preferred but it must be preserved as long as the virus spreads in humans (or until the vaccine becomes available, if this happens first), as transmission on the other hand quickly bounces when measures are relaxed.
Long-term intervention to curb the pandemic causes social and economic costs.
There are no specific antiviral drugs approved for COVID-19, but development efforts are underway, including testing of existing drugs.
Taking over-the-counter cold medications, drinking fluids, and restiration may help relieve symptoms.
Depending on the severity, treatment with oxygen, intravenous fluids and respiratory support may be required.
The use of steroids may aggravate the results.
Many pre-approved compounds have been screened for COVID-19.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has also stated that some “traditional and home remedies” can provide relief for symptoms caused by SARS-COVID-19.
The increased capacity and adaptation of health-care centers to meet the needs of COVID-19 patients have been described by the World Health Organization (WHO) as key measures to respond to the outbreak.
The European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) and the European Regional Office of the World Health Organization (WHO) have issued guidelines for hospitals and primary health-care services to transfer resources at multiple levels, including the focus of laboratory services on COVID-19 testing, the removal of elective procedures as much as possible, the dismissal and isolation of COVID-19 patients, and increased intensive care capabilities through staff training and an increase in the number of ventilators and available beds.
There are multiple theories about where the first case originated (the so-called zero patient).
The first known case of the novel coronavirus may date from December 1, 2019 in Wuhan, Bhubi, China.
Within a month, the number of coronavirus cases in Hubei gradually increased.
This was mostly associated with the Wuhan seafood wholesale market, which also sells living animals, and one theories say that the virus came from one of these types of animals; in other words, it has an animal origin. A group with pneumonia for an unknown reason was observed on December 26 and was handled by Dr. Zhang Jishyan at Hubei County Hospital, which informed the Jianghan Poohan CDC on December 27.
On 30 December, a group of doctors at Wuhan Central Hospital warned their colleagues of “the coronavirus resembles SARS.”
Police alerted eight of these doctors, including Lee Wyn Liang, for spreading false rumors, and another doctor, Ai Finn, was scolded by her superiors to raise the alarm.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Commission later issued a public notice on 31 December and informed the World Health Organization (WHO).
Enough unknown pneumonia cases were reported to health authorities in Wuhan, prompting them to start the investigation in early January. During the early stages of the outbreak, the number of cases doubled about every seven and a half days.
In early and mid-January 2020, the virus spread to other Chinese provinces, with the help of transitions during China’s New Year and the fact that Wuhan is a transportation hub and the intersection point of major railway lines.
On January 20, China reported nearly 140 new cases in a single day, including two in Beijing and one in Shenzhen.
Official data released later shows that 6,174 people have already developed symptoms by January 20, 2020. As of 26 March, the United States has surpassed China and Italy the highest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, more than 1.61 million cases have been reported worldwide, with more than 97,000 deaths and more than 36,4,000 recovered.
Approximately 200 countries and territories recorded at least one case.
Due to the pandemic in Europe, many countries in the Schengen region restricted freedom of movement and imposed border controls.
As of 2 April, nearly 300 million people, or about 90 percent of the population, have undergone a form of closure in the United States, more than 50 million people have been shut down in the Philippines, about 59 million people have been shut down in South Africa, and 1.3 billion people have been shut down in India.
On 26 March, 1.7 billion people worldwide underwent a form of lockdown, which rose to 2.6 billion two days later—about one-third of the world’s population.
The first confirmed case of COVID-19 dates back to 1 December 2019 in Wuhan; one unconfirmed report indicates that the first case was on 17 November.
Dr. Zhang Jishyan noticed a range of pneumonia cases caused unknown on December 26, following which her hospital informed the Jianghan-Bohan CDC on 27 December.
On December 27, 2019, an initial genetic testing of patient samples indicated the presence of SARS-like coronavirus.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Committee issued a public notice on 31 December.
The World Health Organization (WHO) was informed on the same day.
In the presence of these notifications, doctors in Wuhan have been warned by police about “spreading rumors” about the outbreak.
China’s National Health Commission initially claimed no “clear evidence” of transmission from one person to another.
In late January, the Chinese government launched a drastic campaign, which was later described by CPC Secretary-General Xi Jinping as a "popular war" to contain the spread of the virus.
In what has been described as “the largest quarantine in human history,” a collar was announced on January 23 to stop travel to and from Wuhan, spanning a total of 15 cities in Hubey, affecting some 57 million people.
The use of private vehicles in the city has been banned.
Chinese New Year celebrations (January 25) were canceled in several places.
Authorities have also announced the construction of a makeshift hospital, the Hushenshan hospital, which was completed within 10 days.
Another hospital was then built, Leshenshan hospital, to deal with additional patients.
In addition to newly established hospitals, China has also diverted 14 other facilities in Wuhan, such as conference centers and stadiums, into makeshift hospitals. On 26 January, the government took other measures to contain the COVID-19 outbreak, including providing health announcements to travellers and extending the Spring Festival holiday.
Universities and schools have also been closed across the country.
The Hong Kong and Macau regions have taken several measures, particularly with regard to schools and universities.
Remote working measures have been taken in several Chinese regions.
Restrictions have been imposed on travel inside and outside Hubei.
Public transport has been modified, and museums have been temporarily closed across China.
Control of public traffic has been tightened in several cities, and it is estimated that about 760 million people (more than half of the population) have been subjected to a form of exit restriction.
For example, Beijing imposed a mandatory 14-day quarantine on all returnees to the city from other countries. On 23 March, one case moved locally in mainland China over the past five days, via a passenger returning to Guangzhou from Istanbul.
On March 24, 2020, Chinese Premier Li Keqiang stated that the spread of locally transmitted contagions had receded and outbreak control was controlled in China.
Restrictions related to travel in Hubei were eased on the same day, with the exception of Wuhan, two months after the closure was imposed. The Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced on March 26, 2020 the suspension of visa holders or residence permits from March 28 onwards, with no specific details about when this policy would end.
Those wishing to enter China will have to apply for visas with Chinese embassies or consulates.
The Chinese government has urged companies and factories to resume work on March 30, offering a range of monetary incentives to companies. The State Council declared a three-minute day of silence on April 4 at 10:00, coinciding with the Qingming Festival, although the central government called on families to pay their respects online for physical distancing to avoid the COVID-19 outbreak again.
Covid-19 transmission from China to South Korea was confirmed on January 20, 2020.
The National Health Agency announced a significant increase in confirmed cases on 20 February, largely due to a synthesis in Daegu to a new religious movement known as the Church of Chinchyunji Jesus.
Shincheonji's followers visiting Daegu from Wuhan were suspected to be the source of the outbreak.
As of 22 February, 1,261 people, or about 13% of 9,336 church adherents, reported symptoms. South Korea announced the highest alert levels on 23 February 2020.
On 28 February, more than 2,000 confirmed cases were reported in Korea, rising to 3,150 on 29 February.
All South Korean military bases were quarantined after tests confirmed that three soldiers had contracted the virus.
Flight schedules were also affected, and then changed. South Korea implemented a program that was considered the largest and best organized in the world to screen people for the virus, isolate any infected person, and track their contact and quarantine.
Examination methods included mandatory personal reporting of symptoms by new international expatriates via the mobile application, testing for the virus on people inside their cars, and testing results available the next day, and increasing testing capabilities to reach 20,000 people per day.
South Korea's program represents a success in controlling the outbreak even though no quarantine has not been applied to entire cities. South Korean society was initially divided on President Moon Jae-in's response to the crisis.
Many Koreans have signed petitions that either demand Moon’s impeachment, allegating the government’s mishandling of the outbreak, or praising its response.
On 23 March, it was announced that South Korea had the lowest overall cases in a single day in four weeks.
On 29 March, it has been reported that, as of 1 April, all new arrivals from abroad will be quarantined for two weeks.
According to media reports on April 1, South Korea has received requests from 121 different countries to help test for the virus.
Iran reported the first confirmed case of SARS-CoV-2 on February 19 at Qom, where two people died later the same day, according to the Ministry of Health and Medical Education.
The early measures announced by the government included the cancellation of concerts and other cultural events and sporting events, Friday prayers, and the closure of universities, institutes of higher education, and schools.
Iran has allocated five trillion riyals to fight the virus.
On February 26, 2020, President Hassan Rouhani stated that there was no plan to quarantine areas affected by the outbreak, and only individuals would be isolated.
Plans were announced to limit inter-city travel in March, although heavy intra-urban traffic continued ahead of the new Persian year Nowruz.
Shiite shrines remained open to pilgrims until March 16, 2020. During February, Iran became the epicenter of the spread of the virus after China.
In the midst of the allegations that Iran covered up the extent of the outbreak, more than a dozen countries tracked their cases returning from Iran as of February 28, indicating that the extent of the outbreak may exceed the 388 cases reported by the Iranian government until that date.
Iran’s parliament, where 23 of its 290 members were reported to have tested positive for the virus on March 3.
On 12 March, Human Rights Watch called on Iranian prison authorities to unconditionally release human rights defenders detained due to peaceful dissent, as well as the temporary release of eligible prisoners.
She mentioned that there is a high risk of the spread of the virus in enclosed spaces, such as prisons, which also lack adequate medical care.
On 15 March, the Iranian government announced 100 deaths in a single day, the highest number recorded in the country since the outbreak began.
At least 12 Iranian politicians and current and former government officials have died of the disease by March 17.
By 23 March, Iran had recorded 50 new cases per hour and one death every 10 minutes due to the coronavirus.
According to a World Health Organization (WHO) official, the number of cases in Iran may be up to five times what has been announced.
U.S. sanctions on Iran are also believed to influence the country’s financial capacity to respond to the outbreak.
The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR) has called for easing economic sanctions on the countries most affected by this pandemic, including Iran.
The outbreak was confirmed in Italy on 31 January, when SARS-CoV-2 tests for Chinese tourists in Rome tested positive.
Cases began to increase sharply, prompting the Italian government to suspend all flights to and from China and declare a state of emergency.
Subsequently discovered an unlinked collection of COVID-19 cases, starting with 16 confirmed cases in Lombardy on 21 February. On 22 February, the Council of Ministers promulgated a new law to contain the outbreak, including quarantining of more than 50,000 people from 11 different municipalities in northern Italy.
Prime Minister Giuseppe Conté said, ‘It will not be allowed to enter or exit areas of the outbreak.
It has already been ordered to suspend work activities and sporting events in those areas.” On 4 March, the Italian government ordered the complete closure of all schools and universities across the country, where deaths in Italy reached 100.
All major sporting events, including first division football matches, were scheduled to take place behind closed doors until April, but on 9 March, all sporting events were completely suspended for at least one month.
On 11 March, Prime Minister Conté ordered the cessation of almost all commercial activities, with the exception of supermarkets and pharmacies. On 6 March, the Italian College of Anesthesia, Analgesia, Resuscitation and Intensive Care (SIAARTI) published medical ethics recommendations regarding triage protocols that could be used.
On 19 March, Italy overtook China as the country with the highest number of coronavirus-related deaths in the world after 3,405 pandemic-related deaths were reported.
On 22 March, Russia sent nine military aircraft carrying medical equipment to Italy.
As of 5 April, the confirmed cases amounted to 128,948, the number of deaths amounted to 15,887, and 21,815 cases recovered in Italy, most of which were in the Lombardy region.
According to the CNN report, a combination of a large number of elderly people and the inability to test all those infected with the virus so far in Italy may have contributed to a high mortality rate.
The UK’s response to the virus first emerged as the most laxi among affected countries, and as of March 18, 2020, the UK government did not impose any form of social distancing or comprehensive quarantine measures on its citizens.
As a result, the government has been criticized for noticably lacking speed and severity in its response to the concerns faced by the public. On 16 March, Prime Minister Boris Johnson issued a statement advocating to avoid non-necessary travel or social contact, suggesting that people work from home as much as possible and avoid several places, such as bars, restaurants and theatres.
On 20 March, the government announced that it would close all recreational facilities such as bars and gyms as soon as possible, and promised to pay up to 80% of workers’ wages up to a maximum of £2,500 per month to reduce unemployment at the time of the crisis. On 23 March, the prime minister announced stricter social distancing measures, banning gatherings of more than two people and restricting travel and out-of-the-home activity unless absolutely necessary.
Contrary to previous measures, these restrictions were implemented by the police by issuing fines and dispersing gatherings.
Orders have been issued for the closure of most companies, with the exception of "necessary" commercial projects, including supermarkets, pharmacies, banks, hardware stores, petrol stations, and garments.
On 20 January, the first known case of COVID-19 was confirmed in the north-western Pacific state of Washington for a man returning from Wuhan on 15 January.
The White House Coronavirus Task Force was prepared on January 29.
On 31 January, the Trump administration declared a state of public health emergency, restricting the entry of travelers from China.
On January 28, 2020, the Centers for Disease Control — the leading public health institute of the U.S. government — announced that it had developed its own test kit.
Despite doing so, the U.S. slowed the start of the test, blocking the true extent of the outbreak at that time.
The test spoiled a flawed test set produced by the federal government in February, the lack of approval by the federal government of non-governmental test kits (by academia, companies and hospitals) until the end of February, and the restrictive criteria for people to qualify for the test until early March (the doctor’s order was then required).
By February 27, the Washington Post reported that fewer than 4,000 tests had been conducted in the United States.
By 13 March, The Atlantic had reported that fewer than 14,000 tests had been conducted.
On March 22, The Associated Press reported: "Many people who have had symptoms and have a doctor's order have waited hours or days to test." After the first death was reported in the U.S
Schools in Seattle cancelled the study on 3 March, and by mid-March, schools were closed across the country. On 6 March 2020, the United States was informed of projections regarding the impact of the novel coronavirus on the country by epidemiologists at Imperial College London.
On the same day, President Trump signed into law the Complementary Appropriations for Coronavirus Preparedness and Responsive Acquisitions Act, which provided $8.3 billion in emergency funding to federal agencies to respond to the outbreak.
Companies imposed travel restrictions on employees, cancelled conferences, and urged employees to work from home.
On 11 March, Trump announced travel restrictions for most of Europe, excluding the United Kingdom, for 30 days, as of March 13.
The next day, the restrictions were extended to the United Kingdom and Ireland.
On 13 March, he declared a national state of emergency, which made it possible to use federal funds to respond to the crisis.
As of 15 March, many companies have closed or reduced their working hours across the United States in an effort to limit the spread of the virus.
By 17 March, the epidemic had been confirmed in all 50 states and in the District of Columbia. On 23 March, 10,700 cases of coronavirus were reported in New York City, more than the total number of cases in South Korea.
On March 25, the governor said social distancing appears to have been successful, with estimates showing a slowdown in the multiplication of cases from 2.0 days to 4.7 days.
As of 28 March, there were 32,308 confirmed cases in New York City, and 672 people died from the virus. On 26 March, more confirmed cases of coronavirus were reported in the United States than in any other country in the world, including China and Italy. As of April 8, 400,335 cases had been confirmed in the United States, and 12,841 people had died.
According to media reports on March 30, U.S. President Trump decided to extend the social distancing directive until April 30.
On the same day, USNS Comfort, a hospital ship containing about 1000 beds in New York.
On 3 April, the United States recorded 884 deaths from coronavirus within 24 hours.
In New York, the number of cases exceeded 100,000 on April 3. The White House has been criticized for underestimating the threat and controlling the circulation of news by directing health officials and scientists to coordinate public data and publications related to the virus with the office of Vice President Mike Pence.
The Trump administration’s handling of the crisis has not been fully acclaimed as it has wreaked havoc among parties.
Some U.S. officials and commentators have criticized the U.S. reliance on importing essential items, including essential medical supplies, from China.
Analysis of air travel patterns was used to map and predict spread patterns and was published in the Journal of Travel Medicine in mid-January 2020.
Based on information from the International Air Transport Association (IATA), Bangkok, Hong Kong, Tokyo and Taipei had the highest number of passengers travelling from Wuhan.
Dubai, Sydney and Melbourne were also reported as popular destinations for travellers from Wuhan.
Bali has been reported to be among more than 20 popular destinations in terms of preparedness, while cities in Australia have been considered more capable. Australia released its COVID-19 emergency response plan on February 7.
She stated that not much has yet been discovered about COVID-19, and that Australia will emphasize border control and communications in its response to the pandemic.
On 21 March, a human biosecurity emergency was declared in Australia.
Since public transport has been affected by effective quarantine in Wuhan and Hubei, several countries have planned to evacuate their nationals and diplomatic staff from the region, primarily through chartered flights from their home countries, with Chinese authorities providing permits.
Canada, the United States, Japan, India, Sri Lanka, Australia, France, Argentina, Germany and Thailand were among the first countries to have planned to evacuate their citizens.
Pakistan has said it will not evacuate any of its citizens from China.
On 7 February, Brazil evacuated 34 Brazilians or family members as well as four Poles, a Chinese person, and an Indian citizen.
Polish, Chinese and Indian nationals disembarked from the plane in Poland, where the Brazilian plane stopped before resuming its itinerary to Brazil.
Brazilian citizens who went to Wuhan were isolated at a military base near Brasília.
On the same day, 215 Canadians (176 of the first plane, and 39 of a second plane chartered by the U.S. government) were evacuated from Wuhan to Canadian Forces base Trenton for two weeks.
On 11 February, another aircraft carrying 185 Canadians from Wuhan landed at Canadian troop base Trenton.
Australian authorities evacuated 277 citizens on 3 and 4 February to the Christmas Island Detention Center, which was reused as a quarantine facility, where they stayed for 14 days.
A New Zealand evacuation flight arrived in Auckland on Feb. 5, and its passengers (including people from Australia and the Pacific) were isolated at a naval base in Wangabarawa, northern Auckland.
On February 15, the United States announced that it would evacuate Americans who had been aboad the cruise ship Diamond Princess.
On 21 February, a plane landed in Trenton, Ontario, carrying 129 Canadian passengers evacuated from the Diamond Princess.
In early March, the Indian government began evacuating its citizens from Iran. On 14 March, a plane from the South African Airlines company chartered by the South African government returned 112 South African citizens.
A pre-departure medical examination was conducted, and four South Africans who showed signs of coronavirus were left to mitigate the risks.
South Africans whose test results were only negative were returned.
The test results proved that South Africans, including aviation crew, pilots, hotel staff, police and soldiers involved in the humanitarian mission, all of whom, as a preventive measure, remained under surveillance and quarantine at the Ranch resort for 14 days, were uninjured.
On 20 March, the United States began to partially withdraw its troops from Iraq due to the pandemic.
On 5 February, China’s Foreign Ministry reported that 21 countries (including Belarus, Pakistan, Trinidad, Tobago, Egypt and Iran) had sent aid to China.
Some Chinese students at U.S. universities came together to send aid to HIV-infected areas in China, and a joint group in the Greater Chicago District reportedly was able to send 50,000 N95 masks to hospitals in Hubei Prefecture on January 30. Direct Relief, in coordination with FedEx, sent 200,000 face masks with other personal protective equipment, including gloves and clothing from hospital staff, via the Air Emergency Bridge, to Wuhan Union Hospital by January 30.
On 5 February, Bill and Melinda Gates announced a donation of $100 million to the World Health Organization (WHO) to fund vaccine research and treatment efforts, as well as the protection of “at-risk populations in Africa and South Asia.”
Entraxion reported that the Chinese government donated 200,000 masks to the Philippines on February 6, after Senator Richard Gordon shipped 3.16 million masks to Wuhan.
On 19 February, the Singapore Red Cross announced that it would send $2.26 million in aid to China.
Japan donated one million face-to-face masks, Turkey sent medical equipment, Russia sent more than 13 tons of medical supplies to Wuhan, Malaysia announced the donation of 18 million medical gloves to China, Germany handed over several medical supplies, including 10,000 protective suits, the United States donated 17.8 tons of medical supplies, and promised another $100 million in financial support to the affected countries.
In March, China, Cuba, Russia sent medical supplies and experts to help Italy deal with the coronavirus outbreak.
Businessman Jacques Mama has sent Addis Ababa in Ethiopia 1.1 million test kits, 6 million face masks, and 60,000 protective suits distributed by the African Union.
Subsequently 5,000 test kits, 100,000 face masks and 5 ventilators were sent to Panama.
The Netherlands, Spain, Turkey, Georgia and the Czech Republic have also expressed concern about masks and testing kits made in China.
For example, Spain withdrew 58,000 coronavirus test kits made in China that had only 30% accuracy, meanwhile, the Netherlands returned 600,000 face masks made in China that were defective.
Belgium returned 100,000 unusable face masks that were thought to be from China, but were in fact from Colombia.
On the other hand, parts of Latin America and Africa welcomed the receipt of Chinese aid well. On 2 April, the World Bank launched emergency support operations for developing countries.
The World Health Organization (WHO) hailed the efforts of the Chinese authorities in managing and containing the epidemic.
The World Health Organization (WHO) noted the difference between the 2002-2004 SARS outbreak, in which Chinese authorities were accused of obfuscating prevention and containment efforts and the current crisis, with the central government providing “regular updates to avoid panic before the Lunar New Year holiday.”
On 23 January, in response to the decision of the central authorities to implement the transport ban in Wuhan, the representative of the World Health Organization (WHO) G�üdüşla noted that while “this is certainly not a recommendation made by the World Health Organization (WHO),” it was “a very important indication of the obligation to contain the epidemic in the place where it is heavily concentrated,” describing it as “unprecedented in the history of public health.” On 30 January, after confirming the transition from one person to another outside China, and the increase in the number of cases in other countries, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared an outbreak as a public health contingency of international concern (BHIC), the sixth public health emergency case of international concern since this procedure was first invoked during the swine flu pandemic in 2009.
The Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) said that considering the outbreak a public health contingence is the subject of international attention due to “the risk of global spread, especially in low- and middle-income countries that do not have strong health systems.
In response to the implementation of travel restrictions, Tedros stated that “there is no reason to take measures that unnecessarily interfere with international travel and trade” and that “the World Health Organization (WHO) does not recommend limiting trade and mobility.”
On 5 February, WHO appealed to the international community to contribute $675 million to strategic funds prepared in low-income countries, noting the urgent need to support those countries that “do not have systems to detect people infected with the virus, even if they emerge.”
Tedros also made statements announcing that "our strengdess is measured by the strength of the weakest person among us" and urged the international community to "invest today or pay a high price later." On 11 February, the World Health Organization (WHO), at a press conference, named COVID-19.
On the same day, Tedros stated that UN Secretary-General António Guterres had agreed to provide “the potential of the entire United Nations system to respond.”
As a result, a United Nations crisis management team has been set up, which has allowed the entire United Nations response to be coordinated, and the World Health Organization (WHO) will allow it “to focus on the health response while benefiting from the expertise of other agencies to learn about the broader social, economic, and developmental impacts of the outbreak.”
On 14 February, a joint WHO-led mission team with China was set up to provide international experts and experts from the World Health Organization (WHO) on the ground in China to assist with local governance and assess "the severity and transmissability of the disease" by hosting workshops and meetings with key national institutions, and conducting field visits to assess "the impact of response activities at the provincial and city levels, including urban and rural areas." On 25 February, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that "the world must do more to prepare for the possible coronavirus pandemic."
In response to the outbreak in Iran, the World Health Organization (WHO) dispatched a joint mission team there to assess the situation. On 28 February, WHO officials said that assessing the coronavirus threat at the global level would rise from "high" to "extremely high", which is its highest level of warning and risk assessment.
In a statement, Mike Ryan, executive director of the World Health Organization (WHO) health emergency programme, warned: “This is a real test for every government on earth: so we have to be prepared.
This virus may be on its way and you have to prepare for it," he said, urging the right response measures that could help the world avoid "what's worse."
Ryan also stated that current data does not justify public health officials declaring the disease as a global pandemic, saying that such an announcement means that “we fundamentally accept that every human being on this planet will be exposed to this virus.”
On 11 March, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared a coronavirus outbreak as a pandemic.
The Director-General said that the World Health Organization (WHO) is “deeply concerned about the frightening levels of prevalence, severity and alarming levels of inaction.” The World Health Organization (WHO) has faced severe criticism of what is perceived as inadequate treatment of the pandemic, including the delay in declaring a public health emergency and classifying the virus as a pandemic.
Violent reactions included a petition by WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom to submit his resignation, signed by 73,3,000 people as of 6 April.
On 26 March 2020, dozens of United Nations human rights experts affirmed respect for everyone’s rights during the COVID-19 pandemic.
The group of experts stated that everyone has the right to life-saving interventions and this responsibility is borne by the Government.
The group stressed that the lack of resources or health insurance should not be a justification for discrimination against a particular group.
The experts stressed that everyone has the right to health, including persons with disabilities, those belonging to minorities, older persons, internally displaced persons, homeless people, those living in very poor conditions and detainees, as well as refugees and other unspecified groups in need of government support.
Intergovernmental organizations are addressing the economic and social impacts of the COVID-19 crisis.
OECD has launched a platform to provide comprehensive and timely information on policy responses in countries around the world, as well as perspectives and advice.
One of the policies to strengthen health systems and the global economy to address the effects of closures and restrictions on travel, the digital axis includes a tool that tracks state policy, and aims to help countries learn from each other and facilitate the coordinated global response to the Coronavirus.
The Chinese government has been criticized by the United States, British Cabinet Office Minister Michael Gove, and Edward Bolsonaro, son of Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro, for its handling of the pandemic, which began in China’s Hubei Province.
A number of directors of the Chinese Communist Party (CBCC) have been disconnected across several sectors over their handling of quarantine efforts in central China, referring to resentment at the political establishment’s response to the outbreak in those regions.
Some commentators believe the move was aimed at protecting Chinese Communist Party Secretary-General Xi Jinping from public resentment over the coronavirus outbreak.
Some Chinese officials, for example Zhao Lijian, have previously rejected an earlier admission that the coronavirus outbreak began in Wuhan, in favor of conspiracy theories alleged by the United States or Italy regarding COVID-19.
The U.S. Donald Trump administration described the coronavirus as a "Chinese virus" or "Wuhan virus," saying that China "imposed a blackout on the news that has exacerbated the virus, which has now become a global pandemic," which has been criticized by some critics for being a racist.
Daily Best obtained a U.S. government cable reviewing the communications scheme with clear sources in the National Security Council, with a statement that “everything relates to China.
We have been asked to seek and disseminate these messages in any way possible, including press conferences and television appearances. “News sources such as Politico, Foreign Policy and Bloomberg claimed that China’s efforts to send aid to countries affected by the virus are part of a propaganda push for global impact.
EU foreign policy chief Josep Borrell has warned the existence of “a geo-political component that includes a struggle for influence through the practice of circumvention and generosity policies.”
Borrell also said, “China is aggressively pushing the message that, unlike the United States, it is a responsible and reliable partner.”
China has also called on the United States to lift sanctions on Syria, Venezuela and Iran, while reportedly sending aid to the last two countries.
U.S. Sanctions banned Jack Ma’s donation of 100,000 masks to Cuba on April 3.
U.S. authorities have also been accused of diversing aid to other countries.
There have been disputes over masks that have been reported between other countries, such as Germany, Austria, Switzerland, the Czech Republic and Italy.
In addition, Turkey has seized hundreds of respirators destined for Spain.
In early March, the Italian government criticized the European Union’s lack of solidarity with Italy affected by coronavirus.
Italy’s ambassador to the European Union, Maurizzo Massari, said: “Only China has responded at the bilateral level.
This is certainly not a good sign of European solidarity.”
On 22 March, after a phone call with Italian Prime Minister Giuseppe Conté, Russian President Vladimir Putin prepared the Russian military to send military paramedics, special disinfection vehicles, and other medical equipment to Italy.
Italy's La Stampa newspaper quoted an unnamed "high-level political source" as saying that 80 percent of Russia's aid was "negligent or worthless to Italy".
The source accused Russia of initiating a remarkable “geographical and diplomatic” political attack.
Lombardi’s president, Atilio Fontana and Italian Foreign Minister Luigi Di Maio denied press reports and expressed their gratitude.
Russia has also sent a cargo plane carrying medical aid to the United States.
Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said that “when providing assistance to his U.S. colleagues, [Putin] assumes that when U.S. manufacturers of medical equipment and materials are activated again, they will also be able to return the favor if necessary.”
The planned military drills for NATO 2020 will take place in Germany, Poland and the Baltic states, NATO’s largest naval exercise since the end of the Cold War on a small scale.
“In light of the current public health crisis, the training not only endangers the lives of troops from the United States and many participating European countries, but also the population of the countries in which it will take place.” The Iranian government has been severely affected by the virus, with about two dozen MPs as well as fifteen other former or current political figures.
Iranian President Hassan Rouhani wrote a public letter to world leaders on March 14, 2020 asking for help, saying that his country is struggling to fight the outbreak due to lack of access to international markets as a result of U.S. sanctions against Iran. The outbreak sparked calls on the United States to adopt popular social policies in other rich countries, including universal health care, universal childcare, paid family leave, and higher levels of public health funding.
Political analysts predicted that this could negatively affect Donald Trump's chances of re-election in the 2020 presidential election. Diplomatic relations between Japan and South Korea have deteriorated due to the pandemic.
South Korea has criticized Japan's "mysterious and negative quarantine efforts" after Japan announced that anyone coming from South Korea would be quarantined at certain government sites for two weeks.
South Korean society was initially polarized by President Moon Jae-in’s response to the crisis.
Many Koreans have signed petitions either calling for Moon’s impeachment because they alleged that the government mishandled the outbreak or praised its response. The pandemic allowed countries to pass emergency legislation under the response.
Some commentators have expressed concerns that this could allow governments to tighten their control over power.
In Hungary, parliament voted to allow Prime Minister Viktor Orbán to rule by indefinite decree, suspend parliament as well as elections and punish those who spread false information about the virus and the government’s handling of the crisis.
Coronavirus outbreak has been blamed for numerous supply shortages due to the increasing global use of equipment to combat outbreaks, panic buying, and disrupting the operation of factories and logistics operations.
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration has issued warnings about shortages of medicines and medical equipment due to increased consumer demand and the suspension of suppliers.
Many areas have also seen panic-intensive purchases, resulting in shelves free of essential groceries such as food, toilet paper, and water bottles, causing a shortage of supplies.
In particular, the technology industry has warned of delays in shipments of electronic goods.
According to WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom, the demand for personal protective devices has doubled by 100 times.
This demand led to prices rising by up to twenty times the normal price and also caused medical supplies to be delayed for between four and six months.
It has also caused a shortage of personal protective equipment around the world, with the World Health Organization warning that this will put health workers at risk.
In Australia, the pandemic has created a new opportunity for Diago shoppers to sell Australian products in China.
Activism resulted in a shortage of infant formula in some supermarkets and was later banned by the Australian government. Despite the widespread spread of COVID-19 cases in northern Italy and the Wuhan region, and the subsequent high demand for food products, the two regions survived severe food shortages.
Actions taken by China and Italy to combat the storage and illicit trafficking of vital products have succeeded in avoiding severe food shortages expected in Europe as well as in North America.
Northern Italy’s large agricultural production has not seen a significant decline, but prices may rise according to the expectations of industry representatives.
Shelves were temporarily empty, even in the city of Wuhan, while Chinese government officials released pork stocks to ensure adequate nutrition for residents.
Similar laws exist in Italy that require food producers to keep stockpiles of such emergencies.
According to a March 16 media report, China’s economy was hit hard in the first two months of 2020 by measures taken by the government to curb the spread of the virus, and retail sales fell by 20.5%.
Since mainland China is a major economic and industrial hub, it has been observed that an outbreak of the virus poses a major destabilizing threat to the stability of the global economy.
Agatha Demares of the Economic Intelligence Unit predicted that markets would remain fluctuating until a clearer picture of the possible results appeared.
In January 2020, some analysts estimated that the economic repercussions of the pandemic on global growth could go beyond those that occurred as a result of the SARS outbreak between 2002 and 2004.
An expert at the University of Washington at St. Louis estimated that the global supply chain will be affected by more than $300 billion which could last up to two years.
The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) is reported to have experienced a “disruption” following a sharp drop in oil prices due to a decline in demand from China.
Global stock markets fell on Feb. 24 due to a sharp rise in the number of COVID-19 cases outside mainland China.
On Feb. 27, due to growing concerns about the coronavirus outbreak, several U.S. stock indices, including Nasdaq-100, S&P 500, and the Dow-Jones Industrial Average recorded its biggest drop since 2008, with the Dow falling 1,191 points, its largest one-day decline since the 2007–08 financial crisis.
Three indices ended the week down more than 10%.
On February 28, Scoop Rattings GMBH confirmed China’s sovereign credit rating, but maintained negative expectations.
Stocks fell again due to fears of the coronavirus, which was the largest decline on March 16.
Many believe that an economic recession is possible.
Economist Mohamed El-Erian praised the emergency measures taken by central banks and countries in a timely manner.
Central banks are responding faster than they were in light of the 2008 financial meltdown.
Tourism is one of the sectors most affected by the travel ban, the closure of public places, including tourist attractions, and the warning of governments to travel around the world.
As a result, many airlines have canceled their flights due to reduced demand, including British Airways, China Eastern Airlines and Cants, while British regional airline, flybe, has collapsed.
The impact on the cruise line industry has been at an unprecedented level.
Many train stations and ferry ports have also been closed.
The pandemic coincided with the Spring Festival travel season known as Chunyun, the main travel season associated with China’s New Year holiday.
National and regional governments have canceled a number of events featuring large crowds, including New Year's Eve celebrations, with private companies also independently closing their stores and tourist attractions such as Disneyland Hong Kong and Disneyland Shanghai.
Numerous Lunar New Year events and tourist attractions have been closed to prevent large gatherings, including Beijing’s Forbidden City and exhibitions of traditional temples.
In 24 of the 31 counties, and in municipalities and districts in China, authorities extended the New Year holiday until 10 February, instructing most workplaces not to reopen until that date.
These regions account for 80 percent of the country’s gross domestic product (GDP) and 90 percent of exports.
Hong Kong has raised the level of response to infectious diseases to the highest level and declared a state of emergency, closing schools until March and revoking New Year's ceremonies.
Visits to retailers in Europe and Latin America fell by 40%.
Retailers in North America and the Middle East experienced a decline of between 50 and 60%.
This also resulted in a decrease of between 33 and 43% in pedestrian traffic to shopping malls in March compared to February.
Shopping mall operators around the world have imposed additional measures, such as increased hygiene, the installation of thermal scanners to check shoppers’ temperature, and the cancellation of events. According to estimates by the United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America (UN-LAC), the pandemic-induced recession could leave between 14 and 22 million more people in extreme poverty in Latin America who would have suffered without the pandemic.
In January and February 2020, during the height of the pandemic in Wuhan, about 5 million people in China lost their jobs.
In March 2020, more than 10 million Americans lost their jobs and applied for government assistance.
Closures in India have left tens of millions of Indian immigrant workers (paid through daily wages) unemployed. An Angus Reed Institute survey found that 44% of Canadian households have experienced some kind of unemployment. Approximately 900,000 workers have lost their jobs in Spain since it closed in mid-March 2020.
During the second half of March, 4 million French workers applied for temporary unemployment benefits and 1 million British workers applied for a comprehensive credit scheme. Nearly half a million companies in Germany sent their workers into government-subsidized short-term action plans known as Kurzarbet.
France and Britain have adopted a plan to compensate for short-term German work.
The performing arts and cultural heritage sectors have been profoundly affected by the pandemic, affecting the operations of organizations as well as individuals—workers and independents—globally.
Arts and culture sector institutions have sought to support their mission (often government-funded) to provide access to the cultural heritage of the community, preserve the safety of their employees and the public, and support artists as much as possible.
By March 2020, around the world to varying degrees, museums, libraries, exhibition venues and other cultural institutions were closed indefinitely with their exhibitions, events and performances cancelled or postponed.
In response, there have been intensive efforts to provide alternative services through digital platforms. Among the recent and rapid repercussions of the disease, religious services, major sporting events, and other social events, such as musical festivals and concerts, technological conferences, and fashion shows, have been abolished.
The film industry has also suffered a hiatus. The Vatican has announced the cancellation of Holy Week celebrations in Rome, which take place during the last week of the Christian repentance season in Lent.
Many dioceses have advised elderly Christians to stay at home instead of attending mass on Sundays, as some churches have made church services available via radio, online or television, while others offer worship without leaving their cars.
With Rome's Catholic Diocese in Rome closed its churches and synagogical chapels, and St. Peter's Square evacuated Christian pilgrims, other religious bodies have also abolished limited public services and gatherings in churches, mosques, synagogue synagogues, synagogues, and guerduara.
Iran's health ministry announced the cancellation of Friday prayers in areas affected by the outbreak, after which shrines were closed, while Saudi Arabia banned foreign pilgrims as well as its residents from entering holy sites in Mecca and Medina.
This pandemic has caused the biggest disruption to the global sports calendar since World War II.
Most major sporting events have been canceled or postponed, including the 2019-20 UEFA Champions League, the 2019–20 Premier League, the 2020 Europa League, the 2019–20 NBasketball League and the 2019–20 National Hockey League.
The outbreak disrupted plans for the 2020 Summer Olympics, which were scheduled to begin at the end of July, and the International Olympic Committee announced on 24 March that the event “will be rescheduled beyond 2020 but no later than the summer of 2021.”
This led many gamblers to move to play online, with many online gambling sites reporting significant increases in the rates of new subscriptions. The entertainment industry has also been impacted, as it hung different musical groups or cancelled concert tours.
Many large theaters such as those on Broadway have suspended all performances, too.
Some artists have discovered ways to continue producing and sharing the work online as an alternative to traditional live performance, such as living concerts or creating “ceremonies” online for artists to perform, distribute, and publish their work.
On the Internet, there have been a lot of Internet memes about the coronavirus, with many turning to humor and entertainment in light of uncertainty.
Since the COVID-19 outbreak, extreme prejudice, xenophobic prejudice and racism have been observed towards people of Chinese and East Asian descent, and against people from hotspots in Europe, the United States, and other countries.
Incidents of fear, suspicion and hostility have been observed in many countries, particularly in Europe, East Asia, North America and the Asia-Pacific region.
Reports issued in February (when the majority of cases were still confined to China) documented racist feelings expressed by various groups around the world towards the Chinese that they deserved the virus or received what was alleged to be a justifiable punishment.
Anti-Chinese sentiment has also seen a rise in some African countries.
Many residents of Wuhan and Hubei reported discrimination because of their regional origin.
There has been support for Chinese both online and offline, and for those living in areas affected by the virus.
After the spread of the outbreak in new hotspots, people from Italy, Europe’s first country to experience severe outbreaks of COVID-19, may be skeptical and xenophobic. Citizens in countries including Malaysia, New Zealand, Singapore, and South Korea have initially signed petitions for pressure to prevent Chinese from entering their country in an effort to stop the disease.
In Japan, the hashtag ChineseDontComeTo Japan topped #Twitter.
The Chinese, as well as other Asians in the United Kingdom and the United States, have reported increasing levels of racist abuse, as well as attacks.
U.S. President Donald Trump has faced criticism for referring to the coronavirus as a "Chinese virus," a term that critics have considered racist and anti-Chinese.
Demonstrators in Ukraine attacked buses carrying Ukrainians and foreigners evacuated from Wuhan to Novi Sanzari.
Students coming from north-eastern India, which shares a border with China and studying in major Indian cities, have been harassed by the coronavirus outbreak.
The head of the Bharatiya Janata Party unit in West Bengal state, Delib Gösch, said the Chinese had destroyed nature and “that’s why God has avenged them.”
China’s Consulate in Kolkata later condemned the remarks, calling them “wrong”. In China, the pandemic led to growing xenophobia and racism against non-Chinese residents, with foreigners described as “foreign garbage” and targeted for “dispossession.”
Many newspapers have removed subscription paid pages for some or all of their coronavirus coverage.
Many scientific publishers have made available scientific papers related to the outbreak for open access.
Some scientists have chosen to quickly share their findings on pre-print servers such as bioRxiv.
Emerging infectious diseases – infectious diseases from emerging pathogens – are often evolving in terms of the scope or mode of transmission
Globalization and Disease – Overview of Globalization and Disease Transmission
List of Epidemics and Pandemics – List of death tolls due to infectious diseases
Smuggling of wild animals and zoonotic diseases – health risks associated with trade in exotic wildlife
The laboratory test of respiratory coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and associated SARS-CoV-2 virus includes ways to detect the presence of the virus where they identify antibodies produced in response to the infection.
RT-PCR confirms the presence of viruses in the samples, where RNA detects coronavirus.
This test is only accurate and designed to detect SARS-CoV-2 RNA.
It is used to confirm very recent or active infections.
Detection of antibodies (serology) is used to diagnose and monitor the population.
Antibodies tests show the number of people who have contracted the disease, including those whose symptoms have been too simple to report or who have no symptoms.
The exact mortality rate of the disease and the level of mass immunity of the population can be determined depending on the results of this test.
Due to the limitations of tests, no country in March 2020 had reliable data on the spread of the virus among its population.
On 23 March, no country examined more than 3% of its population, and the number of tests conducted by various countries varies greatly.
This disparity is also likely to have a significant impact on the mortality rates of reported cases, which are likely to be significantly overestimated in some countries.
Using the intraductory polymrase intravenous retraction (RAT-BCR), the examination can be performed on respiratory samples obtained in various ways, such as a nasopharyngeal swab or phlegm sample.
Results are generally available within a few hours to 2 days.
RT-BCR, which is performed using throat swabs, is not used until the first week of the disease.
The virus may then disappear from the throat as it continues to multiply in the lungs.
For infected people who are screened in the second week, it is possible instead to take a sample of deep aerodynamics through suction catheters or the use of culprit (phlegm).
An early BCR test was devised at Charitée in Berlin in January 2020 using intravenous retracting polymerase (RRT-BCR), and formed the basis for 2,50,000 test kits distributed by the World Health Organization (WHO).
The United Kingdom also developed a test on January 23, 2020. The South Korean company Kogenebiotech also created the PowerChek Coronavirus detection kit for SARS-CoV-2 virus suitable for safe clinical use based on PCR on January 28, 2020.
In China, BGI Group was one of the first companies to obtain emergency use approval from the Chinese National Medical Products Administration for SARS-CoV-2 detection kit based on PCR. In the United States, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) distributes its own Coronavirus-2019 diagnostic board based on RT-BCR on real-time public health laboratories through the International Detecting Materials Saving Authority.
One of the three genetic tests in older versions of the test kits produced inconclusive results due to defective detectors, and one of the test phases lagged further at CDC in Atlanta; this led to the successful completion of less than 100 samples per day on average throughout February 2020.
Tests using two components did not have confidence until February 28, 2020, and state and local laboratories were not even allowed to start testing.
The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has approved the test under emergency use authorization. Commercial labs in the United States began conducting tests in early March 2020.
On March 5, 2020, LapCorp announced the availability of COVID-19 testing across the country based on RT-PCR.
Similarly, Quest Diagnostics has enabled COVID-19 testing across the country as of March 9, 2020.
No quantitative restrictions have been advertised; the sampling and processing must be carried out in accordance with CDC requirements.
In Russia, the State Center for Virology and Biotechnology Research Viktor has devised and produced COVID-19 testing.
On February 11, 2020, the Federal Service for Healthcare Surveillance recorded the test. On March 12, 2020, it was reported that Mayo Clinic had developed a test to detect COVID-19 infection. On March 13, 2020, Roche Diagnostics received FDA approval for a test that could be performed within 3.5 hours on large numbers, allowing a single machine to conduct nearly 4128 tests over 24 hours.
On March 19, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) granted emergency use authorization (EUA) to Abbott Laboratories to perform testing on the ABOT m2000 system; the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) had previously granted a similar permit to Hlogic, LabCorp and Thermo Fisher Synthevic.
On March 21, 2020, SIFID obtained a similar emergency use authorization from the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in favor of a test that takes about 45 minutes.
The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has approved a test that is using the technique of amplifiging of other-heat DNA instead of PCR.
Since this doesn’t require a series of successive temperature cycles, this method can show positive results in less than five minutes and negative results in 13 minutes.
There are currently about 18,000 of these machines in the United States and Abbott expects to increase manufacturing to conduct 50,000 tests per day. Taiwan is also developing a test using a monoclonal antibody that is particularly associated with the noodly coronavirus (N protein), in the hope that it will be able to achieve results within 15 to 20 minutes such as a rapid influenza tester.
A review of studies in March 2020 concluded that "chest radiography has little diagnostic value in the early stages, while the results of CT [computed tomography] may be evident even before symptoms appear."
The usual properties shown in computed tomography include multi-lobed double-sided split-glass opacities with an ambient, asymmetric, and rear distribution.
The spread develops under the pleura and shows irregular patches and condensess as the disease develops.
A study in Wuhan comparing PCR and computed tomography in relation to the origin of the current pandemic indicated that computed tomography is more sensitive than PCR, although it is less accurate, with many of its imaging properties combined with pneumonia and other pathological phases.
In March 2020, the U.S. College of Radiography recommends that "computed tomography should not be used to detect COVID-19 or considered a preliminary examination for the diagnosis of the disease." In March 2020, CDC recommends using PCR for the preliminary examination.
Part of the immune response to infection involves the production of antibodies, including IgM and IgG.
They can be used to detect infection of individuals during a period starting from 7 days or after the onset of symptoms, to determine the ability of immunity, and in the case of population monitoring.
High-performance automated systems in many clinical laboratories will be able to perform these analyses, but their availability will depend on the production rate of each system.
For centralized laboratories, one sample of peripheral blood cells is usually used, although successive samples can be used to track the immune response.
For a point-of-clinical care test, a single blood sample is usually removed by a skin hole.
Contrary to the PCR methods, it is not necessary to make the extraction step before the examination. On March 26, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has identified 29 entities that have notified the agency in accordance with the requirements.
On April 7, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved only one test under emergency use authorization. In late March 2020, Euroimmun Medical Laboratory Diagnostics and Epitope Diagnostics received European accreditations for their test kits, which can detect IgG and IgA antibodies in blood samples.
The test delivers several hundred samples within hours and is therefore much faster than the traditional BCR analysis of viral RNA.
Antibodies can often be detected 14 days after the onset of the infection. Early in April, the United Kingdom discovered that the antibody test kits it had purchased were not good enough to be used.
Hong Kong has developed a plan under which suspected patients can stay at home, “Emergency provides a sample tube to patients,” spitting into the tube, and putting it back back to get the test result after a short time. The NHS has announced that it is experimenting with a plan to test suspected cases at home, thus excluding the risk of transmission from the infected when they come to the hospital or the need to sterilize the ambulance used.
South Korea’s in-vehicle screening centres have enabled South Korea to conduct some of the world’s fastest and most comprehensive tests. In Germany, on 2 March, the National Association of Legal Health Insurance Physicians (NSOS) said it had conducted about 12,000 outpatient tests per day and 10,700 people underwent testing last week.
The health insurance bears the costs if a doctor orders the test.
According to the head of the Robert Köch Institute, Germany has a total capacity of about 160,000 tests per week.
On March 19, in-vehicle virus tests were made available in many major cities.
On March 26, 2020, the total number of tests carried out in Germany was not known, because only the results of positive cases were announced.
A preliminary laboratory study revealed that, as of calendar week 12/2020, at least 483,295 samples had been tested until the end of the 12/2020 week, with 33,491 samples (6.9%) positive for SARS-CoV-2. In Israel, researchers in Technion and Rambam Hospital devised a method to examine samples taken from 64 patients at the same time and subjected this method to the test, by collecting samples and conducting no further tests only when it was found that the group sample was positive.
With BGI founder Wang Jian supervising the construction work and taking five days, modelling showed that cases in Hubei would have increased by 47% and related quarantine costs would have doubled if that testing capacity had not been completed.
Wuhan Lab has immediately tracked the construction of Huo-Yan Laboratories in Shenzhen, Tianjin, Beijing and Shanghai and in 12 cities in total across China.
By 4 March 2020 the total daily productivity had reached 50,000 tests per day. Origami Assays has released open-source and multi-transmission designs that can test up to 1122 patient samples for COVID-19 detection using only 93 tests.
In March, the lack and inefficiency of reagents caused a predicament when a large number of tests were carried out in the European Union, the United Kingdom, and the United States.
This prompted some specialists to explore sample preparation protocols, which include heating samples at 98 °C (208 °F) for 5 minutes to release the RNA genome for further testing. On 31 March, it was announced that the United Arab Emirates is at the top of the world in terms of coronavirus testing rate for its population, and is on track to raise the level of testing to reach most of the population.
This was through a combination of activating in-vehicle testing capability, and the purchase of an ultra-produciency laboratory that suits the population of Group 42 and BGI (depending on their “Huo-Yan” emergency detection laboratories in China).
The lab was set up within 14 days, allowing tens of thousands of RT-PCR tests to be conducted daily and is the world’s first laboratory of this size operating outside of China.
Various methods of testing have been devised to target different fragments of coronavirus genetic traits in China, France, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan and the United States.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has adopted the German plan for the crew industry, which is sent to low-income countries that do not have the resources to innovate their own crews.
The German plan was announced on January 17, 2020; the protocol developed by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention wasn't available until January 28, delaying tests available in the United States. China and the United States had problems with the reliability of test kits early in the outbreak, and those countries and Australia were unable to provide sufficient crews to meet demand and follow the recommendations for testing provided by health experts.
On the other hand, experts stated that the abundance of tests in South Korea helped reduce the spread of the emerging coronavirus.
South Korea’s government has greatly boosted testing capacity in private sector laboratories over several years.
On 16 March, the World Health Organization (WHO) called for intensifying testing programs as the optimal solution for limiting the progress of the COVID-19 pandemic.
In March 2020, China reported problems with the accuracy of its testing crews.
In the United States,  �flaws’ were found in the testing kits devised by CDC; then the government removed the bureaucratic barriers that prevented the test in particular. Spain purchased test kits from the Chinese company Shenzhen Biosail Biotechnology Limited, but found that the results were inaccurate.
The company explained that the incorrect results may be due to the failure to collect the samples or not to use the kits correctly.
The Spanish ministry has stated that it will retreat from the purchase of crews that gave incorrect results, and will replace them with other test kits from Shenzhen Bioeasy. 80% of the test kits purchased by the Czech Republic from China gave the wrong results. Slovakia bought 1.2 million test kits from China that turned out to be inaccurate.
Prime Minister Matović suggested dumping them in the Danube. Prof. Ach Kara of the Turkish Ministry of Health reported that the test kits purchased by Turkey from China had a "high rate of error" and had "blocked their use". The United Kingdom also bought from China 3.5 million test kits, but in early April 2020 announced that they were unusable.
The tests, followed by quarantine for those who tested positive and those in contact with SARS-CoV-2, produced positive results.
Researchers working in the Italian city of Vau, which saw Italy’s first COVID-19 death, tested the entire population of about 3,400 people twice, taking about 10 days at a time.
Approximately half of the people who tested positive had no symptomatic symptoms, and all detected cases were isolated.
As a result of a ban on movement between towns, new infections have been completely eliminated.
The coronavirus 2020 pandemic in Singapore has seen a significant decline in prevalence compared to other developed countries, but without strict restrictions such as the forced closure of restaurants and retail establishments, through the stringent tracing of contacts and restrictions on internal travel, testing and quarantine.
Many events have been canceled, and Singapore has already begun urging residents to stay indoors on March 28, but schools were reopened on March 23 on time after the holidays.
Several other countries have also taken control of the pandemic by strictly tracking contacts and imposing restrictions on domestic travel, testing and quarantine, but with less stringent closures, such as in Iceland and South Korea.
A statistical study has revealed that countries that have conducted more tests, compared to the number of deaths, have significantly lower death rates, perhaps because these countries are better able to detect those who suffer from only mild symptoms or do not have any symptoms.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that countries that do not have the ability to conduct tests or have local laboratories with limited experience on COVID-19 will send the first five positive samples and the twelfth negative samples for COVID-19 to one of the 16 WHO-accredited laboratories to confirm the test.
Of the 16 accredited laboratories, there are 7 in Asia, 5 in Europe, 2 in Africa, 1 in North America and 1 in Australia.
In the following diagram, the ‘Positive ‘% of tests’ column is influenced by the country’s policy of conducting the test.
A country that conducts tests only for people transferred to the hospital will have positive cases that are 1% higher than that of a country that conducts the test for its entire population, whether they show symptoms or not, given the equality of other factors.
Handwashing (or hand washing), also known as hand hygiene, is the process of cleaning hands for the purpose of removing dirt, grease, microorganisms or other unwanted substances.
Continuous washing of hands with soap at certain “necessary times” during the day prevents the spread of many diseases, such as diarrhea and cholera, which are transmitted through the oral anal route.
People can also develop respiratory illnesses such as influenza or cold, for example, if they don’t wash their hands before touching their eyes, noses or mouths (i.e. mucous membranes).
The necessary five moments during the day when washing hands with soap is important include: before and after defecation, after cleaning the child’s butt or changing diapers, before feeding the child, before eating, before preparing food or dealing with raw meat, fish or poultry.
If soap and water are not available, your hands can be cleaned with ashes. The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends hand washing:
Before preparing the food, during it, and after it.
Before taking care of a sick person and after him.
After changing the diapers for a child or cleaning it after using it for the toilet.
After cleaning your nose, coughing, or sneezing.
After touching animals, their fodders, or their droppings.
The medical hygiene of the hands refers to hygiene practices associated with medical procedures.
Washing hands before giving medications or providing medical care can prevent or limit the spread of the disease.
The main medicinal purpose of hand washing is to disinfecte hands from pathogens (bacterials, viruses, or other microorganisms that can cause disease) and chemicals that can cause damage or diseases.
This is especially important for people dealing with food or working in the medical field, but it’s also an important procedure for the general population.
Hand washing has many health benefits, including reducing the spread of influenza, coronavirus, and other infectious diseases, preventing infectious diseases that cause diarrhea, and reducing respiratory infections.
Reducing infant mortality rates at birth at home.
A 2013 study showed that improved hand washing practices may lead to minor improvements in height growth in children under the age of five.
In developing countries, child mortality rates related to respiratory diseases and diarrhea can be reduced by introducing simple behavioral changes, such as washing hands with soap.
This simple procedure can reduce the mortality rate from these diseases by about 50%.
Measures that encourage handwashing can reduce diarrhea attacks by nearly one-third, which is similar to providing clean water in low-income areas.
48 per cent of reduced diarrhoeal episodes can be associated with hand-washing with soap. Handwashing with soap is the single most effective and least expensive way to prevent diarrhea and acute respiratory infections (ARE) when applying spontaneous behavior in homes, schools, and communities around the world.
Pneumonia, one of the major acute respiratory infections, is the leading cause of death among children under the age of five, killing about 1.8 million children annually.
Diarrhea and pneumonia together kill about 3.5 million children annually.
According to UNICEF, converting hand washing with soap before eating and after using the toilet into an inherent habit can save more lives than any vaccine or medical intervention, reducing deaths from diarrhea by almost half, and deaths from acute respiratory infections by a quarter.
Handwashing is usually combined with other hygiene measures as part of water, sanitation and hygiene (water, sanitation and hygiene).
Handwashing also protects against herpes transmitted through direct physical contact.
Washing your hands has a simple detrimental effect is that frequent hand washing can damage the skin due to its dryness.
A Danish study revealed in 2012 that excessive hand washing can lead to a skin condition of flaking and itching known as hand eczema or hand dermatitis, which is especially common among health care workers.
Washing hands is also often seen too often as a symptom of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD).
There are five necessary moments during the day in which washing hands with soap is important to reduce the transmission of diseases through the oral anal route: after the use of the bathroom (urination, defecation), after cleaning the child’s butt (changing diapers), before feeding the child, before eating, and before/after preparing food or dealing with raw meat, fish or poultry.
In other cases, the correct method of washing hands should be practiced in order to prevent the transmission of the disease, including before or after the treatment of a cut or wound, after sneezing, coughing or cleaning your nose, after touching or handling animal droppings, and after touching the garbage.
In many countries, the rate of hand washing with soap is decreasing.
A study on handwashing in 54 countries in 2015 found that an average of 38.7% of households wash their hands with soap. A 2014 study showed that Saudi Arabia has the highest rate and is estimated at 97 per cent, and the United States is approaching the middle by 77 percent, and in China with the lowest 23 per center.
The Basic Health Care Program implemented by the Philippine Department of Education is an example of large-scale action to promote children’s health and education.
Treating worms twice a year, in addition to washing daily hands with soap, and brushing their teeth daily with fluoride, is at the heart of this national programme.
It has also been successfully implemented in Indonesia.
Adding soap or detergent to water promotes the removal of microorganisms from the skin.
The main effect of soap and detergents is to reduce the obstacles of degeneration, increasing solubility.
Water alone is an inefficiable disinfection for the skin because fats and proteins, which are components of organic soil, do not easily decompose into water.
However, the flow of the right amount of water helps to disinfect.
Because of its reusable nature, hard soap may carry bacteria acquired from previous uses.
A small number of studies noticing the bacterial transition from contaminated hard soap have concluded that transmission is unlikely because the bacteria are washed with foam.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention continues to state that “liquid soap is preferably used from untouching devices to pump soap.”
Anti-bacterial soap has been heavily promoted to health-cons interested consumers.
To date, there is no evidence that the use of recommended disinfectants or sterilizers is chosen to eliminate antibiotic-resistant organisms in nature.
However, anti-bacterial soap contains common anti-bacterial agents such as triclosane, which does not affect a large number of strains of organisms.
Therefore, if antibacterial soap does not affect antibiotic-resistant strains, it may not be as effective as it is marketed.
In addition to superficial tension reducers and skin protection factor, advanced formulations may contain acids (acetic acid, ascorbic acid, lactic acid) as an organizer of acidity, active antimicrobial benzoic acid and other skin moisturizers (cactus, vitamins, menthol, plant extracts). A comprehensive analysis conducted by the Faculty of Public Health of the University of Oregon indicated that ordinary soap is as effective as antibacterial soap from the consumer category containing triclosan in its prevention of diseases and removal of bacteria from the hands.
Hot water suitable for washing hands is not hot enough to kill bacteria.
Bacteria grow much faster at body temperature (37 °C).
However, warm water and soap are more effective than cold water and soap in the removal of natural oils that retain dust and bacteria.
Contrary to popular belief, scientific studies have shown that the use of warm water has no effect in reducing microbial load on hands.
An antiseptic or hand sanitizer is a substance that is not a water-based material for cleaning hands.
In the late 1990s and the early 21st century, non-aqueous-based liquorous hand cleansers and scrubbing (also known as an alcohol-mounted, disinfected hand lotion or hand sanitizer) began to gain popularity.
Most of them are based on isopropyl alcohol or composite ethanol with a thickening agent such as carbomer (acrylic acid polymer) in a gel, a moisturizer such as glycerin in a liquid, or foam for ease of use and to reduce the dehydration effect caused by alcohol.
The addition of diluted hydrogen peroxide increases antimicrobial activity. Hand sanitizers that contain at least 60 to 95 percent are effectively bactericidal.
Alcoholic sterilizers through rubbing eliminate bacteria, multidrug-resistant bacterium (staphylococcus-Glaude resistant to methysililin and intestine-resistant electrolytes), tuberculosis, and certain viruses (including HIV, herpes, respiratory syncytial virus, nasal viruses, reversals, influenza, hepatitis), and fungi.
Alcohol sterilizers that contain 70 per cent of rubbing alcohol eliminate 99.97% (a logarithmic decrease of 3.5 percent, similar to a decibel reduction of 35 percent of the bacteria on your hands 30 seconds after their placement and 99.99% to 99.999% (a logarithmic decrease of 4 to 5) of bacteria on the hands 1 minute after their application. Hand sterilizers are more effective against bacteria and less effective against certain viruses.
Alcoholic hand sanitizers are almost completely ineffective against norovirus viruses (or Norwalk), which is the most common cause of gastroenteritis caused by the infection. Enough hand disinfectant or rubbing alcohol should be used in order to wet or cover both hands completely.
Rub the front and back of the hands and between all the fingers and limbs for about 30 seconds until the liquid, foam or gel dries.
The fingertips should also be washed well, rubbing at both conveniences. The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends washing hands by rubbing hand sanitizer, especially when the hands are clearly dirty.
The increased use of these detergents is due to their ease of use and their rapid activity in eliminating microorganisms; however, they should not be a substitute for proper hand washing unless water and soap are not available.
Frequent use of alcoholic hand sterilizers can dry off the skin unless emploids or skin moisturizers or both are added to the composition.
The effect of alcohol can be reduced in skin dryness or eliminated by adding glycerin or other emollients or both to the composition.
Clinical trials have shown that alcoholic hand sterilizers that contain emollients cause less irritation and dryness of the skin than soap or antimicrobial detergents.
Sensitive contact dermatitis, contact urinary syndrome, hypersensitivity to alcohol or additives found in liquefacture substances rarely occur.
The reduced susceptibility of irritated collapsory dermatitis has become an attraction compared to washing hands with soap and water.
Despite the efficaciency of detergents that are not water-based, they don’t disinfect hands from organic matter, but simply sterilize them.
For this reason, hand sanitizers are not as effective as soap and water in preventing the spread of many pathogens, as pathogens remain on their hands.
Alcohol-free hand sanitizer is highly effective on ingredients and composition, and historically its effect has been remarkably lower than that which contains alcohol and relies on rubbing alcohol.
More recently, formulations using benzalkonium chloride have been shown to have a cumulative and persistent antimicrobial effect after their use, as opposed to alcohol that has been shown to have a decrease in effectiveness after repeated use, possibly due to progressive adverse skin reactions.
Many individuals in low-income communities cannot afford to buy soap and use ashes or dirt instead.
Ash or dirt may be more efficacy than water alone, but it may be less effective than soap.
One concern is that if dirt or ashes are contaminated with microorganisms, they may increase the spread of the disease rather than reducing it.
Ash, like soap, is also an antiseptic factor because it forms an alkaline solution when it comes into contact with water.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has recommended the use of ash or sand as an alternative to soap when unavailable.
The correct hand washing method recommended by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control to prevent disease transmission includes the following steps:
Wret the hands with warm or cold running water.
It is recommended to use running water because existing basins may be contaminated, and at the same time the water temperature doesn’t seem to make a difference.
Make a foam on the hands by rubbing them with ample amount of soap, including the back of the hands and between the fingers and under the nails.
Soap removes germs from the skin, and studies show that people tend to wash their hands thoroughly when using only soap instead of water.
Scrub for at least 20 seconds.
The rub creates friction, which helps to remove germs from the skin, and removes rubbing for longer periods of more germs.
Rinse thoroughly under running water.
Rinsing the hands inside the pelvis may recontaminate them.
Dry with a clean towel or let it dry in the air.
Wet and wet hands are easily exposed to pollution again. The most common parts that are overlooked are the thumb, wrist, and areas between the fingers, and below the nails.
Synthesized nails and cracked nail polish may contain microorganisms.
Moisturizing lotion is often recommended to prevent dryness of the hands, as dryness of the skin can lead to damage, which may increase the risk of transmission of infection.
Different low-cost options can be provided to make it easier to wash hands in developing countries, where tap water, soap or both are not available, such as pouring water from a punctured pot or knocking with suitable holes and/or using ash if necessary. In places with limited water supplies (such as schools or rural areas in developing countries), there are solutions to rationalize water consumption, such as a “tibi tap” and other low-cost options.
Tepe faucet is a simple technique that uses a pitcher attached to a rope, and a foot-operated control arm to pour a small amount of water on the hands and a piece of soap.
Efficient hand-drying is an essential part of the hand hygiene process, but there is some debate about the most effective form of drying in public toilets.
A growing number of research suggests that paper towels are healthier than electric hand dryers found in many toilets.
In 2008, the University of Westminster in London conducted a study, under the auspices of the European Textile Symposium for the manufacture of paper towels, to compare the levels of hygiene provided by paper towels, hand dryers with warm air, and hand dryers with newer jets.
After washing and drying hands with a warm air dryer, it was found that the total number of bacteria increased on average on the soles of the fingers by 194% and on the palm of the hand by 254%.
Drying using an air jet dryer has increased the average total number of bacteria on the sole of the fingers by 42% and on the palm of the hand by 15%.
After washing and drying hands with a paper towel, the total number of bacteria on the inside of the fingers decreased by up to 76% and on the palm by 77%, and the scientists conducted tests to determine whether there was a possibility of transmission of infection for other users of the water cycle and toilet environment as a result of each type of drying methods.
A jet air dryer, which drives air outside the unit at speeds of 180 m/s (650 km/h; 400 mph), is capable of inflating microorganisms from the hands and the device, and may transmit infection to other users of the restroom and cycle environment up to 2 meters away.
Using a warm air dryer deploys microorganisms 0.25 meters from the dryer.
Paper towels have shown no significant prevalence of microorganisms. In 2005, a study by TÜV Produkt und Umwelt evaluated different hand-drying methods.
The following changes have been observed in the numbers of bacteria after drying hands:
There are many different hand dryer manufacturers, and hand dryers are horned by drying with paper towels.
Washing hands with hand sterilization wipes is an alternative while traveling due to the unavailability of soap and water.
Alcoholic hand sanitizer should contain at least 60 percent alcohol.
Medicinal handwashing became mandatory for a long time after Hungarian physician Agnats Simelves discovered its effectiveness (in 1846) in the prevention of diseases in the hospital environment.
There are electronic devices that provide feedback to remind hospital staff to wash their hands as soon as they’re forgotten.
One study found a decrease in the incidence of their use.
Medical hand washing is for at least 15 seconds, using abundant amounts of soap, water or gel to make foam and rub every part of your hands.
The hands should be rubbed together and the fingers are tangled.
If there is waste under your fingernails, you can use a bruzzed-haired brush to remove them.
Since germs may remain in the water on the hands, it is important to rinse them thoroughly and dry them with a clean towel.
After drying, the paper towel should be used to close the water (and open any exit door if necessary).
This avoids contaminating the hands back from those surfaces.
The purpose of washing hands in a health-care location is to remove pathogenic microorganisms ("spores") and to avoid their transfer.
The New England Journal of Medicine reports that not washing hands remains at unacceptable levels in most medical settings, with large numbers of doctors forgetting to routinely wash their hands before touching patients, transmitting microorganisms.
One study has showned that properly washing hands and other simple procedures can reduce the rate of catheteral-associated bloodstream infections by 66 percent, and the World Health Organization has published a newspaper explaining the way hands are washed and rubbed in the health care sectors.
The organization’s hand hygiene guidance document project can also be found on its website available for public comment.
Wetby and others conducted a relevant review.
Commercial appliances can measure and verify hand hygiene, if it is necessary to demonstrate compliance with the regulations.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has “five moments” for hand washing:
After exposure to blood/body fluids
Before the sterilization mission,
After caring for patients, the addition of antiseptic chemicals to the soap (“medicinal” or ‘anti-microbial’) adds a pesticide to the hand washing agent.
This annihilation element may be desirable before surgery or in places where antibiotic-resistant organisms are most prevalent. When one’s hands are ‘scrubbed’ for surgery, it’s necessary to provide a faucet that can be turned on and off without touching it with your hands, some chlorhexidine or iodine lotion, sterilizing towels to dry hands after washing, a sterilizing scrubbing brush, and another sterilizing tool for cleaning under the nails.
All the jewels have to be removed.
This procedure requires washing hands and forearms to the elbow, usually from 2 to 6 minutes.
The length of rubbing (10 minutes) isn't necessary.
When rinsing, you should prevent water reflucting from the forearms to the hands.
After washing the hands, the hands dry with a sterile cloth and wear a surgical garment.
To reduce the spread of germs, it’s best to wash your hands or use a hand sanitizer before and after taking care of a sick person.
To control staphylococcal infection in hospitals, the greatest benefit of manual disinfection was found to come from the first 20% of washing, and it gained a very small additional benefit when the frequency of hand cleaning increased to more than 35%.
Washing with regular soap leads to more than three times the rate of bacterial infectious diseases that are transmitted to food compared to washing with anti-bacterial soaps. By comparing the rubbing of the hands with an alcohol solution with hand washing with anti-bacterial soap for an average of 30 seconds on average, it has been found that rubbing alcohol reduces bacterial contamination by 26% more than anti-bacterial soap.
However, soap and water are more effective than alcohol-based handwashing to reduce influenza H1N1 of type A and difficulous catalytic spores of hands. Measures to improve hand hygiene in health-care settings may include educating employees about handwashing, the increased availability of huskey, and written and verbal reminders for employees.
More research is needed to find out which of these measures is most effective in different health care settings.
In developing countries, it is recognized that washing hands with soap is an essential and affordable tool for achieving good health, as well as good nutrition.
However, a lack of reliable water supplies, soap, or handwashing facilities at home, schools, and workplaces makes it difficult to come up with generic handwashing behaviors.
For example, in most rural areas of Africa you rarely find handwashing taps near each private or public toilet, although there are cheap options for building handwashing stations.
However, low hand washing rates can be caused by ingrained habits rather than a lack of soap or water.
The promotion and advocacy of handwashing with soap can influence political decisions, increase awareness of the benefits of handwashing, and change the long-term behavior of the population.
For this to function effectively, monitoring and evaluation are necessary.
A systematic review of 70 studies found that community-based approaches are effective in increasing handwashing in middle-income countries from the lower segment, while social marketing campaigns are less effective. One example of promoting handwashing in schools is the ‘three-star approach’ designed by UNICEF to encourage schools to take simple and inexpensive steps to ensure that students wash their hands with soap, among other health requirements.
When the minimum standards are met, schools can move from one star until it eventually reaches three stars.
The construction of handwashing stations can be part of the handwashing promotion campaigns that are conducted to reduce diseases and child mortality.
World Handwashing Day is yet another example of awareness-raising campaigns trying to achieve behaviour change. Due to the 2019-20 coronavirus pandemic, UNICEF has encouraged the adoption of an emoji for handwashing.
Few studies have looked at the overall cost-effectiveness of handwasting in developing countries and their relationship to disability-adjusted years.
However, one of the reviews points out that enhancing handwashing with soap is significantly more cost-effective than other water and sanitation procedures.
For the first time, the importance of handwashing for human health – especially for people in critical conditions such as mothers who have just been born or wounded soldiers in hospitals – was first recognized in the middle of the 19th century by two handculving pioneers: Hungarian physician Agnats Simelvis, who worked in Vienna in Austria, and Englishman Florence Nightingale, the “founder of modern nursing.”
Most people at the time still believed that the infection was caused by unpleasant odors called miyasmaz.
In the 1980’s 1980s, foodborne outbreaks and health care-related infections prompted the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to more effectively promote hand hygiene as an important way to prevent the spread of infection.
Swine flu outbreaks in 2009 and the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020 raised awareness in many countries about the importance of washing hands with soap to protect themselves from these infectious diseases.
For example, labels containing “the right ways to wash hands” have been hungged next to handwashing basins in public toilets and in the toilets of Germany’s office and airport buildings.
“Wasting hands from” means something to advertise one’s unwillingness to take responsibility for one’s complicity.
It originates in a passage in the Biblik in Matôte where Pantalian pylates washed his hands from the crucifixion of Jesus Christ, but has become a phrase with a much broader use in some English societies.
In McBeth’s Shakespeare play, Ms. Macbeth begins to wash her hands excessively in an attempt to purfece a spot of imagination that represents her sense of guilt for the crimes she has committed and the crimes she has urged her husband to commit.
It has also been found that people, after pointing or thinking unethical acts, tend to wash their hands more often than others, and tend to appreciate the handwasting tools more.
Moreover, those who are allowed to wash their hands after this study are less likely to participate in other “cleansing” compensatory procedures, such as volunteering.
Religions describe handwashing for both sanitary and symbolic purposes. Symbolic handwashing, using water without soap for handwashing, is part of the handwashing ritual that has emerged in many religions, including the Bahá’í, Hindu, Tefilah, hand-washing in Judaism, ablution in Christianity and ablution in Islam. Religions also require healthy handwashing, especially after certain acts.
Hinduism, Judaism, and Islam require washing hands after using the toilet.
Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikhism, Judaism and Islam require handwashing before and after every meal.
Covid-19 risk controls in the workplace
COVID-19 risk controls in the workplace are the application of occupational safety and health safety and health methodologies for risk control controls for the prevention of COVID-2019 (COVID-19).
Correct workplace risk controls are based on the workplace and functional task, based on an assessment of the risk of exposure sources, the seriousness of the disease in the community, and risk factors that threaten working individuals who may be vulnerable to COVID-19.
According to the U.S. Department of Occupational Safety and Hygiene (OHSHA), businesses with low exposure risks involve minimal contact with the public and other coworkers, a situation in which it is recommended to apply essential infection prevention measures, including handwashing, encouraging workers to stay at home if they are sick, taking into account coughing and sneezing etiquette, and maintaining normal practices for cleaning and disinfecting the working environment.
Jobs with moderate exposure risks include those that require frequent or close contact with individuals who are unknown or suspected of having COVID-19, but may become infected due to constant transmission in the community or international travel.
This includes workers who have communicated with the general public, such as in schools, labor-intensive working environments, and some large-sized retail venues.
Risk controls for this group, in addition to basic infection prevention measures, include ventilation using high-efficiency air filters, sneezing prevention barriers, and the use of personal protective equipment available in the case of dealing with someone with COVID-19.
The Occupational Safety and Hygiene Administration considers health-care workers and dead bodies who are exposed to a person known to have COVID-19 or a suspect exposed to high levels of exposure risk, which increases the high risk of exposure if workers carry out aerosol-generating procedures, collect samples from a known or suspected COVID-19 person.
Appropriate risk controls for these workers include engineering controls such as passive pressure ventilation chambers and personal protective equipment suitable for the functional task.
Covid-19 outbreaks can have multiple impacts in the workplace.
Employees may be absent from work due to the disease, the need to take care of others, or for fear of potential exposure.
Trade styles may change, in terms of both the quality of goods ordered, and the ways to obtain them (e.g
Finally, shipments of goods from geographic areas severely affected by COVID-19 may be interrupted. An infection-causing disease preparedness and response plan could be used to guide preventive measures.
Plans address the risk levels associated with different workplaces and job assignments, including sources of exposure, risk factors arising from home and community spaces, and risk factors for individual workers such as old age or chronic medical conditions.
It also determines the controls needed to address those risks, and contingency plans for situations that may arise as a result of an outbreak.
Infective disease preparedness and response plans may be subject to national and sub-national recommendations.
Objectives to respond to the outbreak include reducing transmission between employees, protecting those most at risk of negative health complications, maintaining business processes, and minimizing negative impacts on other entities in their supply chains.
The severity of the disease in the society in which the business is located affects the responses taken.
Hierarchy of risk control controls is a widely used framework in the field of occupational safety and health to classify risk control controls according to effectiveness.
Because COVID-19 risks cannot be eliminated, the most effective controls are engineering controls, followed by administrative controls and, finally, personal protective equipment.
Engineering controls involve isolating employees from work-related risks without relying on workers’ behavior, and can be the most cost-effective solution.
Administrative controls are changes in the employment policy or procedures that require action on the part of the worker or employer.
Personal protective equipment (BPE) is less effective than engineering and administrative controls, but it can help prevent certain exposure sites.
All types of personal protective equipment should be selected based on the hazard to which the worker is exposed, the correct suitability as appropriate (e.g
According to the U.S. Department of Occupational Safety and Hygiene (OHSHA), jobs that involve less exposure to risks involve minimal communication with the public and other co-workers.
Basic infection prevention measures recommended for all workplaces include frequent and complete hand washing, encouraging workers to stay at home if they are sick, taking into account cough and sneezing etiquette including covering the mouth and nose when coughing and sneezing, providing tissues and garbage containers, getting ready to work remotely or organizing work shifts successively if necessary, dissuasing workers from using other people’s tools and equipment, and maintaining routine cleaning and disinfection of the working environment.
The immediate identification and impeachment of potentially transmissible individuals is a crucial step in protecting employees, customers, visitors, and others in the workplace.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that employees who experience symptoms of acute respiratory diseases remain at home until the fever, signs of fever and any other symptoms go away for at least 24 hours without usinging anti-fever medications or other medications that improve symptoms.
According to the Department of Occupational Safety and Hygiene, jobs with an average exposure risk include those that require repetitive or close communication within a six-foot (1.8 °C) distance from unknown or suspected COVID-19-infected individuals, but may develop SARS-CoV-2 due to ongoing transmission in the community surrounding the workplace, or the person’s recent conduct of an international travel to a site where COVID-19 transmission is widespread.
This includes workers who come into contact with public audiences such as in schools, labor-intensity working environments, and some high-volume retail venues. Engineering controls and high-risk groups include the installation of high-efficiency air filters, increased ventilation rates, the installation of physical barriers such as transparent plastic sneezing protectors, and the installation of windows to serve customers who shop without leaving the car. Administrative controls for this and high-risk groups include encouraging sick workers to stay at home, replacing face-to-face meetings with virtual contacts, setting up organized working shifts successively, stopping unnecessarily traveling to sites where there is an ongoing COVID-19 outbreak, and a hand-wrenching, hand-to-working, hand-to-hand, hand-to-wear, hand-to-wrender-to-of-us-exicance, hand-in-conscousded, hand-to-usur-required work-to-and-on-one, hand-to-urgenerized work-in-consistenct, and hand-wreatting, and job-whalttering.
Employees in this risk group rarely need to use respirators.
If a person becomes ill while on an airplane, appropriate prevention controls to protect other workers and passengers include keeping the sick passenger away from others by 6 feet, assigning one member of the aircraft team to serve the sick person, providing the sick person with a face mask or asking the sick person to cover their mouth and nose with tissues when coughing or sneezing.
The crew of the aircraft should wear disposable medical gloves when dealing with a sick traveler or touching body fluids or contaminated surfaces, and may use additional personal protective equipment if the sick traveler has a fever, persistent cough or difficulty breathing.
Gloves and other disposable objects must be disposed of in a biological risk prevention bag, and polluted surfaces must be cleaned and sterilized afterwards. For commercial shipping, including cruise ships and other passenger vessels, risk controls include postponing travel in illness, self-isolation and reporting to the medical center on board immediately if a person develops fever or other symptoms while on board.
Ideally, medical follow-up should take place in an isolated person’s compartment. For schools and children’s caregiving facilities, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends a short-term closure of cleaning or disinfection if an infected person is present in the school building regardless of the spread in the community.
When there is a limit on transmission in the community ranging from limited to medium, social distancing strategies can be implemented such as the cancellation of field trips, gatherings, and other large gatherings such as physical education classes, group classes or meals in the canteen, increased distances between desks, the organization of successive attendance and departure times, the reduction of non-necessary visitors, and the use of a separate health office for children with flu-like symptoms.
When there is significant transmission of infection in the community, in addition to social distancing strategies, the extension of school departures can be taken into consideration. For law enforcement personnel who carry out routine daily activities, direct health risks are low on the part of the CDC.
Law enforcement officials who must communicate with individuals who have been confirmed or suspected of having COVID-19 are advised are advised to follow the same guidelines as emergency medical technicians, including appropriate personal protective equipment.
In the case of direct contact during detention, workers should clean and disinfect work belts and tools before reusing them with a home cleaning spray or mop, and follow unified working procedures to deal with pollution, dispose of personal protective equipment used, and collect and wash clothes.
The Department of Occupational Safety and Hygiene believes that some health-care workers and dead bodies are included in categories with high or very high risk exposure levels.
Jobs with a high level of risk include health care, support, laboratory and medical referrals who are exposed to known or suspected COVID-19 patients.
These have a very high level of risk exposure if workers perform aerosol-generating procedures, as well as collecting samples from confirmed or suspected COVID-19 patients.
Procedures for the generation of aerosols include intubation, cough induction procedures, bronchoscopy, certain dental procedures and tests, or invasive sample collection.
High-risk mortuary functions involve workers who are involved in the preparation of the bodies of people confirmed or suspected of having COVID-19 at the time of their deaths; these become extremely high-risk if they perform an autopsy. Additional engineering controls for these endangered groups include isolation rooms for well-identified or suspected COVID-19 patients, including when performing aerosolic generation procedures.
Negative pressure ventilation may be suitable in some health-care settings and the preservation of dead bodies.
Samples must be handled following level 3 biosafety precautions.
The World Health Organization (WHLO) recommends isolating incoming patients in separate waiting areas based on whether they are suspected of having COVID-19 or not. In addition to other personal protective equipment, the Occupational Safety and Health Administration recommends the use of respirators for those who work at a distance of 6 feet from patients known or suspected to have SARS-CoV-2, and those performing aerosolic-generating procedures.
In the United States, face mask respirators equipped with an N95 filter, approved by the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health, or better devices, should be used in the context of a comprehensive Written Respiratory Protection Program, which includes installation and convenience testing, training, and medical check-ups.
Other types of respirators can provide greater protection and improve the comfort of workers. The World Health Organization does not recommend the use of aprons, because COVID-19 is a respiratory disease rather than being transmitted through body fluids.
The World Health Organization only recommends the use of a surgical mask for examination staff at the point of entry.
For those who collect respiratory samples from COVID-19 patients, take care of them, or transport them without any aerosol-generating procedures, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommends the use of a surgical mask, protective glasses, or face-protecting, serbal and gloves.
If an aerosol is performed, the face mask is replaced by an N95 or FFP2 breathing mask.
Given that the global supply of personal protective equipment is inadequate, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommends reducing the need for personal protective equipment through telemedicine, physical barriers such as transparent windows, allowing only those involved in direct care to enter a room with a COVID-19 patient, using personal protective equipment necessary only for the specific task, continuing to use a breathing mask itself without removing it while caring for many patients with the same diagnosion, monitoring and coordinating the supply chain of personal protective equipment, and dissuading the use of masks for asymptomatic individuals.
From: Katherine Maher, Executive Director of the Wikimedia Foundation
All employees of the Wikimedia Foundation
[Covid 19] Easing the burden and preparing for the future
Date/Sent Time: March 14, 2020, 00:24 UTC
Licensing: CC0: No rights reserved
We found ourselves in the midst of extraordinary circumstances this month.
The Covid-19 pandemic has become something that clearly demonstrates global human interdependence and the responsibilities we have towards others.
Our history has never seen similar challenges, but we know for sure that the bestiest response is based on a kind of global empathy, collaboration, and community-building, which are entrenched at the heart of this institution.
The intense friendship and care we have seen among all our colleagues through emails, calls and conversations is an astonishing demonstration of the incredible human emotions that, fortunately, surround the way we do business.
I am very grateful and deeply proud that you are true colleagues.
Last week, someone sent me a letter expressing appreciation for our actions.
They reminded me of how critical the world’s ability to move towards Wikipedia at the moment is, and the powerful symbolism evidenced by the survival of this very important resource is available on the Internet and is available to all individuals.
Your efforts have made it possible, whether it’s through the continued functioning of the sites, our colleagues receiving salaries, or providing safety for our communities.
The world has in dire need of the information available on Wikipedia more than ever before.
It’s a moment where the positive impact in the world is not limited to our efforts, but also to the way we do it.
Because of the importance of this mission and your role in it, we will make significant adjustments in how we work together starting next week.
Modifications on the working method and schedules
As Rubén mentioned earlier, Team C met Yesterday to discuss our method and schedule for the coming days and months.
In that discussion, we looked at what we thought could be an appropriate response to what we are facing, and the ideal way to maintain the sustainability of the Foundation’s business during this period.
We wanted very enthusiastically to get rid of nervous stress and stress, and to support our long-term mission.
If you want to reduce burdens and tasks, that's fine.
All employees, contractors and contract workers:
The expected daily working period is 4 hours per day, or 20 hours per week until further notice.
If you’re able to work longer than your normal working hours, your efforts may add to the task.
However, the conditions of the world are currently unpredictable, and whether you want to take care of your loved ones, get goods from groceries or go to the doctor, your safety is our top priority.
We don't trace your time.
If you're sick, don't work.
It should be understood to allow this matter without expressing it, but we mention it as an affirmation.
You don’t have to take sick or paid vacations, just inform your manager and help your team review the calendars and schedules to make sure you cover key business areas.
(If your doctor diagnoses your COVID-19 with a positive diagnosis, please let Bryan know for managing training and testimonials so that training and certification management can help provide support and ensure that your condition receives appropriate care from management.)
Colleagues working per hour receive their full wages.
We’ve already mentioned that, and we renew this pledge of our commitments to our contractors and our hourly colleagues.
Each employee will receive their fee based on normal working hours during normal circumstances.
This includes whether the employee is sick or unable to work.
If you want to work, we support you.
Many individuals are exploiting the work to overcome the widespread pressure in the world around us.
What we can do will be fruitful in an amazing way, especially during difficult times like this.
Once again, it's about taking care of one'sself.
Let’s only ask you to get in touch with your boss so that he or she can find out what to expect and modify the procedures to adjust accordingly.
Some business is necessary.
There are also some things that we have to keep going.
Location religiosity engineering teams, HR operations, confidence, honesty, and fundraising (and others) are doing critical work, which may need more support.
We will begin a process in collaboration with all departments to assess current goals and shift our focus to supporting the elements necessary to achieve our mission.
There’s a lot of work that all of us have to do, and we’re just going to focus on the most important projects.
Going slower now, it won't hurt us later.
We don’t plan to “double the working time to fix what’s lost” after the end of the pandemic.
It’s not expected that we’ll work extra hours to meet the deadlines that have now become unrealistic.
We accept the fact that those circumstances have changed, and we will work to set new goals and timetables when appropriate.
What’s going on in Annual Planning (APP)?
To adjust to the new reality and the expectations of daily working hours, we intend to adjust the delivery schedule for our annual planning for 2020-2021.
We intend to propose an extension of our 2019-2020 plan to allow more time for budget design to allow employees to prioritize critical work, self-care and care for loved ones while adjusting the conditions of those who need or want to work on a reduced working schedule over the next few weeks.
This extension of the schedule reduces the workload of current planning and pressure across the entire organization.
We will submit our proposal to the Board of Directors next week and will inform delegates and teams of updates on the next steps as soon as confirmation is obtained.
We thank the annual planning team for its leading role in this matter.
The condition of the office, exposure and cleaning
We learned last week that one of our colleagues working in San Francisco may have been infected with COVID-19.
However, out of extreme caution and prohibition, we have hired a virus-resistant cleaning team to disinfect all surfaces in the San Francisco office.
The team has used hospital-to-degree antiviral solution to disinfect all surfaces, as well as lobbies and elevator units that lead to our floor.
The building has implemented its duty-care protocol using products that support the safety of tenants.
We feel comfortably that the office is properly ready until we decide to get back to work.
Our Washington, D.C. office is located in a building belonging to WeWork, whose COVID-19 protocol and with all members of our staff at our headquarters in Washington, D.C.
Beginning last week, our Washington, D.C. office has moved to an entirely remote location as directed jointly with our office in San Francisco.
As some of our colleagues at our headquarters in New York City know, we had a discussion about renting a place in Brooklyn.
Those discussions are still ongoing but may be delayed for some time.
Some of our colleagues are working remotely for the first time.
Our colleagues, who have been working remotely for a long time, know that it may be an adjustment, and they wanted to give you some advice:
The meeting time should be set for a maximum of one hour or a gradual increase of up to two hours.
If it is necessary to hold longer sessions, think about how to divide them over several days.
The meeting should be clearly defined, an agenda should be prepared, and materials intended for reading should be communicated in advance.
Video should make the default solution, using tools such as Google Docs and Zoom to facilitate collaboration and direct communication.
Every meeting should be facilitated, a person should monitor the conversation to find out questions and keep track of a list of speakers, and someone else to help take notes (or carry out a collaborative note-taking process).
An e-mail should be sent to technical support when comfortable headphones are needed.
The health and safety compensation program can be used to get the cost of snacks.
You can join #remoties channel in the Slack app to talk to colleagues about distributed work
The HR operations team is looking for guidance for user-friendly elements based on online meetings, to support an increase in business distributed across our organization.
Over the past week, we have asked all recipients of community grants to cancel all public events funded by Wikimedia, such as Editathon’s online site editors, until the World Health Organization (WHO) announces the end of the pandemic.
We have told them that we understand that our request for cancellations and other restrictions may make it impossible to complete agreed grant activities, and that no person will be fined due to the need to delayant or modify those objectives.
Next week we will issue additional follow-up guidance related to Wikipedia and other regional community conferences with specific topics.
The public sentiment that prevails in the global community flounds between sadness due to turbulence, and satisfaction with clarity and the ability to focus on their communities, Wikimedia and others.
Going forward with these actions, the Crisis Response Team is working on designing a page in MetaWiki to provide a space for the community to monitor the impact and monitor all communications with it.
Stay informed about COVID-19-related topics
We'll send an invitation to your calendars next Thursday, 14:00 UTC, 07:00 PTST for the staff meeting.
We’ll use this time to share additional updates, respond to questions, and spend time communicating between us.
We experience this together and are always ready to help as much as possible.
In the meantime, we can continue to obtain information from this email, and all other necessary information related to COVID-19 available at Office Wiki.
The crisis response team will keep these pages updated and keep all the information in one place.
We are also working to maintain regular contacts with personnel residing in severely affected States.
If you have any questions about travel, events, key business areas, coverage challenges or anything else you need help with, please don’t hesitate to let us know and cooperate with the Crisis Response Team.
We are always ready to provide support and communication as necessary.
If you have a confidential or sensitive issue, please send an e-mail to Brian Godden, Director of Global Human Resources Global Operations.
No such change shall be considered as an abdication of the work assigned to us or the obligations incumbent upon us.
Instead, it’s a realization at this moment, our work and our commitments need to adapt in a way that we haven’t seen in the past.
We believe these steps are necessary to support each other, so that we can continue to work and provide our employees with the support they need, and provide the world with the service they depend on.
The work we have planned to accomplish will be waiting for us when the time comes.
Now, it’s time for each person to help the other and create space for the important work that comes in the coming weeks and months.
We need all of you to make this happen, and that’s why we need all of you to take care of yourself and your families to be in your best condition when it’s time to need your efforts.
Now we ask you to wash your hands and not touch the face!
Catherine, Director of the Crisis Response Team (Amanda K, AMIV, Bryan J, Doreen Di, Gregory V, Jamie VI, Joel L, Lynette LL, Ryan M, and Tony S), and the rest of the leadership team (Grant Eye, Heather W., Jamie VI, Janine Yu, Lisa S, Rubin A, Ryan M, and Toby Inn).
Angiotensin-conversive enzyme 2 (ACE2) is associated with the outer surface (cellular membranes) of cells in the lungs, arteries, heart, kidney, as well as the intestines.
Angiotensin-conversive enzyme 2 is counteracted by reducing the amount of angiotensin 2 and increasing angiotensin (1-7); making it a promising drug in the treatment of cardiovascular diseases. It is also an introduction to some types of coronaviruses into cells.
The human version of the enzyme is often referred to as hACE2.
Angiotensin 2 is a metal enzyme that contains zinc and is located on the surface of endothelial cells and other cells.
Angiotensin-conversing enzyme protein 2 contains a peptidase M2 range that has an amino end, and a carboxylic colectrine chain that transmits renal amino acids.
The angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2 is a single-scrowing membrane protein of type 1, and has active enzyme receptors found on the surface of lung cells and other tissues.
The extracellular part of the angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2 is separated from the cross-membrane by an enzyme called chidaz, resulting in the release of a soluble protein into the bloodstream and then excreted through urine.
Angiotensin-conversive enzyme 2 is found in most organs of the body: it is associated with the cellular membrane of type 2 alveolar cells in the lung, in the intestinal cells of the small intestine, endothelial cells of the arteries and veins, as well as in the cells of the smooth muscles of arterial wall in most organs.
The messenger RNA of the angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is present in the cerebral cortex, diagram, hypothalamic area, and encephaloma.
The primary function of the angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2 is to act as a balance of the angiotensin-converting enzyme.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme stimulates the conversion of angiotensin-1 hormone into angiotensin 2 astringent of blood vessels.
In contrast, the angiotensin-converving enzyme 2 separates the carboxylic end containing the amino acid phenylalanin from angiotensin 2 (aspartite-argenin-valin-tyrosine-izolicin-hystidine-prolyne-phenylalanine) and analyzes it, converting it into angiotensin (1-7), (hystidine-aspartite-argenine-valin-tyrosine-izuliusin-hystedine-proline-phenylalanine).
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 separates into a number of other peptides, such as Des-Argenine9—Pradrykinin, Aplene, Neurotinsin, Dinorphine A, and Gerlin.
Angiotensin-conversive enzyme 2 also regulates the membrical passage of a Neutral conveyor of SLC6A19 neutral amino acids, and has a role in Hartenp's disease.
Angiotensin-convergent enzyme 2, as a cross-membrane protein, is the main entrance to cells for certain coronaviruses, which include human coronavirus NL63, SARS-CoV (the virus that causes SARS), and SARS-CoV-2 (corruption with COVID-19).
In particular, the synthesis of the Spike S1 protein of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 with the enzymatic range of the angiotensin-conversive enzyme 2 on the surface of the cells leads to cellular synthesis and the transmission of the virus and the enzyme to the internal particles located in the cells.
This insertion process needs to be configurated S protein by an acrosphanemic protease enzyme, the host's cerene2, whose inhibitor is still being tested as a potential treatment. This makes some hypothesize lowering the levels of angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2 in the cells that may help resist the infection.
In any case, several specialized associations and self-regulating bodies recommend the continued use of the usual angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers as a treatment.
A methodological study and meta-analysis published on July 11, 2012, showed that "the use of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors was accompanied by a clear reduction in the risk of pneumonia by 34% compared to the reference group."
Furthermore, the risk of pneumonia has decreased among those who have been treated with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, who have been at higher risk of developing pneumonia, especially those with a stroke or heart failure.
“The use of angiotensin-conventing enzyme inhibitors has led to a reduction in the mortality rate from pneumonia, although the results have been less potent compared to the overall risk of pneumonia.”
It is believed that recombinant angiotensin-conversive angiotensin 2 (rhACE2) will be a new treatment for acute lung injuries. It has been found to improve pulmonary blood distribution, as well as oxygen saturation in pigs suffering from acute shortness of shortness of breath induced by polysaccharides.
Recombinanted angiotensin-conventing enzyme 2 is about 10 hours, while it starts after 30 minutes, as well as continuing its effectiveness (time period) up to 24 hours.
There are many indications suggesting that the recombinant angiotensin-conversing enzyme 2 may be a promising treatment for those suffering from traditional angiotensin resonance inhibitors (RAS inhibitors), and in diseases accompanied by a spike in angiotensin 2 in the blood.
Covid-19 apps are mobile software applications specifically designed to help track people in contact in response to the 2019-20 coronavirus pandemic, meaning the identification of people (“contacts”) who may have come into contact with an infected person.
Numerous applications have been designed or proposed with formal government support in some provinces and jurisdictional areas.
Several frameworks have been put in place to design applications to track contacts.
Many have expressed concerns about privacy, in particular about systems that rely on the geographic location tracking of app users.
Less intrusive alternatives to privacy involves using Bluetooth signals to record a user’s proximity to other cell phones.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple together announced that they might integrate a functionality that supports these Bluetooth-based applications, directly into their Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the Chinese government, in collaboration with Alipay, has published an app that allows citizens to check if they’ve come into contact with people with COVID-19.
It is an app used by individuals in more than 200 Chinese cities. In Singapore, individuals use an app called TraceTogether.
A local information technology association has designed the application, issued it as an open-source application and is scheduled to be handed over to the government. North Macedonia has launched StopKorona, a Bluetooth-based application that tracks exposure to contact with individuals with potential infections and provides a rapid response to health-care authorities.
The Ministry of Communications and Technology, in cooperation with the Ministry of Health, has taken over the design of the application.
On April 14, 2020, the app was still waiting for Apple’s Google Play and App Store to be approved.
On April 12, the government stated that the contact tracing app was in advanced development, and may become available for distribution within weeks.
Australia and New Zealand alike are considering designing apps based on Singapore’s TraceTogether app, as well as the Blue Treas protocol.Russia intends to roll out an app that uses the geospherical perimeter of patients diagnosed with COVID-19 residing in Moscow and is designed to make sure they don’t leave home.
Ross Anderson, a professor of security engineering at the University of Cambridge, has mentioned a number of potential practical problems surrounding application-based systems, including false-positive cases and potential inefficiencies if the use of applications is limited to only a small segment of the population.
To deal with concerns related to the spread of misleading or harmful coronavirus applications, Apple imposed restrictions on the types of organizations that can add their coronavirus-associated applications to its app store, limiting this to “official” or other reputable organizations only.
Google and Amazon have implemented similar restrictions.
Privacy advocates have expressed concerns about the consequences of mass surveillance resulting from the use of coronavirus applications, especially with regard to whether the monitoring infrastructure designed to deal with the coronavirus pandemic will disappear as soon as the threat expires.
Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations have issued a statement calling for restrictions on this type of surveillance.
The organizations also announced eight conditions for government projects:
Surveillance should be “regular, necessary and proportionate”;
Extendents for monitoring and control should include clauses relating to the determination of the time of their discontinuation;
The use of data should be limited to COVID-19 purposes;
The security of data and the confidentiality of the identity of its owners should be protected and established on the basis of evidence;
Digital surveillance should avoid increasing the intensity of discrimination and marginalization;
Any sharing of data should be identified with third parties in law;
Factors of protection against abuse and the protection of the rights of citizens should be provided to address abuses;
“Effective Engagement” is required from all relevant stakeholders, including public health experts and marginalized groups. Chaos Computer Club (CCC), Germany’s Reporter ohne Grenzen (RSF) has also released checklists.
The proposed Google/Apple plan aims to deal with the problem of continuous monitoring by removing the tracking mechanism from hardware operating systems as soon as the need arises.
Some countries have used network-based location tracking instead of using apps, eliminating both the need to download an app and making it possible to avoid tracking.
In Israel, officials have adopted network-based tracking.
But network-based solutions that have access to primary location data have significant potential privacy issues.
However, not all centralized server systems need access to personal site data; a number of privacy preservation systems have been created that only use central intercommunication servers (see section below).
In South Korea, officials used a non-application-based system to carry out contact tracing.
Instead of using a dedicated application, the system collects tracking information from various sources, including mobile tracking data and card transaction data, and incorporates this data to send text notifications to individuals with potential infections.
In addition to using this information to alert potential contacts, the government has made site information publicly available as well, which has been allowed due to wide-ranging changes in information privacy laws following the outbreak of the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (MERS) in that country.
This information is publicly available through a number of applications and websites. Countries, including Germany, have considered the use of both centralized and privacy-preserving systems.
On April 6, 2020, details about this have not yet been released.
On April 7, 2020, more than a dozen groups of experts have accessed privacy-friendly solutions, such as low-energy Bluetooth (PLE) to record the user’s proximity to other cell phones.
However, BPBT was a coordinating effort that contained both centralized and decentralized approaches, not an individual protocol. Decentralized protocols include decentralized approach-to-privacy (DPPT/DP-3T), temporary contact numbers (TCN, formerly known as CIN numbers), privacy sensitive protocols, mobile contact tracking mechanisms (BACT) and others.
In these protocols, the personal identification data does not leave the device, and all matches occur on the device.
MIT Media Lab’s privacy group has been developing SafePaths, a platform to use privacy-preserving technologies when collecting site data or juncturing paths and utilizing them to track the spread of COVID-19.
It builds on research learned from the “Evil Face of Applications: Preserving Personal Privactivity in the Epidemica” released in March 2020. Enigma MPC’s SafeTrace platform, a privacy technology developer that originally originated in MIT Media Lab, is another similar effort.
SafeTrace uses secure device technologies to allow users to share sensitive location and health data with users and other administrators without compromising data privacy.
On April 5, 2020, the Global Contact Number Tracking Coalition was founded by groups whose focus is on what was essentially a similar approach and highly interconnected protocols, whose goal was to reduce dispersion and enable global interchangeability between tracking and alerting applications, a key trait to achieving widespread use.
On April 9, 2020, the Singaporean government announced that it had made the Bleu Trees protocol, which is used by the official government’s application, open source.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, the two companies that control the Android and iOS mobile operating systems, announced an initiative to track their contacts, claiming that they would maintain privacy based on the combination of low-energy low-power Bluetooth technology and privacy-conserving encryption.
They also published the specifications of the main techniques used in the system.
According to Apple and Google, the goal is to introduce the system in three phases:
Introducting tools to enable governments to create official privacy-keeping applications to track the coronavirus
Integrating this function directly into Android and iOS, Google and Apple also plan to resolve ongoing usage and monitoring issues by distributing systems first via operating system updates and then by removing them in the same way once the threat is over.
Remodeling the drug (also known as re-use, reallocation, changing the goal or therapeutic transformation of the drug) is the redefinition of the use of an approved drug for the treatment of a different disease or medical condition different from what was originally designed for its treatment.
This is one of the scientific research methods that researchers are currently pursuing to develop safe and effective treatments for COVID-19.
Other research trends include the development of the COVID-19 vaccine and the transmission of contrast plasma. SARS-CoV-2 contains approximately 66 drug-treatable proteins, each of which contains multi-linking sites.
Analyzing these link sites provides a reasonable plan on the development of an effective antiviral drug that eliminates COVID-19 proteins.
Among SARS-CoV-2’s critical target proteins are two-door-like protease, RNA-based polymerase based on RNA, helicase, S-protein, and phosphatase ribo for diphosphosphate.
Hussain AA and others have studied several candidate compounds that have subsequently undergone improvement and analysis because of the similarity of their structure to approved drugs of the highest similarity in order to accelerate the development of a potent SARS-COV-2 drug in its pre-clinical study to recommend it in the design of a clinical study.
Chloroquine is a malaria treatment drug that doctors also use to treat certain autoimmune diseases.
On 18 March, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that chloroquine and associated hydroxychloroquine could be among four drugs under study as part of a clinical solidarity trial.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo has announced that New York State's trials of chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine may begin on March 24. On March 28, the Food and Drug Administration authorized the use of hydroxychloroquine sulfate and chloroquine phosphate under an emergency use license (EUA).
The treatment was not approved by the Food and Drug Administration's clinical trial process and was licensed under an emergency use license only as an experimental treatment for emergency use to treat patients who have been admitted to the hospital but are unable to receive treatment in the clinical trial.
CDC has announced that “hydroxychloroquine has not yet been identified for the prevention, treatment, dosing, or period of SARS-CoV-2 infection.”
Doctors said they were using the drug when “there’s no other option.”
A Turkish research team in Istanbul is currently conducting a small study on the use of chlorquine in addition to zinc, vitamin A, vitamin C and vitamin D.
Significant studies are underway at Duke University and the University of Oxford.
The Langone School of Medicine at New York University is experimenting on the safety and effectiveness of the preventive use of hydroxychloroquine.
Chinese clinical trials in Wuhan and Shenzhen have claimed that Vavipiravir was "clearingly effective."
Of the 35 patients in Schengen, the test results were negative on an average of 4 days, while the duration of the disease was 11 days in 45 patients who did not receive it.
In a study conducted in Wuhan on 240 patients with pneumonia, half of whom were administered favipiravir and the other half received umifenovir.
The Italian Medicines Agency has alerted people to the fact that the existing evidence supporting the drug is incomplete and preliminary.
On 2 April, Germany announced that it would buy the drug from Japan for its reserve stock, and would use the military to deliver the drug to university hospitals, where the drug would be used to treat COVID-19 patients.
According to South China’s morning newspaper, Shinzo Abe has held preliminary talks with the Trump administration about buying the drug.The drug may be less effective in treating the most severe cases as the virus has already multiplied.
Its use may not be safe for pregnant women or women who want to get pregnant.
One study that used lopinavir/retonavire (Calitra), a combination of antivirals for lupinavir and retonavir, stated that the results “didn’t detect any benefit.”
The drugs were designed to inhibit HIV to prevent it from copping by binding to protease.
A team of researchers at the University of Colorado is trying to modify the drugs to reach a compound associated with SARS-CoV-2 protease.The scientific community is witnessing a state of criticism about directing resources to redefining the purposes of drugs specifically designed for the treatment of HIV/AIDS.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has added Lupinavir/Ritonavire to the experience of international solidarity.
Gilead Sciences has developed remedisivir as a treatment for Ebola virus disease and Marburg virus infection. Gilead Sciences later discovered that remedisivir is characterized by antiviral activity in the laboratory against many nematode viruses, mucous viruses and coronaviruses.
One of the problems associated with antiviral therapy is the development of resistance through transitions that can lead to more serious diseases and transmission.
Some early pre-clinical studies suggest that remdecivir may be characterized by an elevated genetic barrier to resistance. There are several current multiple clinical trials, including two conducted by University of Cleveland hospitals; one revolves around those with the moderate disease, and the other for patients with the most severe disease.
There are three clinical trials on antiviral vitamin C on patients who have been hospitalized and suffering from an acute illness due to COVID-19, as well as two controlled trials using a placebo (China, Canada) and an uncontrolled trial (Italy).
The state of New York began experiments on the effect of the azithromycin antibiotic on March 24, 2020.
Japan’s National Center for Public Health and Medicine (NCGM) is planning to carry out a clinical trial on the use of Teijin’s Alfesco (cyclisonide), an inhaled corticosteroid for asthma, to treat patients infected with the novel coronavirus before the onset of symptoms.
A second-stage trial is currently underway using an angiotensin-transforming enzyme for 200 patients who are required to join the trial of acute cases hospitalized in Denmark, Germany, and Austria to determine the effectiveness of the treatment.
Researchers from the Montreal Heart Institute in Canada are currently studying the role of colchicin in reducing inflammation and pulmonary complications in patients with mild symptoms of COVID-19.
The study, called Colcorona, includes 6,000 adults aged 40 and over who have been diagnosed with COVID-19 and have had mild symptoms without the need for hospitalization.
Pregnant women who are breastfed or who don’t have an effective contraceptive are not eligible.
Many anticoagulants have also been tested in Italy.
Low-molecular heparin is currently widely used to treat patients, which would require the Italian Medicines Agency to disseminate guidance on its use.
A multi-central study was commissioned in Italy on 300 patients to research the use of sodium inoxaparine in doses for the prevention of diseases and therapeutic doses on April 14.
Because SARS-CoV-2 was a major scientific focus focused on the reuse of approved antiviral drugs designed to deal with previous outbreaks such as Mars, SARS, and the West Nile virus.
Ribavirin: Specialists recommended the use of ribavirin for the treatment of COVID-19 according to the 7th version of the Chinese directives
Omevinovir: Specialists recommended the use of Omefenovir to treat COVID-19 according to the 7th version of the Chinese directives
Some antibiotics that specialists have identified their potential for reusability as treatments for COVID-19 include:
Tusilizumab (Interlukin-6 anti-receptor): approved by China.
Experiments in Italy and Chinea as well. And see Tosilizumab#Covid-19.
The COVID-19 Vaccine is a presumed vaccine against Coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19)
Although no vaccines have been completed for clinical trials, many attempts are under way to develop this vaccine.
In late February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) said it did not expect a vaccine against SARS-CoV-2, the causative virus, to be available in less than 18 months.
There were five candidate vaccines in the first phase of safety studies in April.
Covid-19 was identified in December 2019.
A major outbreak has spread around the world in 2020, resulting in significant investments and research activity to develop a vaccine.
Many organizations use published genomes to develop potential vaccines against SARS-CoV-2.
The requirements of the Coalition for Epidemiological Preparedness Initiative for Vaccine Development, announced in April, are speed, manufacturing capacity, large-scale deployment and global reach.
In April, scientists of the Epidemiological Preparedness Innovations Coalition reported that 10 different technical platforms were under research and development during early 2020 to create an effective COVID-19 vaccine.
The platform’s main objectives that have progressed to the first phase of safety studies include:
DNA (DNA degenerated rebose acid and RNA) (first stage developer and candidate vaccine: Moderna, mRNA-1273)
Viral vector (phase I developer and candidate vaccine: Moderna, type 5 adenovirus vector)
According to the scientists of the Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations in April, there are 115 total vaccine candidates in the early stages of development, with 78 active projects confirmed (79, according to the Milken Institute), and 37 other projects have been announced, but with little public information (presumably in the planning stage or being designed).
The Phase I and II trials conduct an initial safety and immension test, usually randomized, overlooked, in multiple locations, with more accurate and effective doses.
Phase III trials typically involve a larger number of participants, including the reference group, and a test for the vaccine’s efficacy to prevent the disease, while observing the adverse effects of the optimal dose.
Out of 79 candidate vaccines under active development (confirmed as of early April 2020), 74 were not yet under human evaluation (remaining under "pre-clinical" research).
Approximately January 24, 2020 in Australia, the University of Queensland has announced that it is investigating the possibility of a molecular synaptic vaccine that will genetically modify viral proteins in order to stimulate an immune reaction.
Approximately January 24, 2020 in Canada, the International Vaccine Center (VIDO-InterVac) at the University of Saskatchewan has announced the start of work on a vaccine, which aims to start testing in humans in 2021.
Vaccine development projects were announced at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention on January 26, 2020, and at the University of Hong Kong on January 28.
Approximately January 29, 2020, Janssen Pharmaceutical Companies, led by Hanniki Schweitimaker, announced that they had started working on the development of a vaccine.
Janssen is involved in the development of an oral vaccine with its biotechnology partner, Vaxart.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced a manufacturing partnership with Vaxart to develop the vaccine.
On February 8, 2020, OncoGen's laboratory in Romania published a study on the design of a vaccine with technology similar to that used in the treatment of cancer with the novel antigen vaccine.
On 25 March, the head of the Research Institute announced that they had completed the manufacture of the vaccine and had begun testing.
On February 27, 2020, NuGenerex Immuno-Oncology, a subsidiary of Generex, announced that it had started a vaccine project to synthesize the Ii-Key peptide vaccine against COVID-19.
They wanted to produce a candidate vaccine that could be tested in humans “within 90 days.”
On March 5, 2020, the University of Washington in St. Louis announced its projects to develop a vaccine.
On March 5, 2020, the U.S. Army Medical and Materiel Research Command at Fort Derrick and the Walter Reed Military Research Institute in Silver Spring, both in West Maryland, announced that they were working on a vaccine.
Approximately March 10, 2020, Emergent Biosolutions announced that it had formed a team with Novavax Inc.
In the development and manufacture of a vaccine.
The partners also announced plans for pre-clinical tests and a Phase I clinical trial by July 2020.
On March 12, 2020, India’s Ministry of Health announced that it was working with 11 isolated cases, and that even as it accelerates the pace of work, it will take at least one and a half to about two years to develop a vaccine.
On March 12, 2020, Medicago, a biotechnology company in Québéc Pekébék City, reported that it was developing a particle similar to the coronavirus with partial funding from the Canadian Institutes of Health Research.
The candidate vaccine undergoes laboratory research, with plans for testing in humans in July or August 2020.
Earlier that week, the Guardian reported that U.S. President Donald Trump offered CureVac “significant sums of money” for exclusive access to the COVID-19 vaccine, which the German government protested.
On March 17, 2020, the U.S. pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced a partnership with Germany’s BioNTech to jointly develop a vaccine based on messenger RNA.
BNT162-indificated RNA vaccine is currently undergoing pre-clinical testing with clinical trials expected to begin in April 2020.
In Italy on March 17, 2020, Takis Biotech, an Italian biotechnology company, announced that it would receive pre-clinical testing results in April 2020 and its final candidate vaccine could begin testing in humans by autumn.
In France on March 19, 2020, the Coalition for Epidemiological Preparedness Innovations (CIPA) announced an investment of US$4.9 million in a consortium of COVID-19 vaccine research that includes the Pasteur Institute, Themis Bioscience (Vienna, Austria) and the University of Pittsburgh, pushing the total investment of the Alliance for Epidemiological Innovations in the development of the COVID-19 vaccine to US$29 million.
Coalition Partners on Epidemiological Preparedness Innovations for COVID-19 Vaccine Development are Moderna, Curevac, Novavax, the University of Hong Kong, the University of Oxford and the University of Queensland.
On March 20, 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had started testing in animals for six different candidate vaccines.
On March 20, 2020, Imperial College researchers in London announced that they are developing a self-inflatted RNA vaccine for COVID-19.
The nominated vaccine was developed within 14 days of receiving the sequence from China.
In late March, the Canadian government announced funding of C$275 million for 96 research projects on COVID-19 medical measures, including several candidate vaccines at Canadian companies and universities, such as Medicago Initiatives and the University of Saskatchewan.
Around the same time, the Canadian government has announced C$192 million specifically to develop the COVID-19 vaccine, with plans to create a national “vaccine bank” of several new vaccines that could be used in the event of another coronavirus outbreak.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at Pittsburgh University School of Medicine reported tests for PittCoVacc, a possible COVID-19 vaccine in mice, where they stated that "SARS-CoV-2 subunit S1 subunit vaccines administered with microscopic needles have provoked strong responses to specific antibodies [in mice] that have been evident starting from 2 weeks after vaccination."
In Canada on April 16, 2020, the Faculty of Pharmacy at the University of Waterloo announced the design of a candidate vaccine based on degenerated RNA as a possible nasal spray.
Using phystems, degenerated RNA will be designed to replicate within human bacteria to produce harmless particles similar to the virus, which may stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies for SARS-CoV-2.
In March 2020, the U.S. government, industry, and three universities assembled resources to access IBM’s supercomputers, along with cloud computing resources from Hewlett Packard Enterprise, Amazon, Microsoft, and Google.
Some vaccines have different effects, and are also called unspecified effects.
This means they can have benefits that go beyond the disease they’re fighting.
Another randomized trial in Australia seeks to register 4,170 health-care workers.
Vaccines under development are unlikely to be safe or effective.
Early research to assess the effectiveness of the vaccine using specific animal models for COVID-19, such as genetically modified mice with an angiotensin-converting enzyme 2, other laboratory animals, and non-humanic primates, points to the need for level 3 biological safety containment measures to deal with living viruses, and international coordination to ensure standardized safety measures.
Vaccines against SARS and MERS have been tested on non-human animal models.
As of 2020, there is no Preventive Treatment or Vaccine for SARS that has been shown to be both safe and effective in humans.
According to research papers published in 2005 and 2006, the identification and development of new vaccines and drugs for SARS treatment was a priority for governments and public health agencies around the world. There is also no approved vaccine against MERS.
When March went viral, it was believed that existing research on SARS had provided a useful model for the development of vaccines and treatments against Mars-Cove infection.
As of March 2020, there was one MERS vaccine (based on hypoxian RNA) that completed the first phase of clinical trials in humans, and three other vaccines are under development, all of which were vaccines oriented to the virus, two adenoviral-directed vaccines (ChAdOx1-MERS, BVRS-GamVac), and one MVA-MERS-S-directed vaccine.
Social media posts have sparked a conspiracy theory that claims COVID-19 virus was known and that the vaccine has already been available.
Patents cited by several social media posts point to existing patents for genetic sequencing and vaccines for other strains of coronaviruses such as SARS-causing coronavirus.
Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) is caused by Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Common symptoms include fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Other symptoms may include fatigue, muscle pain, diarrhea, sore throat, loss of smell, and abdominal pain.
The period between exposure and the appearance of symptoms is usually about five days, but may range from two to fourteen days.
Although most cases have mild symptoms, some develop viral pneumonia and multiple organ failure.
On 17 April 2020, more than 2.24 million people were reported infected in 210 countries and territories, resulting in more than 15,3,000 deaths.
More than 568,000 people have recovered. The virus is mainly spread among people during mixing, often through spready scattered when coughing, sneezing, or talking.
When splashed during the exhalation process, it usually falls to the ground or on surfaces rather than infectious over long distances.
People may also be injured when touching a contaminated surface and then touching their eyes, noses, or mouths.
Virus can survive on surfaces up to 72 hours.
It is most contagious in the first three days of the onset of symptoms, although it may spread before the onset of symptoms and in the late stages of the disease.The standard method of diagnosis is the polymerase sequencing reaction to instantaneous retraction (RRT-PCR) through a nasopharyngeal swab.
Masks are recommended for those who are suspected of being infected with the virus and their caregivers.
Recommendations for the use of masks by the general public vary, with some authorities recommending against their use, some of which are otherwise, and others that are necessary for their use.
At the moment, there is no specific vaccine or antiviral treatment for COVID-19.
Transmission of the disease has been reported at the local level in most countries in all six WHO regions.
There may be no symptomy on people infected with the virus or flu-like symptoms such as fever, cough, fatigue and shortness of breath.
Immediately medical care is recommended if there are emergency symptoms such as difficulty breathing, pain or constant pressure in the chest area, disorder, difficulty waking up, cyanosis of the face or lips.
In less common cases, upper respiratory tract symptoms such as sneezing, runny nose or sore throat may be observed.
Gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, vomiting and diarrhea have been observed in varying proportions.
Initially, some cases in China only suffered from chest tightness and palpitations.
In some, the disease may develop into pneumonia, multiple organ failure, and death.
It's called the custody period.
Incubation period for COVID-19 is usually five to six days, but may range from 2 to 14 days.
97.5% of those who show symptoms will do so within 11.5 days of the infestion.
To date, the role of carriers of the disease has not yet been fully recognized without symptoms in transmission; however, preliminary evidence indicates that they may contribute to the spread of the disease.
The percentage of people who are asymptomatically affected is currently unknown and is under study, and the Korea Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (KCDC) reported that 20 percent of all confirmed cases showed no symptoms during their hospital stay.
China's National Health Commission (NHCC) began including asymptomatic cases in the daily toll on 1 April; of the 166 cases on that day, 130 (78 per cent) had no symptomatic cases on the test.
Phlegm and saliva may carry large proportions of the virus.
Speaking loudly produces more spray than speaking normally.
A study in Singapore has shown that coughing without covering the mouth may lead to a splatter to a distance of up to 4.5 meters (15 feet).
Although the virus is not usually airborne, the National Academy of Sciences has indicated that it can be transported by bioaerobolis, and the examination of samples of air collectors found in the lobby outside the rooms of individuals has yielded positive results for viral RNA.
Some medical procedures, such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CBR), may cause respiratory secretions to be released in the form of a spray, and then the virus spreads through the air.
Although there are concerns that it may spread through feces, it is believed that the risk is limited. The virus is more contagious when people experience symptoms; while it may spread before the onset of symptoms, the risk is lower.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) says that despite some ambiguity about how easily the disease spreads, one person can encroach from two to three other people in general. The virus remains stuck on surfaces from hours to days.
Specifically, it was found that the virus remained present for one day on cardboard, up to three days on plastic (polypropylene), stainless steel (AISI 304), and up to four hours on copper 99%.
However, this varies depending on the humidity and temperature.
Soap and disinfectants are also effective on the condition that they are used correctly; soap dissolves and stops the protective fatty membrane of the virus, as well as ridding the skin and other surfaces from it.
