Preventive measures to reduce the odds of injury include staying at home, avoiding crowded places, washing hands with soap and water frequently for at least 20 seconds, exercising good respiratory hygiene, and avoiding touching the eye, nose or mouth with unwashed hands.
CDC recommends covering the mouth and nose with a handkerchief when coughing or sneezing, and it is recommended to use the inside of the elbow if a handkerchief is not available.
It is recommended to clean your hands thoroughly after any cough or sneezing.
CDC has recommended the use of canvas face coverings in public places, in part aimed at reducing the transmission of asymptomatic infections. Social distancing strategies are aimed at reducing the mixing of people with large groups by closing schools and workplaces, restricting travel and eliminating large public gatherings.
Spacing guidelines also include that people walk away from each other by at least 6 feet (1.8 m).
There is no drug known to be effective in preventing COVID-19. Because a vaccine is not expected to be invented until 2021 at the earliest, the main part of controlling COVID-19 is trying to reduce the peak of the epidemic, which is known as “flattening the curve.”
CDC also advises people to wash their hands frequently with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when the hands are clearly dirty, before eating, and after cleaning the nose, coughing or sneezing.
It is also recommended to use a hand sanitizer, which contains at least 60% alcohol, when only soap and water are not available. For areas where commercial hand sanitizers are not readily available, the World Health Organization offers two formulations for local production.
Antimicrobial activity originates in these two formulations of ethanol or isopropanol.
Hydrogen peroxide is used to help eliminate bacterial spores in alcohol; it is “not an effective substance for disinfecting hands.”
Glycerol is added as moisturizing.
People receive supportive care, which may include fluid therapy, oxygen support, and the subsidy of other affected vital organs.
CDC recommends that those who are suspected of contracting the virus wear a simple face mask.
Extracorporeal membrane oxygen (ECMO) is used to treat the problem of respiratory failure, but its benefits are still being studied.
Personal hygiene is recommended and reliance on a healthy lifestyle and diet to improve immunity.
Supportive therapies may benefit those with mild symptoms during the early stage of infection. The World Health Organization (WHO) and the National Health Commission of Chinesea have published recommendations for the care of those who have been hospitalized for COVID-19.
Intensive caregivers and pulmonologists in the United States have collected treatment recommendations from a variety of bodies in a free resource, a critical care care book available online.
As of April 2020, no specific treatment for COVID-19 has been available.
As for symptoms, some specialized physicians recommend using paracetamol (acetaminophen) instead of ibuprofen as the first treatment.
The necessary precautions should be taken to minimize the risk of transmission of the virus, especially in health-care settings, when carrying out procedures that may result in spray, such as intimidation or manual breathing.
For healthcare professionals who take care of people with COVID-19, CDC recommends placing a person in an isolation room of airborne infection (AIIR) as well as using standard precautions, mixing precautions and precautions for airborne diseases.
Recommended tools: personal protective equipment aprons, respirator or face mask, eye protector, and medical gloves. Respirators (instead of face masks) are preferred if available.
Respirators N95 has been adopted for industrial facilities, but the Food and Drug Administration has authorized the use of masks under an emergency use license (EUA).
They are designed to protect against airborne particles such as dust, but their effectiveness against a particular biological agent is uncertain in the case of uses for other unspecified purposes.
When masks aren’t available, CDC recommends using face shields or homemade masks as the last solution.
The most cases of COVID-19 aren’t degraded to the extent that they require artificial respiration or alternatives, but in some cases they need to.
Respiratory support for those suffering from COVID-19-related respiratory failure for those in hospitals is actively studied, with some evidence that intubation can be avoided using a high-flow nasal canal or dual-level positive pressure of the airway.
It is not known whether either of these two options is of the same benefit to those with a critical condition.
Some doctors prefer to continue using invasive ventilation when available because this technique reduces the spread of aerosol particles compared to high-flow nasal vesicles. Critical conditions abound among the elderly (expanded over 60 years of age, especially those over 80 years of age).
Many developed countries don’t have enough hospital beds relative to the population, restricting the health system’s ability to deal with the sudden rise in the number of risky COVID-19 cases requiring hospitalization.
One study in China found that 5% of cases were admitted to intensive care units, 2.3% required artificial respiratory support, and 1.4% died.
In China, nearly 30 percent of those hospitalized for COVID-19 eventually enter intensive care units.
Ventilation becomes more complicated with the development of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) in people with COVID-19 and the oxygen supply becomes more difficult.
Respirators are required to have pressure controls and elevated ventilative pressure to maximize the delivery of oxygen while minimizing the risk of lung injury associated with the respirator and pneumothorax.
Expirated high positive expiratory pressure may not be available in older respirators.
The search for potential therapies began in January 2020, and several antiviral drugs are in clinical trials.
Remedcivir seems to be the most evangelizing one.
Although new drugs may take until 2021 to evolve, many of the drugs that are being tested have already been approved for other uses or are already in an advanced stage of testing.
Antiviral drugs can be tested on those with a critical condition.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has recommended volunteers to participate in experiments on the effectiveness and safety of potential therapies. The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has granted a temporary license to use convalescence plasma as an empirical treatment in cases where a person’s life is seriously or directly threatened.
He has not undergone the clinical studies necessary to prove that it is safe and effective against the disease.
In February 2020, China launched a mobile application to deal with the outbreak.
Users are required to enter the name and ID number.
The app can detect ‘contacts’ using monitoring data and thus the risk of possible infection.
Each user can also check the status of three other users.
If a potential risk is discovered, the app not only recommends self-quarantine, but also alerts local health officials. Large data analyses are used for mobile data, facial recognition technology, mobile phone tracking, and artificial intelligence to track the infected and mixed in South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore.
In March 2020, the Israeli government enabled the security services to track mobile phone data of those who were presupposed to be infected with coronavirus.
Action has been taken to quarantine and protect those who are likely to associate with injured citizens.
Also in March 2020, Deutsche Telekom shared blockchained phone location data with Germany’s federal government agency, the Robert Koch Institute, for the purposes of researching and preventing the spread of the virus.
Russia used facial recognition technology to detect quarantine violators.
Italy’s regional health commissioner, Giulio Gallera, said mobile phone companies had informed him that “40 percent of people are still moving everywhere.”
The German government conducted a 48-hour hackathon on the weekend in which more than 42,000 people took part.
The President of Estonia, Ms. Kirstie Caliulade, also made a global call to present innovative solutions to prevent the spread of the coronavirus.
