As of March 2020, it was not known whether the previous infection earned those recovering from the disease effective and long-lasting immunity.
Acquisition of immunology is seen as probable, based on the behavior of other coronaviruses, but it has been announced that there have been cases recovered from COVID-19 and subsequently contracted again.
It is believed that these conditions are exacerbated if the infection persists rather than when it is repeated.
It is believed that the virus is natural and has animal origin, in view of the spread of infection.
The actual origin is unknown, but by December 2019, the spread of the infection was largely the result of human-to-human transmission.
A study of the first 41 confirmed cases of COVID-19, published in January 2020 in The Lancet, revealed that December 1, 2019 is the first date for the appearance of symptoms.
According to official publications of the World Health Organization (WHO), December 8, 2019 is the first date for the appearance of symptoms.
Numerous measures are usually taken to determine the number of deaths.
These numbers vary by region and over time and are affected by the size of tests, the quality of the health care system, treatment options, the time taken from the onset of the outbreak and the characteristics of the population such as age, gender and public health.
In late 2019, the World Health Organization (WHWTO) devoted the 10th review of the International Classification of Dermitology to the emergency code U 07.1 to laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection deaths and code 0.02 for COVID-19 deaths diagnosed with SARS-CoV-2 clinically or epidemiologically without confirming infection with the virus in the laboratory.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, global casualty deaths are 6.9% (153,822/2,240,191) as of April 17, 2020.
The number varies depending on the region. Other metrics include the CFR mortality rate, which reflects the percentage of those who have been diagnosed and have died from an IFR disease, which reflects the percentage of those infected (inspected and undetected) and those who have died of an illness.
These statistics do not have a time frame, and they also follow a specific population from injury until the fate of the condition is known.
Although antibodies may not be produced by the bodies of all those infected, their presence may provide information about the number of infected people.
