Pandemic impact and resulting mortality vary between men and women.
The mortality rate is higher among men in studies conducted in China and Italy.
The maximum risk for men is at 50 years of age, with the difference between men and women fading at the age of only 90.
In China, the mortality rate was 2.8 per cent in men and 1.7 per cent in women.
The nuances of these sexual differences are unknown, but genetic and behavioral factors may be a cause.
Immuning differences based on gender, the lack of prevalence of smoking among women and men’s conjunctival conditions such as hypertension at a younger age may have contributed to a higher mortality rate in men.
In Europe, 57% of those infected were men and 72% of those who died from COVID-19 were men.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government does not track sex-disaggregated data on COVID-19 infections.
Research has shown that viral diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza and SARS affect men and women differently.
The highest percentage of health workers, especially nurses, are women, and they have an increased risk of contracting the virus.
The World Health Organization (WHO) announced on 11 February 2020 that the official name of the disease is "COVID-19".
The Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO), Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, explained that Ko is an abbreviation of Coronavirus, an abbreviation of the novel virus and an abbreviation for the disease and 19 refers to the year of the discovery of the outbreak for the first time: 31 December 2019.
The name was chosen to avoid indicating a specific geographical location (e.g. China), animal species or group of people, in line with the international recommendations for the designation aimed at preventing stigmancy.The virus that causes COVID-19 is called the Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
In addition, the World Health Organization (WHO) uses “COVID-19” and “Covid-19 Responsible Virus” in public data.
The disease and the virus are usually referred to as “coronavirus.”
During the first outbreak in Wuhan, China, the virus and the disease were commonly referred to as “coronavirus” and ‘Wuhan coronavirus’.
In January 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommended the use of the 2019 novel coronavirus and acute respiratory disease associated with the novel coronavirus 2019 as temporary names for the virus and the disease in accordance with the 2015 guidelines prohibiting the use of sites in the names of diseases and viruses.
The official names Covid-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were released on February 11, 2020.
Due to limited capabilities in standard supply chains, digital industrial companies issue healthcare materials such as nasal swabs and respirator parts.
One example, when an Italian hospital urgently needed a respirator valve, and the supplier couldn’t deliver on time, a local start-up reversed its architecture and released the required 100 valves in one night.
After the first outbreak of COVID-19, conspiracy theories, misinformation and misleading emerged in relation to the disease’s origin, scope, prevention, treatment and other aspects and spread rapidly online.
It seems that humans are able to transmit the virus to some other animals.
The study failed to find evidence that the virus multiplied in pigs, ducks, and chickens.
There are no approved drugs or vaccines to treat the disease.
Government institutions, academia and industry researchers conduct international research on COVID-19 vaccines and medicines.
In March, the World Health Organization (WHO) launched a “solidarity experiment” to assess the therapeutic effects of four existing antiviral compounds with the most promising efficacies.
A vaccine is not available, but different bodies are actively developing candidate vaccines.
Previous studies on SARS-CoV have been used because it resembles SARS-CoV-2 in the use of ACE2 receptors to enter human cells.
Three vaccination strategies are under study.
Firstly, the researchers aim to create an entire vaccine for the virus.
The use of such a virus, whether inactive or dead, aims to provoke an immediate immune response in the human body against a new COVID-19 infection.
The second strategy, the partial units vaccine, aims to create a vaccine that makes the immune system sensitive to some of the partial units of the virus.
In the case of SARS-CoV-2, this research focuses on Spike-S protein, which helps the virus break into the ACE2 receptors.
The third strategy is DNA vaccines (DNA or RNA vaccines, which is a new technique for innovating a vaccine).
Experimental vaccines resulting from any of these strategies must be tested to ensure their safety and efficaciency. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of a vaccine began on four volunteers in Seattle.
The vaccine contains harmless genomic bladder copied from the virus that causes the disease. The antibody-dependent provocation has been cited as a potential challenge facing the creation of the SARS-CoV-2 vaccine, but this is controversial.
There are more than 300 active clinical trials underway starting in April 2020.
Seven experiments have already been carried out for malaria-approved therapies, of which four are related to hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Antiretroviral drugs proposed to be reused in the treatment of the new disease make up most of Chinese research, through nine Phase III trials on remedcivir across several countries whose results are due to be reported by the end of April.
A dynamic review of the clinical development of vaccines and potential COVID-19 drugs has been conducted as of April 2020. Several existing antiviral drugs are being evaluated to treat COVID-19, including remedisivir, chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine, lopinavir/retonavir and lopinafir/tonafer combined with beta interferon.
There is tentative evidence of the effectiveness of remdesivir, as of March 2020.
Clinical improvement has been observed in patients who have been treated with remedicivir for compassionate use.
Phase III clinical trials are being conducted in the United States, China, and Italy. Chloroquine, formerly used for malaria treatment, underwent a study in China in February 2020, with preliminary results.
However, there are calls for peer-to-peer review.
The Korean and Chinese health authorities recommend the use of chloroquine.
However, the Wuhan Virus Research Institute points out that, despite recommending a dose of one gram, the weakness of this dosage is very dangerous and may cause death.
On March 28, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) issued an emergency use license for hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine at the discretion of physicians treating those with COVID-19.
Preliminary data indicate that high doses of ribavirin are necessary to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in the laboratory.
It was recommended that further study of nitazoxanide's effect on living organisms was carried out after proving its inhibition of SARS-CoV-2's low concentration inhibition. Studies have showned that the initial configuration of the spike protein via transvembrane serine protease 2 (TMBRSS2) is essential for SARS-CoV-2 entry through interaction with the ACE2 receptor.
