The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has approved anti-interlocine-6 anti-interlocine receptor therapy (T-cell therapy) of the chimer antigen, based on retrieval case studies to treat cytokine secretion syndrome for non-estroidists, which had a different cause in 2017.
So far, there's no dispensing controlled evidence that tusilizumab is an effective treatment for cytokine excretion syndrome.
The method of transfighting selected and concentrated antibodies produced by COVID-19 recovering immune systems to those who need it is studied as a non-vaccinatory method of passive insemination.
This strategy was tested on SARS and had inconclusive results.
Neutrating the virus represents the expected mechanism of action through which passive antibodial therapy can act as an intermediary against SARS-CoV-2.
However, other mechanisms such as antibodied or phagocytosis or both may be used.
Other forms of passive antibodial therapy, such as the use of manufactured monoclonal antibodies, are under development.
